# user/wsyswl.ad — a Wayland server for the Hamnix desktop.
#
# WHY THIS AND NOT AN X BRIDGE
# ============================
# user/xbridge.ad puts an X application on this desktop by running Xvfb with
# its framebuffer in a file and blitting that file into ONE window. It works,
# and it is a dead end: a framebuffer file holds the COMPOSITED screen, so
# every dialog, popup and second toplevel the application owns arrives fused
# into a single rectangle with no way to separate them. You cannot recover a
# window layout from a picture of a screen.
#
# docs/wayland_passthrough_design.md (in the Hamnix tree) settles the question:
# implement ONE protocol -- a minimal Wayland server -- and you get Wayland
# clients directly AND X11 clients for free through XWayland, which is an X
# server that is itself a Wayland client. A Wayland client does not hand us a
# screen; it hands us one SURFACE per window, with a role, a size and a title.
# One xdg_toplevel becomes one wsys window, which is the thing the bridge could
# never do.
#
# WHAT THE DESIGN CALLS THE GATING RISK IS NOT A RISK HERE
# --------------------------------------------------------
# That document is written against the native kernel, where it concludes
# "Phase 1 is blocked until we add SCM_RIGHTS fd passing": a Wayland client
# gives the compositor its pixels by passing a memfd over the socket in an
# ancillary message, and the native kernel dispatches neither sendmsg nor
# recvmsg. On the Linux line AF_UNIX, SCM_RIGHTS, memfd_create and MAP_SHARED
# are all real and already work. The single hardest thing about this port on
# the other line is, here, a recvmsg call. See user/wsyswl-sys.c, which is
# where that call and its four siblings live until they can move into
# user/linux-syscalls.c.
#
# THE SHAPE
# ---------
#   client (libwayland, in the Debian namespace)
#       |  wire protocol: object id, opcode, args -- over $WAYLAND_DISPLAY
#       |  pixels: a memfd passed with SCM_RIGHTS, mapped MAP_SHARED
#       v
#   wsyswl  -- this program. One wsys window per xdg_toplevel.
#       |  'B' blit + 'D' publish on /dev/wsys/<wid>/draw/ctl
#       |  /dev/wsys/<wid>/keys + /pointer read back for input
#       v
#   wsysd   -- the compositor, which knows nothing about Wayland
#
# The pixel path is the v2 blit protocol, the same one xbridge uses, for the
# same reason: the v1 scene display list can express a widget tree and cannot
# express a photograph. A wl_shm buffer is ARGB8888, which is B,G,R,A in
# memory, which is FMT_BGRA8888 -- so the format conversion is free and the
# only per-pixel work is forcing alpha opaque.
#
# WHAT IS HERE AND WHAT IS NOT
# ----------------------------
# Here: wl_display, wl_registry, wl_compositor, wl_surface, wl_shm +
# wl_shm_pool + wl_buffer, xdg_wm_base + xdg_surface + xdg_toplevel +
# xdg_positioner + xdg_popup, wl_seat + wl_pointer + wl_keyboard with a real
# XKB keymap over a memfd, wl_output + zxdg_output_manager_v1, wp_viewporter,
# wl_data_device_manager WITH A REAL CLIPBOARD BEHIND IT (see "the Wayland
# clipboard" below), and wl_subcompositor as far as its object graph (Firefox
# aborts without it).
#
# wl_data_device_manager USED TO BE AN OBJECT GRAPH AND NOTHING ELSE, and it
# is worth saying so because it is exactly the failure NORTH_STAR.md names.
# GTK will not create a seat without the manager, so it was advertised at
# version 3 to satisfy the handshake -- and then create_data_source and
# get_data_device minted an object apiece, every offer and set_selection fell
# off the end of dispatch into the silent-consume branch, and no data_offer or
# selection event was ever sent to anybody. A Wayland client copying got
# `paste 0 0` on the Hamnix side; a Wayland client pasting got no selection at
# all; neither printed a word. Since Firefox in a distribution namespace is a
# NATIVE Wayland client, that was the clipboard most users would actually
# meet, while the same copy out of an X application worked through
# user/xsnarfd.ad. It is bridged now, to the same /dev/snarf, so there is one
# clipboard and not three.
#
# The rule this file learned the hard way is that a Wayland client does not
# report a compositor gap, it just goes quiet -- see the frame-callback table
# and send_toplevel_configure for two separate ways that happened.
#
# Not here, and each one is a decision rather than an oversight:
#   * DAMAGE IS IGNORED. Every commit blits the whole surface. Honouring
#     wl_surface.damage would be a straightforward win and the place to start
#     when this is too slow -- and with a browser on the screen it IS slow.
#   * SUBSURFACES ARE FLATTENED into the parent toplevel's window, at the
#     parent's origin -- set_position is recorded and not used. A client that
#     puts its content in a subsurface (Firefox does) draws correctly only
#     because the child covers the parent.
#   * A POPUP GETS ITS OWN undecorated window, placed from its positioner's
#     anchor and gravity. The constraint-adjustment rules (flip/slide/resize
#     when a menu would run off the screen) are not implemented; a popup is
#     clamped onto the screen instead, without telling the client.
#   * ONE OUTPUT, one seat, no clipboard, no drag and drop, no dmabuf. A
#     client that insists on GPU buffers gets nothing; software rendering is
#     the only path.
#   * A RESIZE IS NEVER INITIATED BY US. A client can change its own size and
#     the backbuffer follows it (linux-wsys.c's `geometry` verb calls
#     bb_resize), but wsysd has no way to tell this server that the user
#     dragged a window edge, so a Wayland window cannot be resized by the
#     desktop. A maximised client is therefore told a size up front --
#     MAX_W/MAX_H -- rather than being offered the whole output.
#
# HOW FAR THIS HAS BEEN TESTED: weston-simple-shm, weston-flower and
# weston-terminal each get their own decorated Hamnix window and render, and a
# client that exits gives its backbuffer slot back to the next one. XWayland
# runs against it (`Xwayland -shm -noreset`, software pixman, no GL) and X11
# clients -- xeyes, xclock, xsetroot -- render inside its window. Firefox runs
# as a NATIVE Wayland client with MOZ_ENABLE_WAYLAND=1 and paints its full
# chrome. INPUT has now been driven end to end by writing the /keys and
# /pointer rings directly (see tests/linux/wsys_poke.ad): a client receives
# wl_pointer enter/motion/button and wl_keyboard enter/key.
#
# Usage: wsyswl [<socket-path>]
#   default: $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/wayland-0, or /tmp/wayland-0 if that is unset.
#   Point a client at it with WAYLAND_DISPLAY=<basename> XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=<dir>.

extern def sys_write(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_read(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_read_nb(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_open(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_open_write(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_close(fd: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_waitfds(fds: Ptr[int32], nfds: uint64, timeout_ms: int64) -> int64
extern def sys_wsys_free(wid: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_pipe(fds: Ptr[int32]) -> int32
extern def sys_chmod(path: Ptr[char], mode: uint32) -> int32
extern def sys_exit(code: uint64)

# user/wsyswl-sys.c — the five entry points the runtime does not have yet.
extern def sys_unix_listen(path: Ptr[char], backlog: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_unix_accept(lfd: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_scm_recv(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], cap: uint64,
                        fds: Ptr[int32], maxfds: int32,
                        nfds: Ptr[int32]) -> int64
extern def sys_scm_send(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64,
                        passfd: int32) -> int64
extern def sys_mmap_shared(fd: int32, len: uint64) -> uint64
extern def sys_munmap_at(addr: uint64, len: uint64) -> int32
extern def sys_fd_size(fd: int32) -> int64
extern def sys_memfd(name: Ptr[char], len: uint64) -> int32
extern def sys_getenv(name: Ptr[char], buf: Ptr[uint8], cap: uint64) -> int64

# The ONE place a client is allowed to ask how big the screen is. See
# lib/hamscreen.ad: every DE client used to answer this question privately and
# every one of them answered it wrong. This server answers it for EVERY
# Wayland client at once -- and, through Xwayland, for every X11 client -- so
# getting it wrong here is that bug multiplied by the whole distribution.
from lib.hamscreen import hamscreen_dims_or_fail

# THE THIRD CLIPBOARD. /dev/snarf is the Hamnix clipboard and user/xsnarfd.ad
# bridges the X selections to it; wl_data_device was the one left over, and it
# is the one Firefox uses. Everything about it that is not Wayland wire lives
# in lib/wlsnarf.ad -- the device I/O, the change detector, the mime rules and
# the anti-ping-pong invariant.
from lib.wlsnarf import wlsn_present, wlsn_poll, wlsn_put, wlsn_ptr, wlsn_len, \
                        wlsn_mime_bit, wlsn_pick, wlsn_mime_name, \
                        wlsn_slen, WLSN_MAX


# --- protocol constants ---------------------------------------------

# A CONNECTION IS THE UNIT OF INDEPENDENCE ON THIS SERVER, and four of them was
# not enough to go round. Every limit below -- the shm mapping table, the object
# id space, the frame-callback slice, the window budget -- is indexed by
# connection, so two clients share a fate exactly when they share a connection
# and never otherwise. What that makes MAXCONN is not "how many programs may
# draw" but "how many INDEPENDENT fates this server can hold", and the
# distribution already wants more than four: `enter debian { … }` and
# `enter alpine { … }` run at once (HANDOFF §0) and each carries an Xwayland,
# Firefox is a native Wayland client beside them, and the desktop's own chrome
# is another. That is four before anything the user started, and the fifth
# client was refused ENTIRELY -- not one window, the whole program, silently
# until this pass named it.
#
# SIXTEEN, AND FIREFOX IS THE MEASUREMENT THAT PICKED IT.
#
# Eight was not "enough for Firefox plus a bit". Firefox ALONE opens exactly
# EIGHT connections -- measured on this host, `conns 8` four seconds after
# launch -- so the table was full with one program on the screen and the next
# client to arrive, a namespace's Xwayland, was refused ENTIRELY. That is why
# rootless Xwayland could not be the default arm.
#
# Eight is Firefox's real appetite and not a truncation, which had to be
# checked rather than assumed: with the ceiling lifted to 32 it still opens
# exactly 8, with one tab and with ten (`conn_refused 0` both ways). So the
# browser's demand is a BOUNDED 8, and the question is only what must fit
# beside it. NORTH_STAR's named workload is the desktop plus a browser plus
# `enter debian { … }` and `enter alpine { … }` at once, and an Xwayland is
# one connection per namespace however many X clients are behind it
# (measured, rootful and rootless alike): 8 + 1 + 1 = TEN, which does not fit
# in eight and does fit here with six to spare.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, measured and not estimated: 55,320 bytes per connection --
# 45,056 of it the eleven object tables, 8,192 the reassembly buffer, 1,536
# the shm mapping table, and the rest change. Confirmed by building the same
# source at 8 and at 32 and differencing the ELF: 3,178,736 - 1,850,976 =
# 1,327,760 bytes of BSS for 24 connections, which is 55,320 each to within
# four bytes of padding. Sixteen is therefore 864 KiB of BSS where eight was
# 432 KiB, and it is BSS: pages the kernel never faults in for a connection
# that never arrives.
#
# (The comment this replaces said "about 350 KB" for eight. It was wrong --
# the real figure is 432 KiB -- because obj_h and obj_i were added to the
# object table after it was written and nobody re-did the arithmetic. Hence
# the byte-exact build-and-difference above, and tests/linux/wsyswl_conn_
# ceiling.sh, which recomputes it from the declarations rather than trusting
# this paragraph.)
#
# WHY NOT HIGHER. Two hard walls, both arithmetic:
#   * MAXWIN >= MAXCONN * WINPERCONN is the no-starvation invariant, and the
#     window table is user/linux-wsys.c's `struct wshm`, which the first
#     attacher memsets WHOLE -- so a row is 72.2 KiB of RESIDENT shared
#     memory, not address space. 16 * 16 takes it to 256 rows and 18.17 MiB,
#     doubling what was already spent. 32 connections would need 512 rows and
#     36.21 MiB, always resident on every boot, and buying that with a
#     weakened starvation guarantee is a different pass with its own test.
#   * user/linux-syscalls.c's WAITFDS_MAX is 64 and the poll set is
#     1 listener + MAXCONN + the XWM fd + the clipboard pipe, so nothing
#     above MAXCONN = 61 can ever be waited on at all.
# Sixteen sits under both with room. What it does NOT fit is TWO browsers at
# once (8 + 8 + 2 = 18); that is named in HANDOFF as the next ceiling rather
# than quietly hoped away.
MAXCONN: uint64 = 16
MAXOBJ:  uint64 = 1024                 # client ids stay dense and low --
                                       # but Xwayland is a bigger client
                                       # than anything this server carried
                                       # before, and an id at or above
                                       # MAXOBJ is an object that is
                                       # UNKNOWN FOR EVER: every later
                                       # request to it falls off the end of
                                       # dispatch in silence. It is counted
                                       # now (obj_id_refused), and the
                                       # ceiling is four times higher.
ACC:     uint64 = 8192                 # per-connection reassembly buffer

T_NONE: int32 = 0
T_DISPLAY: int32 = 1
T_REGISTRY: int32 = 2
T_CALLBACK: int32 = 3
T_COMPOSITOR: int32 = 4
T_SHM: int32 = 5
T_SHM_POOL: int32 = 6
T_BUFFER: int32 = 7
T_SURFACE: int32 = 8
T_REGION: int32 = 9
T_XDG_WM: int32 = 10
T_XDG_SURF: int32 = 11
T_XDG_TOP: int32 = 12
T_SEAT: int32 = 13
T_POINTER: int32 = 14
T_KEYBOARD: int32 = 15
T_OUTPUT: int32 = 16
T_DDM: int32 = 17
T_DATA_DEVICE: int32 = 18
T_DATA_SOURCE: int32 = 19
T_SUBCOMPOSITOR: int32 = 20
T_SUBSURFACE: int32 = 21
T_VIEWPORTER: int32 = 22
T_VIEWPORT: int32 = 23
T_XDG_OUT_MGR: int32 = 24
T_XDG_OUT: int32 = 25
T_POSITIONER: int32 = 26
T_XDG_POPUP: int32 = 27
T_XWL_SHELL: int32 = 28
T_XWL_SURF: int32 = 29

G_COMPOSITOR: uint32 = 1
G_SHM: uint32 = 2
G_XDG: uint32 = 3
G_SEAT: uint32 = 4
G_OUTPUT: uint32 = 5
G_DDM: uint32 = 6
G_SUBCOMPOSITOR: uint32 = 7
G_VIEWPORTER: uint32 = 8
G_XDG_OUTPUT: uint32 = 9
G_XWL_SHELL: uint32 = 10

DISPLAY_ID: uint32 = 1

SEAT_VERSION: uint32 = 5
OUTPUT_VERSION: uint32 = 3
SEAT_CAP_POINTER: uint32 = 1
SEAT_CAP_KEYBOARD: uint32 = 2

SHM_ARGB8888: uint32 = 0
SHM_XRGB8888: uint32 = 1

STATE_RELEASED: uint32 = 0
STATE_PRESSED: uint32 = 1
TOP_STATE_MAXIMIZED: uint32 = 1
TOP_STATE_ACTIVATED: uint32 = 4

# What a maximised toplevel is told it may have. wsysd draws a title bar and a
# frame around every window, so "maximised" here cannot mean the whole output:
# a client handed the whole screen would have its chrome pushed off the bottom
# by the decoration it cannot see. Set from the real screen size at startup --
# these are the inset, not a resolution.
MAX_INSET_W: int32 = 120
MAX_INSET_H: int32 = 100
MAX_W: int32 = 1160
MAX_H: int32 = 700

BTN_LEFT: uint32 = 272
BTN_MIDDLE: uint32 = 274
BTN_RIGHT: uint32 = 273

MOD_SHIFT: uint32 = 1

# The blit protocol's FMT_BGRA8888. wl_shm ARGB8888 is byte-for-byte this.
FMT_BGRA8888: uint8 = 2


# --- state ------------------------------------------------------------

listen_fd: int32 = -1
serial: uint32 = 0
# NOT a default and not a guess -- resolve_screen() overwrites both from
# /dev/wsys/screen before the socket exists, and refuses to serve if the
# compositor has not published one.
scr_w: int32 = 0
scr_h: int32 = 0

conn_inuse: Array[16, int32]
conn_fd:    Array[16, int32]
conn_reg:   Array[16, int32]
conn_acc:   Array[131072, uint8]        # 16 * 8192
conn_acclen: Array[16, uint64]
conn_seat:  Array[16, int32]
conn_pointer: Array[16, int32]
# The version the connection bound wl_seat at, carried onto its wl_pointer.
# wl_pointer.axis_source and .axis_discrete are version 5 events, and
# libwayland ABORTS on an event a proxy has no listener slot for -- the same
# trap wl_seat.name is gated for. A client that bound at v1 gets the v1
# wl_pointer.axis and nothing else.
conn_ptrver: Array[16, int32]
conn_keyboard: Array[16, int32]
conn_xdg_wm: Array[16, int32]
conn_output: Array[16, int32]           # the client's wl_output object, for enter

# --- THE WAYLAND CLIPBOARD ---------------------------------------------
# wl_data_device is implemented BY the compositor -- see lib/wlsnarf.ad for
# why that means this cannot be a separate process the way user/xsnarfd.ad is.
conn_ddev:  Array[16, int32]            # the client's wl_data_device, 0 = none
conn_offer: Array[16, int32]            # the wl_data_offer WE last made it

# A wl_data_offer is an object the SERVER creates, so its id comes out of the
# server half of the id space (0xff000000 and up) and cannot live in the
# per-connection object table, which is indexed by a dense client id below
# MAXOBJ. There is exactly one live offer per connection -- announcing a new
# selection retires the old one -- so conn_offer IS the table.
DOFF_BASE: uint32 = 0xff000000
doff_next: uint32 = 0

# Which client owns the selection right now, or -1 for "the Hamnix side does".
# It is only used to keep the bridge from announcing a client's own bytes back
# at it; every paste, including that client's, is answered out of /dev/snarf.
sel_conn: int32 = -1

# An in-flight pull: the compositor asked a wl_data_source for its bytes and
# is reading the pipe. ASYNCHRONOUS ON PURPOSE. The obvious version reads the
# pipe to EOF right there in set_selection, and that is a compositor that
# stops painting for as long as a client takes to answer -- a client which,
# being a client, may take for ever or never answer at all. The whole desktop
# would hang on a copy.
pull_fd:    int32  = -1
pull_conn:  int32  = -1
pull_got:   uint64 = 0
pull_over:  uint64 = 0                 # bytes past the cap, for the loud drop
pull_ticks: int32  = 0
PULL_TICKS_MAX: int32 = 180            # 180 * 16 ms = about 3 s
pullbuf: Array[65600, uint8]
mimebuf: Array[256, uint8]
pipebuf: Array[2, int32]

TITLE_CAP: uint64 = 48
conn_title: Array[768, uint8]          # 16 * 48
conn_title_len: Array[16, uint64]

# Per object, indexed conn * MAXOBJ + id.
obj_type: Array[16384, int32]
obj_a: Array[16384, int32]
obj_b: Array[16384, int32]
obj_c: Array[16384, int32]
obj_d: Array[16384, int32]
obj_e: Array[16384, int32]
obj_f: Array[16384, int32]
obj_g: Array[16384, int32]
# Two more per-object words, added for xdg_positioner: placing a menu needs a
# size, an anchor rect, an anchor, a gravity and an offset, which is ten
# numbers, and obj_a..obj_g are seven.
obj_h: Array[16384, int32]
obj_i: Array[16384, int32]
surf_parent: Array[16384, int32]

# SHM MAPPINGS ARE OWNED BY THE CONNECTION, NOT BY THE POOL OBJECT.
#
# A client is entitled to destroy a wl_shm_pool the instant it has carved its
# wl_buffers out of it -- "the mapping will be released when all the buffers
# have been destroyed" -- and weston-simple-shm does exactly that, three
# requests after create_pool and long before it attaches anything. Unmapping
# on pool destroy therefore threw away the pixels of every buffer the client
# was about to draw with, and the first commit found nothing to blit. So a
# mapping lives in this per-connection table, both the pool and its buffers
# hold an INDEX into it, and it is unmapped when the client goes.
#
# ...AND IT IS REFERENCE COUNTED, or a browser runs out of them. The pool
# holds one reference and every wl_buffer cut from it holds another; the
# mapping is unmapped when the last of them goes. Waiting for the connection
# to close instead is fine for a client with one pool and fatal for Firefox,
# which creates a fresh pool for every new buffer size and destroys the old
# one immediately: the slots filled up, map_alloc started returning -1, and
# from that moment every commit was dropped. What that looked like was a
# browser whose window painted and whose MENUS were invisible -- the menu was
# simply the first surface unlucky enough to be created after the table was
# full.
#
# SIXTEEN WAS THE STEAM BUG, MEASURED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. Steam's X session
# holds TWENTY-SIX of these at once. With the table at 16 it fills, map_alloc
# returns -1, and from that moment every commit is dropped at `mi < 0` -- so
# Xwayland's ONE rootful surface freezes and both the login window and the
# xterm sitting behind it stop reaching the framebuffer, which is exactly what
# three passes of this investigation were looking at. Two boots of the same
# image differing only in this number: at 16, `map_alloc_failed 10`,
# `drop_no_mapping 508`, 3 commits, a black screen and an xterm still at the
# size it started at; at 64, `maps_high_water 26`, zero drops, 506 commits, and
# `Sign in to Steam` on the desktop.
#
# A bare Xwayland with one xterm on it already touches 8, so 16 was half a
# table for the smallest session there is. Sixty-four costs 2 KB of BSS.
#
# THIS TABLE IS PER CONNECTION, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT -- see the census
# in docs/linux_window_manager.md §8a. Xwayland makes exactly ONE Wayland
# connection whether it is rootful or rootless (measured, both ways), so every
# X client on one X display shares this table and always will. What buys an X
# session its own table is its own Xwayland, not its own wl_surface.
MAXMAP: uint64 = 64
map_addr: Array[1024, uint64]           # 16 conns * 64
map_len:  Array[1024, uint64]
map_used: Array[1024, int32]
map_ref:  Array[1024, int32]

# The EFFECTIVE size of that table, so the shared-fate property can be tested
# without rebuilding the compiler's constant. Set by WSYSWL_MAXMAP, clamped to
# [4, MAXMAP]; it is MAXMAP unless a test says otherwise. §6.2a proved this
# number matters by booting the image twice with two different values -- a
# property worth a test is worth a knob that does not need a second build.
map_limit: uint64 = 64

# RECEIVED DESCRIPTORS ARE A QUEUE, NOT A PROPERTY OF THE MESSAGE.
#
# The kernel hands ancillary data to the recvmsg that read the FIRST BYTE of
# the sendmsg it accompanied -- not to the message inside that stream which
# actually wants it. A client flushes create_pool together with create_buffer
# and destroy, so pairing "the fd from this read" with "the first request in
# this read" attached the memfd to whatever happened to be at the front, and
# create_pool saw nothing. libwayland's own connection code does the only
# thing that works: push received fds onto a FIFO and let each request with an
# fd argument pop one, in order. This is that FIFO.
FDQ: uint64 = 8
fdq:     Array[128, int32]              # 16 conns * 8
fdq_head: Array[16, uint64]
fdq_tail: Array[16, uint64]

# FRAME CALLBACKS ARE A LIST PER SURFACE, NOT ONE SLOT.
#
# wl_surface.frame may be called any number of times before the commit that
# arms them; the spec says the callbacks are queued and all of them fire on the
# next frame. Keeping only the newest looks harmless and is not: Firefox asks
# for its INITIAL-DRAW callback on the toplevel surface while it is still
# building the window, commits only the subsurface for a while, and asks for a
# second one on the toplevel when it finally attaches a buffer. With one slot
# the first request is overwritten and never answered -- and that first one is
# the one Firefox waits on to decide the window is ready to draw. The window
# comes up, renders one empty frame, and stops forever, with nothing in any log
# to say why. This table is the fix.
#
# ...AND IT IS PARTITIONED BY CONNECTION, which is a second fix on top of the
# first. It used to be ONE table of 64 scanned end to end, so the connection
# that asked for the 64th frame callback denied the 65th to EVERY OTHER CLIENT
# ON THE SERVER -- Firefox's menus could silence Xwayland's whole X screen, and
# the failure is the worst kind this table already knows about: a client whose
# initial-draw callback is never answered renders one empty frame and stops for
# ever. Nothing measured had reached 64 yet, which is exactly why it was worth
# closing before something did; `frame_callbacks_full` was a counter waiting for
# a bug report. Each connection now has its OWN slice of FCPERCONN and can only
# starve itself. MAXCONN * FCPERCONN is the array, so the slices never overlap
# and no arithmetic anywhere can make them.
FCPERCONN: uint64 = 32
FCMAX: uint64 = 512                    # 16 conns * 32
fc_conn: Array[512, int32]             # -1 when the slot is free
fc_surf: Array[512, int32]
fc_cb:   Array[512, int32]

out:     Array[8192, uint8]
out_pos: uint64 = 0
out_fd:  int32 = -1                    # fd to pass with the next flush, or -1

rowbuf:  Array[8192, uint8]
blithdr: Array[32, uint8]
pathbuf: Array[256, uint8]
ctlbuf:  Array[256, uint8]
rbuf:    Array[256, uint8]
numbuf:  Array[24, uint8]
# THE POLL SET, AND IT IS SIZED FROM MAXCONN, WHICH IT WAS NOT.
#
# This was Array[16] while MAXCONN was 8, and the main loop fills it with the
# listener plus ONE ENTRY PER LIVE CONNECTION with no bound check at all --
# only the XWM fd and the clipboard pipe after it were guarded, and they were
# guarded against the literal 16. Nine entries fitted in sixteen, so the
# absence of the check cost nothing and was invisible. Raising MAXCONN to 16
# without this line would have written nineteen entries into a sixteen-entry
# array on every pass of the event loop -- straight into whatever BSS follows
# it -- which is the exact shape of failure this tree keeps paying for: a
# ceiling raised in one place and corrupting memory in another, silently.
#
# WAITSET = 1 listener + MAXCONN + the XWM fd + the clipboard pull pipe, and
# the guards below are written against WAITSET rather than a literal so the
# next person to move MAXCONN moves one number. user/linux-syscalls.c's
# sys_waitfds caps nfds at WAITFDS_MAX = 64 and returns EINVAL above it, so
# this must also stay <= 64 -- which is the real ceiling on MAXCONN (61).
WAITSET: uint64 = 19
waitset: Array[19, int32]
recvfds: Array[8, int32]
recvn:   Array[2, int32]
sockpath: Array[256, uint8]
envbuf:  Array[256, uint8]

# wid -> the fds we hold open on that window. Small and linear: a session has
# a handful of windows and the lookup is off the hot path.
#
# THE TABLE IS COMPACT; THE BUDGET IS PER CONNECTION. MAXWIN used to be 12 for
# the WHOLE SERVER, checked as `n_win >= MAXWIN` with no idea whose windows
# they were, so the first client to open twelve windows denied a thirteenth to
# everybody -- and what a denied window looks like from the client's side is
# `win_open` returning -1, `drop_no_window`, and a toplevel that simply never
# appears. MAXWIN is now MAXCONN * WINPERCONN and win_open counts THIS
# connection's windows, so the arithmetic guarantees what a comment cannot: a
# connection asking for its WINPERCONN-th window can always be given one, no
# matter what any other client has done.
#
# WINPERCONN WAS 8, AND EIGHT WAS THE WHOLE REASON ROOTLESS COULD NOT BE THE
# DEFAULT. user/linux-wsys.c's v2 backbuffer pool was BB_SLOTS = 8 for the
# whole machine, so a ninth window would have existed and never been painted,
# and this number was pinned to it deliberately: a refused window is better
# than a blank one. Rootful spends ONE of those slots on an entire X session;
# rootless spends one per TOPLEVEL, so eight was "a namespace may have eight X
# windows, and Firefox is sharing them".
#
# That pool is no longer a number: BB_SLOTS is now WSYS_MAX_WINDOWS, checked
# at compile time, so the paint pool can never be the first thing to run out
# and /dev/wsys/pool states it. The ceiling that is left is the window table,
# which is 256, and this is 16 * 16 -- the same reservation arithmetic as
# before (every connection can always be given its whole budget), with a
# budget a Debian namespace can actually live in.
#
# THE BUDGET DID NOT MOVE WHEN MAXCONN DOUBLED, and that was a choice with a
# price. Sixteen per connection is what tests/linux/wsyswl_ceiling.sh needs:
# it drives TWELVE X clients through ONE Xwayland, which is one connection
# holding twelve windows, so any WINPERCONN below twelve turns that gate red.
# Halving it to 8 would have bought MAXCONN 16 for nothing -- MAXWIN would
# have stayed 128 and no segment would have grown -- and it would have
# undone the doubling the pass before this one paid for on purpose. So the
# window table follows instead: 128 -> 256, which is the option HANDOFF §0
# named ("taking the device's table to 256") and 18.17 MiB of resident
# shared memory rather than 9.15. See WSYS_MAX_WINDOWS in user/linux-wsys.c,
# which this must equal, and WSYS_VERSION, which had to move with it.
WINPERCONN: uint64 = 16
MAXWIN: uint64 = 256                   # 16 conns * 16, and = WSYS_MAX_WINDOWS
w_wid:  Array[256, int32]
w_draw: Array[256, int32]
w_keys: Array[256, int32]
w_ptr:  Array[256, int32]
w_conn: Array[256, int32]
w_surf: Array[256, int32]
w_btn:  Array[256, int32]               # last pointer button mask seen
w_in:   Array[256, int32]               # pointer has entered this surface
w_focus: Array[256, int32]              # keyboard focus is on this surface
w_x:    Array[256, int32]               # where this window was placed, so a
w_y:    Array[256, int32]               # popup can be placed against it
# THE WINDOW'S OWN EVENT RING, read every pump. Two things arrive on it and
# neither could be learned any other way:
#
#   "close"                 the user pressed the title bar's close button.
#                           wsysd writes `delete <wid>`; the device turns that
#                           into this line for a window whose owner asked to
#                           be asked (`wmdelete 1`), instead of destroying the
#                           window and leaving the program running blind.
#   "geometry <x> <y> <w> <h>"
#                           somebody ELSE moved or resized this window -- the
#                           desktop dragging it, a script, hamdesktop. This is
#                           X's ConfigureNotify arriving as a file, and it is
#                           what lets an X client know where it is.
#
# It is a ring, so a parked compositor is woken by it through the same futex
# every other ring uses. An idle desktop posts nothing and costs nothing.
w_evt:  Array[256, int32]
n_win:  uint64 = 0
cascade: int32 = 0

# One line of a /keys or /pointer ring, reassembled across reads.
inbuf:  Array[1024, uint8]
carry:  Array[1024, uint8]             # 12 windows would need 12 of these; the
carrylen: uint64 = 0                   # rings are line-atomic in practice, so
                                       # one shared carry is honest enough --
                                       # see drain_window.

keymap_fd: int32 = -1
keymap_len: uint64 = 0
kmbuf: Array[8192, uint8]
kmlen: uint64 = 0

evt_time: uint32 = 0
verbose: int32 = 0
tick: int32 = 0

# The one wl_display.error this server ever sends: the connection refusal. Its
# own buffer, so that turning a client away can never disturb the bytes or the
# pending descriptor staged for a client that WAS let in. See conn_refuse.
# 8 bytes of header + object_id + code + string length, then the message.
REFUSE_STR: uint64 = 20
REFUSE_CAP: uint64 = 192
refusebuf: Array[192, uint8]


# --- small helpers ----------------------------------------------------

def slen(s: Ptr[uint8]) -> uint64:
    n: uint64 = 0
    while s[n] != 0:
        n = n + 1
    return n


def puts(fd: int32, s: Ptr[uint8]):
    sys_write(fd, s, slen(s))


def putd(fd: int32, v: int32):
    n: uint64 = 0
    x: int32 = v
    if x < 0:
        sys_write(fd, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("-"), 1)
        x = 0 - x
    if x == 0:
        numbuf[0] = 48
        n = 1
    while x > 0:
        numbuf[n] = cast[uint8](48 + x % 10)
        x = x / 10
        n = n + 1
    i: uint64 = n
    while i > 0:
        i = i - 1
        sys_write(fd, &numbuf[i], 1)


def emit_int(buf: Ptr[uint8], at: uint64, v: int32) -> uint64:
    d: Array[12, uint8]
    n: uint64 = 0
    x: int32 = v
    i: uint64 = at
    if x < 0:
        buf[i] = 45
        i = i + 1
        x = 0 - x
    if x == 0:
        d[0] = 48
        n = 1
    while x > 0:
        d[n] = cast[uint8](48 + x % 10)
        x = x / 10
        n = n + 1
    while n > 0:
        n = n - 1
        buf[i] = d[n]
        i = i + 1
    return i


def put_le32(buf: Ptr[uint8], at: uint64, v: int32):
    u: uint32 = cast[uint32](v)
    buf[at]     = cast[uint8](u & 255)
    buf[at + 1] = cast[uint8]((u >> 8) & 255)
    buf[at + 2] = cast[uint8]((u >> 16) & 255)
    buf[at + 3] = cast[uint8]((u >> 24) & 255)


def ru32(p: Ptr[uint8], off: uint64) -> uint32:
    return cast[uint32](p[off]) \
         | (cast[uint32](p[off + 1]) << 8) \
         | (cast[uint32](p[off + 2]) << 16) \
         | (cast[uint32](p[off + 3]) << 24)


def str_copy(dst: Ptr[uint8], s: Ptr[uint8], cap: uint64) -> uint64:
    i: uint64 = 0
    while s[i] != 0 and i + 1 < cap:
        dst[i] = s[i]
        i = i + 1
    dst[i] = 0
    return i


# --- counters: a dropped frame must never be silent -------------------
#
# commit_buffer used to have SIX bare `return`s in it, and every one of them
# means "this client's pixels are not going on the screen this frame". A
# compositor that does that without saying so is the exact failure this tree is
# organised against: it cost three passes to work out that a Steam window which
# X says is mapped, viewable and PAINTED never reached the framebuffer, because
# the one process that knew it had thrown the buffer away said nothing.
#
# WHY COUNTERS AND NOT A LOG. WSYSWL_VERBOSE=1 prints a line per commit into
# /var/log on the initramfs tmpfs -- the VM's RAM -- and the guest went silent
# inside two minutes of Steam starting, twice. So: a monotonic counter per
# reason, ONE named line on stderr the first time each fires, and the whole set
# published as a small file beside the socket, which is the one directory that
# spans the namespace boundary (§6 of docs/steam_namespace.md). Reading a
# server's own state with `cat` is how the wsysd bug before this one was
# caught; `/dev/wsys/wsysd/state` is the precedent.
NSTAT: uint64 = 29
stat_n:    Array[29, int32]
stat_said: Array[29, int32]
stat_dirty: int32 = 1

S_COMMIT:   uint64 = 0          # buffers actually blitted to a window
S_ROWS:     uint64 = 1          # rows blitted
S_NOTBUF:   uint64 = 2          # attached id is not a wl_buffer
S_BADDIM:   uint64 = 3          # width or height < 1
S_NOMAP:    uint64 = 4          # mi < 0: the buffer has no shm mapping
S_NOBASE:   uint64 = 5          # the mapping is not mapped
S_NOROLE:   uint64 = 6          # a surface with no xdg_surface (cursor)
S_NOWIN:    uint64 = 7          # win_open failed
S_NOSLOT:   uint64 = 8          # the window lost its wsysd slot
S_UNCONF:   uint64 = 9          # commit with a buffer before ack_configure
S_SHORT:    uint64 = 10         # pos + rowbytes > maplen: rows cut off
S_MAPFULL:  uint64 = 11         # map_alloc found no free slot
S_IDHIGH:   uint64 = 12         # an object id at or above MAXOBJ
S_FCFULL:   uint64 = 13         # the frame-callback table was full
S_MAPUSE:   uint64 = 14         # gauge: mappings in use right now
S_MAPHI:    uint64 = 15         # high water of the above
S_IDHI:     uint64 = 16         # highest object id ever seen
S_WINHI:    uint64 = 17         # high water of open windows
S_WINFULL:  uint64 = 18         # a window refused: this CONNECTION's budget
# HOW MANY CLIENTS ARE ON THIS SERVER, published for one reason: every limit
# above is per CONNECTION, and the only way to read "26 mappings" correctly is
# to know how many clients that is spread over. Xwayland is ONE connection
# whether it runs rootful or rootless -- so a rootful X session with fifteen
# windows on it reads `conns 1`, and that number is the argument in
# docs/linux_window_manager.md §8a stated as a measurement.
S_CONN:     uint64 = 19         # gauge: connections in use right now
S_CONNHI:   uint64 = 20         # high water of the above
# ROOTLESS XWAYLAND. An X toplevel becomes a wsys window only when TWO facts
# meet: Xwayland has told this server a surface's serial over
# xwayland_shell_v1, and the X window carrying that same serial in its
# WL_SURFACE_SERIAL property has been seen by the window manager inside this
# compositor. Either half arriving without the other is a window that exists
# and is not on the screen, which is precisely the failure this file counts
# rather than swallows.
S_XWLPAIR:  uint64 = 21         # X window <-> wl_surface associations made
S_XWLUNP:   uint64 = 22         # a commit on an X surface with no X window yet
S_XWLERR:   uint64 = 23         # the XWM refused something, by name on stderr
# THE CLOSE BUTTON, AND WHAT IT DID. These two are separate on purpose: "the
# window would not close" and "the client was killed" are different bugs with
# the same symptom, and a single `closes` counter would make them one.
S_CLOSEREQ: uint64 = 24         # a client was ASKED to close and agreed to be
S_CLOSEKILL: uint64 = 25        # ... a client that could not be asked, killed
# COMPOSITOR-SIDE MOVES PUSHED BACK TO X. wsysd moving a window used to be
# invisible to the client inside it; this is how many times the new position
# was sent on as a ConfigureWindow, which the X server turns into the
# ConfigureNotify the client is waiting for.
S_XCONF:    uint64 = 26         # a move/resize pushed back to an X client
S_ORFOLLOW: uint64 = 27         # an override-redirect child moved with parent
# A WHOLE CLIENT REFUSED, which is the most severe thing in this list and was
# the only one with no counter: MAXCONN is the ceiling on INDEPENDENT clients,
# so hitting it does not cost a window, it costs a program. It said so on
# stderr and nowhere a test could read it -- and it is close: Firefox ALONE
# reaches conns 8 with its content and GPU processes, measured, which is
# MAXCONN exactly. A namespace's Xwayland arriving next is the ninth.
S_CONNFULL: uint64 = 28         # a client was refused: MAXCONN

statepath: Array[256, uint8]


def hit(k: uint64):
    stat_n[k] = stat_n[k] + 1
    stat_dirty = 1


def gauge(k: uint64, v: int32):
    if stat_n[k] != v:
        stat_n[k] = v
        stat_dirty = 1


def high(k: uint64, v: int32):
    if v > stat_n[k]:
        stat_n[k] = v
        stat_dirty = 1


# A drop, counted, and NAMED ONCE on stderr. Once, because the thing that made
# the last attempt at this useless was volume.
def drop(k: uint64, name: Ptr[uint8]):
    stat_n[k] = stat_n[k] + 1
    stat_dirty = 1
    if stat_said[k] == 0:
        stat_said[k] = 1
        puts(2, "wsyswl: DROPPING FRAMES -- ")
        puts(2, name)
        puts(2, " (first time; running totals in ")
        puts(2, &statepath[0])
        puts(2, ")\n")


def st_kv(fd: int32, name: Ptr[uint8], k: uint64):
    puts(fd, name)
    puts(fd, " ")
    putd(fd, stat_n[k])
    puts(fd, "\n")


# --- wire output ------------------------------------------------------

def o_u32(v: uint32):
    if out_pos + 4 > 8192:
        return
    put_le32(&out[0], out_pos, cast[int32](v))
    out_pos = out_pos + 4


def o_cstr(s: Ptr[uint8]):
    # A Wayland string arg: u32 length INCLUDING the NUL, the bytes, the NUL,
    # then zero padding to a 32-bit boundary.
    n: uint64 = slen(s)
    o_u32(cast[uint32](n + 1))
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n and out_pos < 8192:
        out[out_pos] = s[i]
        out_pos = out_pos + 1
        i = i + 1
    out[out_pos] = 0
    out_pos = out_pos + 1
    while (out_pos & 3) != 0:
        out[out_pos] = 0
        out_pos = out_pos + 1


def emit_begin(obj: uint32) -> uint64:
    start: uint64 = out_pos
    o_u32(obj)
    o_u32(0)                                    # size<<16 | opcode, patched
    return start


def emit_end(start: uint64, opcode: uint32):
    size: uint64 = out_pos - start
    put_le32(&out[0], start + 4,
             cast[int32]((cast[uint32](size) << 16) | (opcode & 0xffff)))


# Everything staged since the last flush goes out in ONE sendmsg. Wayland is a
# stream and a client parses it incrementally, so a short write would leave
# half an event on the wire and desynchronise its parser for good; sys_scm_send
# blocks until the whole burst is gone, which is the only safe choice.
def flush(fd: int32):
    if out_pos == 0 and out_fd < 0:
        return
    sys_scm_send(fd, &out[0], out_pos, out_fd)
    if out_fd >= 0:
        sys_close(out_fd)
        out_fd = -1
    out_pos = 0


# --- object table -----------------------------------------------------

def oidx(c: uint64, id: uint32) -> uint64:
    return c * MAXOBJ + cast[uint64](id)


def valid_id(id: uint32) -> int32:
    if id == 0:
        return 0
    if cast[uint64](id) >= MAXOBJ:
        return 0
    return 1


def new_obj(c: uint64, id: uint32, otype: int32):
    if valid_id(id) == 0:
        # AN ID THIS SERVER REFUSES IS AN OBJECT THAT IS UNKNOWN FOR EVER.
        # Every later request addressed to it falls off the end of dispatch and
        # is consumed in silence -- so if it was a wl_buffer, that client's
        # pixels stop arriving and nothing anywhere says why.
        if id != 0:
            drop(S_IDHIGH, "an object id at or above MAXOBJ -- raise MAXOBJ")
        return
    high(S_IDHI, cast[int32](id))
    oi: uint64 = oidx(c, id)
    obj_type[oi] = otype
    obj_a[oi] = 0
    obj_b[oi] = 0
    obj_c[oi] = 0
    obj_d[oi] = 0
    obj_e[oi] = 0
    obj_f[oi] = 0
    obj_g[oi] = -1
    obj_h[oi] = 0
    obj_i[oi] = 0
    surf_parent[oi] = 0


def fdq_push(c: uint64, fd: int32):
    if fdq_tail[c] - fdq_head[c] >= FDQ:
        sys_close(fd)                       # a client sending fds we never
        return                              # consume is a client bug, not a leak
    fdq[c * FDQ + fdq_tail[c] % FDQ] = fd
    fdq_tail[c] = fdq_tail[c] + 1


def fdq_pop(c: uint64) -> int32:
    if fdq_head[c] == fdq_tail[c]:
        return -1
    fd: int32 = fdq[c * FDQ + fdq_head[c] % FDQ]
    fdq_head[c] = fdq_head[c] + 1
    return fd


def fc_add(c: uint64, surf: uint32, cb: uint32) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = c * FCPERCONN
    end: uint64 = i + FCPERCONN
    while i < end:
        if fc_conn[i] < 0:
            fc_conn[i] = cast[int32](c)
            fc_surf[i] = cast[int32](surf)
            fc_cb[i] = cast[int32](cb)
            return 1
        i = i + 1
    return 0


def fc_drop_surf(c: uint64, surf: uint32):
    i: uint64 = c * FCPERCONN
    end: uint64 = i + FCPERCONN
    while i < end:
        if fc_surf[i] == cast[int32](surf):
            fc_conn[i] = -1
        i = i + 1


def fc_drop_conn(c: uint64):
    i: uint64 = c * FCPERCONN
    end: uint64 = i + FCPERCONN
    while i < end:
        fc_conn[i] = -1
        i = i + 1


def map_count(c: uint64) -> int32:
    n: int32 = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < MAXMAP:
        if map_used[c * MAXMAP + i] != 0:
            n = n + 1
        i = i + 1
    return n


def map_alloc(c: uint64) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < map_limit:
        if map_used[c * MAXMAP + i] == 0:
            map_used[c * MAXMAP + i] = 1
            map_addr[c * MAXMAP + i] = 0
            map_len[c * MAXMAP + i] = 0
            map_ref[c * MAXMAP + i] = 1         # the pool's own reference
            n: int32 = map_count(c)
            gauge(S_MAPUSE, n)
            high(S_MAPHI, n)
            return cast[int32](i)
        i = i + 1
    drop(S_MAPFULL, "this connection's wl_shm mappings are exhausted -- raise MAXMAP")
    return -1


def map_ref_up(c: uint64, mi: int32):
    if mi < 0:
        return
    m: uint64 = c * MAXMAP + cast[uint64](mi)
    if map_used[m] != 0:
        map_ref[m] = map_ref[m] + 1


def map_unref(c: uint64, mi: int32):
    if mi < 0:
        return
    m: uint64 = c * MAXMAP + cast[uint64](mi)
    if map_used[m] == 0:
        return
    map_ref[m] = map_ref[m] - 1
    if map_ref[m] > 0:
        return
    if map_addr[m] != 0:
        sys_munmap_at(map_addr[m], map_len[m])
    map_addr[m] = 0
    map_len[m] = 0
    map_used[m] = 0
    gauge(S_MAPUSE, map_count(c))


def obj_get_type(c: uint64, id: uint32) -> int32:
    if valid_id(id) == 0:
        return T_NONE
    return obj_type[oidx(c, id)]


def free_obj(c: uint64, id: uint32):
    if valid_id(id) == 0:
        return
    obj_type[oidx(c, id)] = T_NONE


# --- events -----------------------------------------------------------

def send_delete_id(id: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(DISPLAY_ID)
    o_u32(id)
    emit_end(s, 1)


def send_callback_done(cb: uint32, data: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(cb)
    o_u32(data)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_global(reg: uint32, name: uint32, iface: Ptr[uint8], version: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(reg)
    o_u32(name)
    o_cstr(iface)
    o_u32(version)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_shm_format(shm: uint32, fmt: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(shm)
    o_u32(fmt)
    emit_end(s, 0)


# wl_surface.enter(output) -- "this surface is now on that monitor".
#
# It reads like a hint and it is not. A toolkit learns the scale, the refresh
# and the colour of a surface from the output it is ON, and until it has been
# told it is on one it has no output at all: GTK's wl_surface has an empty
# output list, so Firefox's compositor thread has nothing to schedule frames
# against and never produces a second frame. Weston sends this the moment a
# surface is mapped; not sending it is why Firefox drew one empty window and
# then sat still.
def send_surface_enter(surface: uint32, output: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(surface)
    o_u32(output)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_buffer_release(buf: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(buf)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_xdg_configure(xs: uint32, ser: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(xs)
    o_u32(ser)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_toplevel_configure(top: uint32, w: uint32, h: uint32, maxed: int32):
    # (0,0) means "you choose your own size", which is what the initial
    # configure must say for a client that has not asked for anything. ACTIVATED
    # goes on every configure so a client draws its focused decorations -- there
    # is one window manager here and it is wsysd, so a Wayland client is never
    # told it is inactive.
    #
    # MAXIMIZED IS NOT COSMETIC. A client that sent set_maximized has made a
    # request the compositor is required to answer, and it answers with the
    # state in a configure -- there is no other channel. Firefox maximises its
    # first window, and until the configure comes back carrying MAXIMIZED *and*
    # a concrete size it renders exactly one frame (an empty client-side-
    # decorated frame with a shadow) and then stops dead, waiting for a size it
    # was promised. That looks identical to a client that crashed on startup.
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(top)
    o_u32(w)
    o_u32(h)
    if maxed != 0:
        o_u32(8)                                # array: two u32 states
        o_u32(TOP_STATE_MAXIMIZED)
        o_u32(TOP_STATE_ACTIVATED)
    else:
        o_u32(4)
        o_u32(TOP_STATE_ACTIVATED)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_toplevel_close(top: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(top)
    emit_end(s, 1)


def send_seat_caps(seat: uint32, caps: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(seat)
    o_u32(caps)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_seat_name(seat: uint32, name: Ptr[uint8]):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(seat)
    o_cstr(name)
    emit_end(s, 1)


def send_output_all(output: uint32):
    # geometry(x, y, phys_w, phys_h, subpixel, make, model, transform)
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(output)
    o_u32(0)
    o_u32(0)
    o_u32(cast[uint32](scr_w))
    o_u32(cast[uint32](scr_h))
    o_u32(0)
    o_cstr("Hamnix")
    o_cstr("wsys")
    o_u32(0)
    emit_end(s, 0)
    # mode(flags = current|preferred, w, h, refresh mHz)
    s = emit_begin(output)
    o_u32(3)
    o_u32(cast[uint32](scr_w))
    o_u32(cast[uint32](scr_h))
    o_u32(60000)
    emit_end(s, 1)
    # scale(1)
    s = emit_begin(output)
    o_u32(1)
    emit_end(s, 3)
    # done()
    s = emit_begin(output)
    emit_end(s, 2)


# zxdg_output_v1: where the monitor is, in the coordinate space a client
# actually thinks in.
#
# wl_output.geometry reports PHYSICAL millimetres and wl_output.mode reports
# the mode in device pixels; neither answers "how big is this screen in the
# units my windows are measured in", which is what a toolkit enumerating
# monitors wants. xdg-output does, and Firefox binds it before it lays
# anything out. A Firefox that cannot enumerate a single monitor brings its
# window up and then has nowhere to render it.
def send_xdg_output_all(xo: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(xo)                  # logical_position
    o_u32(0)
    o_u32(0)
    emit_end(s, 0)
    s = emit_begin(xo)                          # logical_size
    o_u32(cast[uint32](scr_w))
    o_u32(cast[uint32](scr_h))
    emit_end(s, 1)
    s = emit_begin(xo)                          # name (v2)
    o_cstr("HAM-0")
    emit_end(s, 3)
    s = emit_begin(xo)                          # description (v2)
    o_cstr("Hamnix wsys display")
    emit_end(s, 4)
    s = emit_begin(xo)                          # done
    emit_end(s, 2)


def fixed(v: int32) -> uint32:
    # wl_fixed_t is signed 24.8.
    return cast[uint32](v << 8)


def send_kbd_keymap(kbd: uint32, size: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(kbd)
    o_u32(1)                                    # XKB_V1
    o_u32(size)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_kbd_enter(kbd: uint32, ser: uint32, surface: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(kbd)
    o_u32(ser)
    o_u32(surface)
    o_u32(0)                                    # keys[] : empty array
    emit_end(s, 1)


def send_kbd_leave(kbd: uint32, ser: uint32, surface: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(kbd)
    o_u32(ser)
    o_u32(surface)
    emit_end(s, 2)


def send_kbd_key(kbd: uint32, ser: uint32, t: uint32, key: uint32,
                 state: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(kbd)
    o_u32(ser)
    o_u32(t)
    o_u32(key)
    o_u32(state)
    emit_end(s, 3)


def send_kbd_modifiers(kbd: uint32, ser: uint32, dep: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(kbd)
    o_u32(ser)
    o_u32(dep)
    o_u32(0)
    o_u32(0)
    o_u32(0)
    emit_end(s, 4)


def send_kbd_repeat_info(kbd: uint32, rate: uint32, delay: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(kbd)
    o_u32(rate)
    o_u32(delay)
    emit_end(s, 5)


def send_ptr_enter(ptr: uint32, ser: uint32, surface: uint32,
                   sx: int32, sy: int32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ptr)
    o_u32(ser)
    o_u32(surface)
    o_u32(fixed(sx))
    o_u32(fixed(sy))
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_ptr_leave(ptr: uint32, ser: uint32, surface: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ptr)
    o_u32(ser)
    o_u32(surface)
    emit_end(s, 1)


def send_ptr_motion(ptr: uint32, t: uint32, sx: int32, sy: int32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ptr)
    o_u32(t)
    o_u32(fixed(sx))
    o_u32(fixed(sy))
    emit_end(s, 2)


def send_ptr_button(ptr: uint32, ser: uint32, t: uint32, btn: uint32,
                    state: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ptr)
    o_u32(ser)
    o_u32(t)
    o_u32(btn)
    o_u32(state)
    emit_end(s, 3)


def send_ptr_frame(ptr: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ptr)
    emit_end(s, 5)


# --- the SCROLL WHEEL -------------------------------------------------
#
# THE DEFECT THESE THREE FIX. `wsysd` already had the whole wheel: evdev
# EV_REL/REL_WHEEL accumulates into `ptr_dz`, `deliver_pointer` gives the line
# kind `'s'` for scroll, and `route_pointer` writes the delta as the FIFTH
# field of `<kind> <x> <y> <buttons> <dz>`. This file read the first four
# fields and threw the fifth away, and had no wl_pointer.axis at all -- so
# every Wayland client behind this compositor, and through Xwayland every X11
# client too, had a dead scroll wheel. Measured on the real thing: eight
# wheel-down events over Steam's store page changed **0 of 564400 pixels**,
# while dragging the scrollbar with the same pointer scrolled it 96%. The
# page was scrollable; the wheel was not connected.
#
# wl_pointer.axis is version 1, so it is always safe to send. axis_source (6)
# and axis_discrete (8) are version 5 and are GATED on the version the client
# bound the seat at -- libwayland aborts on an event a proxy has no listener
# slot for, which is the trap send_seat_name already documents one screen up.
AXIS_VERTICAL: uint32 = 0
AXIS_HORIZONTAL: uint32 = 1
AXIS_SOURCE_WHEEL: uint32 = 0
# One wheel notch, in surface units. libinput and every other compositor
# report 10.0 for a detented wheel, and Xwayland's accumulator turns each
# 10.0 into one button-4/button-5 press; a smaller number scrolls nothing
# until several notches have added up, which reads as a wheel that misses.
AXIS_NOTCH: int32 = 10


def send_ptr_axis(ptr: uint32, t: uint32, axis: uint32, val: int32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ptr)
    o_u32(t)
    o_u32(axis)
    o_u32(fixed(val))
    emit_end(s, 4)


def send_ptr_axis_source(ptr: uint32, src: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ptr)
    o_u32(src)
    emit_end(s, 6)


def send_ptr_axis_discrete(ptr: uint32, axis: uint32, disc: int32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ptr)
    o_u32(axis)
    o_u32(cast[uint32](disc))
    emit_end(s, 8)


# --- the XKB keymap ---------------------------------------------------
#
# wl_keyboard.keymap hands the client an fd holding an XKB keymap, and the
# client compiles it with libxkbcommon before it will interpret a single key.
# This one is SELF-CONTAINED -- it includes no XKB data files, because the
# client resolves includes on ITS side and the Debian namespace's xkb data may
# not be where a client expects. It covers the ASCII the Hamnix keyboard
# produces, with a Shift level so uppercase resolves. It is the reference
# implementation's keymap (Hamnix linux_abi/wayland.ad), transcribed.

def km(s: Ptr[uint8]):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while s[i] != 0 and kmlen < 8190:
        kmbuf[kmlen] = s[i]
        kmlen = kmlen + 1
        i = i + 1


def km_key(name: Ptr[uint8], syms: Ptr[uint8]):
    km("key <")
    km(name)
    km("> {type[Group1]=\"TWO_LEVEL\",[")
    km(syms)
    km("]};\n")


def build_keymap():
    if keymap_fd >= 0:
        return
    kmlen = 0
    km("xkb_keymap {\n")
    km("xkb_keycodes \"hm\" {\n")
    km("minimum = 8;\nmaximum = 255;\n")
    # A keycode is the evdev code plus 8 -- the client adds 8 to whatever the
    # server sends in wl_keyboard.key, so the names below must land on that.
    km("<A9> = 23; <A22> = 22; <A28> = 36; <A57> = 65;\n")
    km("<K02>=10;<K03>=11;<K04>=12;<K05>=13;<K06>=14;\n")
    km("<K07>=15;<K08>=16;<K09>=17;<K10>=18;<K11>=19;\n")
    km("<K12>=20;<K13>=21;<K16>=24;<K17>=25;\n")
    km("<K18>=26;<K19>=27;<K20>=28;<K21>=29;<K22>=30;\n")
    km("<K23>=31;<K24>=32;<K25>=33;<K26>=34;<K27>=35;\n")
    km("<K30>=38;<K31>=39;<K32>=40;<K33>=41;<K34>=42;\n")
    km("<K35>=43;<K36>=44;<K37>=45;<K38>=46;<K39>=47;\n")
    km("<K40>=48;<K41>=49;<K42>=50;<K43>=51;<K44>=52;\n")
    km("<K45>=53;<K46>=54;<K47>=55;<K48>=56;<K49>=57;\n")
    km("<K50>=58;<K51>=59;<K52>=60;<K53>=61;\n")
    km("<LFSH>=50;<RTSH>=62;\n")
    km("};\n")
    km("xkb_types \"hm\" {\n")
    km("virtual_modifiers NumLock;\n")
    km("type \"ONE_LEVEL\" { modifiers=none; level_name[Level1]=\"Any\"; };\n")
    km("type \"TWO_LEVEL\" { modifiers=Shift; map[Shift]=Level2; ")
    km("level_name[Level1]=\"Base\"; level_name[Level2]=\"Shift\"; };\n")
    km("};\n")
    km("xkb_compatibility \"hm\" { };\n")
    km("xkb_symbols \"hm\" {\n")
    km("key <LFSH> {[Shift_L]};\nkey <RTSH> {[Shift_R]};\n")
    km("key <A9> {[Tab]};\nkey <A22> {[BackSpace]};\n")
    km("key <A28> {[Return]};\nkey <A57> {[space]};\n")
    km_key("K02", "1,exclam")
    km_key("K03", "2,at")
    km_key("K04", "3,numbersign")
    km_key("K05", "4,dollar")
    km_key("K06", "5,percent")
    km_key("K07", "6,asciicircum")
    km_key("K08", "7,ampersand")
    km_key("K09", "8,asterisk")
    km_key("K10", "9,parenleft")
    km_key("K11", "0,parenright")
    km_key("K12", "minus,underscore")
    km_key("K13", "equal,plus")
    km_key("K16", "q,Q")
    km_key("K17", "w,W")
    km_key("K18", "e,E")
    km_key("K19", "r,R")
    km_key("K20", "t,T")
    km_key("K21", "y,Y")
    km_key("K22", "u,U")
    km_key("K23", "i,I")
    km_key("K24", "o,O")
    km_key("K25", "p,P")
    km_key("K26", "bracketleft,braceleft")
    km_key("K27", "bracketright,braceright")
    km_key("K30", "a,A")
    km_key("K31", "s,S")
    km_key("K32", "d,D")
    km_key("K33", "f,F")
    km_key("K34", "g,G")
    km_key("K35", "h,H")
    km_key("K36", "j,J")
    km_key("K37", "k,K")
    km_key("K38", "l,L")
    km_key("K39", "semicolon,colon")
    km_key("K40", "apostrophe,quotedbl")
    km_key("K41", "grave,asciitilde")
    km_key("K43", "backslash,bar")
    km_key("K44", "z,Z")
    km_key("K45", "x,X")
    km_key("K46", "c,C")
    km_key("K47", "v,V")
    km_key("K48", "b,B")
    km_key("K49", "n,N")
    km_key("K50", "m,M")
    km_key("K51", "comma,less")
    km_key("K52", "period,greater")
    km_key("K53", "slash,question")
    km("modifier_map Shift {<LFSH>,<RTSH>};\n")
    km("};\n")
    km("};\n")
    kmbuf[kmlen] = 0
    keymap_len = kmlen + 1                      # the client mmaps the NUL too
    fd: int32 = sys_memfd(cast[Ptr[char]]("wl-xkb-keymap"), keymap_len)
    if fd < 0:
        return
    sys_write(fd, &kmbuf[0], keymap_len)
    keymap_fd = fd


# --- windows ----------------------------------------------------------

def winpath(wid: int32, leaf: Ptr[uint8]):
    i: uint64 = 0
    p: Ptr[uint8] = "/dev/wsys/"
    k: uint64 = 0
    while p[k] != 0:
        pathbuf[i] = p[k]
        i = i + 1
        k = k + 1
    i = emit_int(&pathbuf[0], i, wid)
    pathbuf[i] = 47
    i = i + 1
    k = 0
    while leaf[k] != 0:
        pathbuf[i] = leaf[k]
        i = i + 1
        k = k + 1
    pathbuf[i] = 0


def write_path(path: Ptr[char], data: Ptr[uint8]) -> int32:
    fd: int32 = sys_open_write(path)
    if fd < 0:
        return -1
    sys_write(fd, data, slen(data))
    sys_close(fd)
    return 0


def win_ctl(wid: int32, verb: Ptr[uint8]):
    winpath(wid, "ctl")
    write_path(cast[Ptr[char]](&pathbuf[0]), verb)


def newwindow() -> int32:
    if write_path(cast[Ptr[char]]("/dev/wsys/ctl"),
                  cast[Ptr[uint8]]("newwindow\n")) != 0:
        return -1
    fd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]]("/dev/wsys/ctl"))
    if fd < 0:
        return -1
    n: int64 = sys_read(fd, &rbuf[0], 32)
    sys_close(fd)
    if n <= 0:
        return -1
    v: int32 = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < cast[uint64](n) and rbuf[i] >= 48 and rbuf[i] <= 57:
        v = v * 10 + cast[int32](rbuf[i]) - 48
        i = i + 1
    if v < 2:
        return -1
    return v


def send_title(wid: int32, c: uint64):
    i: uint64 = 0
    p: Ptr[uint8] = "title "
    k: uint64 = 0
    while p[k] != 0:
        ctlbuf[i] = p[k]
        i = i + 1
        k = k + 1
    k = 0
    while k < conn_title_len[c] and k < TITLE_CAP:
        ctlbuf[i] = conn_title[c * TITLE_CAP + k]
        i = i + 1
        k = k + 1
    if k == 0:
        ctlbuf[i] = 87                                   # "Wayland"
        ctlbuf[i + 1] = 97
        ctlbuf[i + 2] = 121
        ctlbuf[i + 3] = 108
        ctlbuf[i + 4] = 97
        ctlbuf[i + 5] = 110
        ctlbuf[i + 6] = 100
        i = i + 7
    ctlbuf[i] = 10
    ctlbuf[i + 1] = 0
    win_ctl(wid, &ctlbuf[0])


def send_geometry(wid: int32, x: int32, y: int32, w: int32, h: int32):
    i: uint64 = 0
    p: Ptr[uint8] = "geometry "
    k: uint64 = 0
    while p[k] != 0:
        ctlbuf[i] = p[k]
        i = i + 1
        k = k + 1
    i = emit_int(&ctlbuf[0], i, x)
    ctlbuf[i] = 32
    i = emit_int(&ctlbuf[0], i + 1, y)
    ctlbuf[i] = 32
    i = emit_int(&ctlbuf[0], i + 1, w)
    ctlbuf[i] = 32
    i = emit_int(&ctlbuf[0], i + 1, h)
    ctlbuf[i] = 10
    ctlbuf[i + 1] = 0
    win_ctl(wid, &ctlbuf[0])


def win_slot(wid: int32) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n_win:
        if w_wid[i] == wid:
            return cast[int32](i)
        i = i + 1
    return -1


# Give a surface a real Hamnix window. GEOMETRY GOES FIRST because
# user/linux-wsys.c's bb_for() sizes the v2 backbuffer from the window's w/h
# the first time a blit arrives and never resizes it -- a window that is still
# its default 640x480 when the first blit lands is stuck at that stride.
# px/py < 0 means "you place it": a toplevel gets the cascade. A popup arrives
# with a position already worked out from its positioner and must land exactly
# there, undecorated -- a menu with a title bar is not a menu.
def win_count(c: uint64) -> int32:
    n: int32 = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n_win:
        if w_conn[i] == cast[int32](c):
            n = n + 1
        i = i + 1
    return n


def win_open(c: uint64, surf: uint32, w: int32, h: int32,
             px: int32, py: int32, decor: int32) -> int32:
    # THIS connection's budget, not the server's. See the table's comment: a
    # global ceiling let one client's window count decide whether another
    # client's window existed, and neither of them was ever told.
    if cast[uint64](win_count(c)) >= WINPERCONN:
        drop(S_WINFULL, "this connection's window budget is full -- raise WINPERCONN")
        return -1
    if n_win >= MAXWIN:
        return -1
    wid: int32 = newwindow()
    if wid < 2:
        return -1
    # Cascade far enough that two windows do not sit on top of each other.
    # There is no placement policy here worth the name -- wsysd owns stacking
    # and the user can move things -- but a new toplevel landing 28 pixels
    # from the last one reads as a rendering bug rather than as two windows.
    x: int32 = px
    y: int32 = py
    if px < 0 or py < 0:
        x = 60 + cascade % 3 * 340
        y = 60 + cascade / 3 % 2 * 340
        cascade = cascade + 1
    if x + w > scr_w:
        x = scr_w - w - 20
    if y + h > scr_h:
        y = scr_h - h - 20
    if x < 0:
        x = 0
    if y < 0:
        y = 0
    send_geometry(wid, x, y, w, h)
    if decor != 0:
        win_ctl(wid, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("decorate 1\n"))
    else:
        win_ctl(wid, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("decorate 0\n"))
    # A popup is BY DEFINITION on top of the window it came from, so it goes
    # one z-band higher; a menu that opens behind its own menu bar is worse
    # than no menu at all.
    if decor != 0:
        win_ctl(wid, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("z 7\n"))
    else:
        win_ctl(wid, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("z 8\n"))
    send_title(wid, c)
    win_ctl(wid, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("version 2\n"))
    # ASK ME BEFORE YOU CLOSE ME. Without this the desktop's close button
    # destroys the window record and leaves the client running with nothing on
    # screen -- an invisible xterm holding a shell, a browser you cannot see
    # and cannot quit. With it the request arrives on the event ring below and
    # becomes WM_DELETE_WINDOW or xdg_toplevel.close, which is what the client
    # is waiting to be told.
    win_ctl(wid, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("wmdelete 1\n"))
    winpath(wid, "draw/ctl")
    dfd: int32 = sys_open_write(cast[Ptr[char]](&pathbuf[0]))
    if dfd < 0:
        return -1
    winpath(wid, "keys")
    kfd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]](&pathbuf[0]))
    winpath(wid, "pointer")
    pfd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]](&pathbuf[0]))
    winpath(wid, "event")
    efd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]](&pathbuf[0]))
    w_wid[n_win] = wid
    w_draw[n_win] = dfd
    w_keys[n_win] = kfd
    w_ptr[n_win] = pfd
    w_conn[n_win] = cast[int32](c)
    w_surf[n_win] = cast[int32](surf)
    w_btn[n_win] = 0
    w_in[n_win] = 0
    w_focus[n_win] = 0
    w_x[n_win] = x
    w_y[n_win] = y
    w_evt[n_win] = efd
    n_win = n_win + 1
    puts(1, "wsyswl: surface -> wid ")
    putd(1, wid)
    puts(1, "\n")
    return wid


def win_close(wid: int32):
    s: int32 = win_slot(wid)
    if s < 0:
        return
    i: uint64 = cast[uint64](s)
    if w_draw[i] >= 0:
        sys_close(w_draw[i])
    if w_keys[i] >= 0:
        sys_close(w_keys[i])
    if w_ptr[i] >= 0:
        sys_close(w_ptr[i])
    if w_evt[i] >= 0:
        sys_close(w_evt[i])
    # sys_wsys_free, not a `close <wid>` on the global ctl. Only the syscall
    # releases the window's v2 backbuffer slot, and user/linux-wsys.c has
    # THREE of them for the whole system -- a leaked slot is a window that can
    # never be drawn into again, which shows up much later as a client that
    # renders nothing for no visible reason.
    sys_wsys_free(wid)
    k: uint64 = i
    while k + 1 < n_win:
        w_wid[k] = w_wid[k + 1]
        w_draw[k] = w_draw[k + 1]
        w_keys[k] = w_keys[k + 1]
        w_ptr[k] = w_ptr[k + 1]
        w_conn[k] = w_conn[k + 1]
        w_surf[k] = w_surf[k + 1]
        w_btn[k] = w_btn[k + 1]
        w_in[k] = w_in[k + 1]
        w_focus[k] = w_focus[k + 1]
        w_x[k] = w_x[k + 1]
        w_y[k] = w_y[k + 1]
        w_evt[k] = w_evt[k + 1]
        k = k + 1
    n_win = n_win - 1


# --- rootless Xwayland: the X11 window manager INSIDE the compositor --
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, AND THE REASON THAT DOES NOT.
# ==============================================
# It is NOT to end shared fate. docs/linux_window_manager.md §8a measured that
# and it is false: every limit in this file is per CONNECTION, and Xwayland
# opens exactly one connection whether it is rootful or rootless (`conns 1`,
# measured both ways). The shared-fate bugs that were really there were fixed
# by partitioning the frame-callback and window tables per connection, and the
# way to give an X session its own fate is to give it its own Xwayland.
#
# The reason that survives is the project's own, and it is architectural:
#
#     WINDOW MANAGEMENT BELONGS IN wsysd. WINDOWS ARE ALREADY FILES UNDER
#     /dev/wsys/<wid>/.
#
# A rootful Xwayland collapses every X client in a namespace onto ONE
# wl_surface and therefore ONE wsys window -- `windows_high_water 1` for an
# entire Steam session. The Hamnix desktop cannot move, raise, stack or close
# an individual X window because it cannot SEE one; it sees a rectangle. That
# is the only reason a namespace needs `jwm` inside it, and it is why a Debian
# application is a second-class citizen on this desktop while Firefox, a native
# Wayland client, gets one wsys window per xdg_toplevel.
#
# Rootless makes each X toplevel its own wl_surface and therefore its own wsys
# window: a file, addressable by name, managed by the compositor that manages
# everything else.
#
# THE PIECE THAT IS NOT OPTIONAL.
# ===============================
# A rootless Xwayland with no X11 window manager on the compositor side
# produces NO wsys window at all -- measured, tests/linux/wsyswl_shared_fate.sh.
# Xwayland -rootless creates a wl_surface per X toplevel and hands it over
# through `xwayland_shell_v1`, which carries ONE fact: a 64-bit serial. The
# same serial appears on the X window as the WL_SURFACE_SERIAL property. Only
# something holding BOTH an X connection and the Wayland connection can put the
# two halves together, which is why the window manager has to live in here.
#
# So this section is two things at once: the Wayland half (the
# xwayland_shell_v1 global, below in do_registry/dispatch) and an X11 client
# that holds SubstructureRedirect on the root window. It speaks the X wire
# protocol by hand for the same reason user/xsnarfd.ad does -- linking libX11
# would make this a foreign binary -- and it borrows that file's shape: nothing
# assumes a fixed root window or resource base, both are read out of the
# connection setup reply.
#
# WHAT IT IS AND IS NOT, TODAY. Stage one: association, mapping, geometry,
# titles, and one wsys window per mapped X toplevel. NOT yet: WM_PROTOCOLS /
# WM_DELETE_WINDOW (so a title-bar close destroys the wsys window and leaves
# the X client running), _NET_WM_STATE round trips, WM_TRANSIENT_FOR stacking,
# _NET_SUPPORTED, and compositor-side move/resize pushed back to the X client
# as a ConfigureNotify. Every one of those is named in HANDOFF and in
# docs/linux_window_manager.md §8b rather than half-done here.
#
# THE CLIPBOARD IS ALREADY DONE AND IS NOT ON THAT LIST. §8's item 2 counted
# "selection ownership for CLIPBOARD/PRIMARY" as part of this work. It is not:
# user/xsnarfd.ad is an ordinary X client on the same display and owns the
# selections there whether the display is rootful or rootless.

# SIXTY-FOUR, not thirty-two. This counts every X window that exists on the
# display, not just the ones that become Hamnix windows: a CreateNotify adds
# a row whether the window is ever mapped or not, and a toolkit makes several
# unmapped helpers per visible window. Thirty-two was under the twelve-window
# ceiling this file now permits.
XWMAX: uint64 = 64                     # X windows this compositor will manage
XDEFER: uint64 = 64                    # X events parked while awaiting a reply
XTITLE_CAP: uint64 = 64

xwm_on:    int32 = 0                   # 1 = WSYSWL_XWM named an X socket
xwm_path:  Array[256, uint8]
xwm_retry: int32 = 0
xfd:       int32 = -1
xdead:     int32 = 0
xseq:      uint32 = 0
x_root:    uint32 = 0
x_resbase: uint32 = 0
x_maxreq:  uint32 = 0
x_atom_serial:  uint32 = 0
x_atom_allow:   uint32 = 0
x_atom_netname: uint32 = 0
x_atom_protocols: uint32 = 0
x_atom_delete:    uint32 = 0
# EWMH. Not decoration: a toolkit asks these questions before it decides how
# big to open, whether it is already maximised, and whether there is a window
# manager at all -- and until now this compositor answered exactly as a screen
# with NO window manager does, which is what §8b said and what
# hamnix_x11session.sh had to skip its window-manager check for.
x_atom_supported:  uint32 = 0
x_atom_wmcheck:    uint32 = 0
x_atom_active:     uint32 = 0
x_atom_clientlist: uint32 = 0
x_atom_clientstk:  uint32 = 0
x_atom_wmstate:    uint32 = 0
x_atom_wmname:     uint32 = 0            # _NET_WM_NAME again, for the check
x_atom_workarea:   uint32 = 0
x_atom_desktopgeo: uint32 = 0
x_atom_viewport:   uint32 = 0
x_atom_ndesktops:  uint32 = 0
x_atom_curdesktop: uint32 = 0
x_atom_wmwindowtype: uint32 = 0
x_atom_wintype_normal: uint32 = 0
x_atom_wintype_dialog: uint32 = 0
x_atom_wmstate_max_h: uint32 = 0
x_atom_wmstate_max_v: uint32 = 0
x_atom_wmstate_focused: uint32 = 0
x_atom_utf8:       uint32 = 0
x_check_win:       uint32 = 0            # the _NET_SUPPORTING_WM_CHECK window
x_ewmh_seen:       int32 = 0             # the client list we last published
x_ewmh_active_win: int32 = 0             # ... and the active window
x_prop_fmt: int32 = 0
x_prop_n:   uint32 = 0
xerr_seq:   uint32 = 0
xerr_code:  int32 = 0

xreq:   Array[1024, uint8]
xin:    Array[64, uint8]
xrep:   Array[4096, uint8]
xrep_n: uint64 = 0
xsink:  Array[1024, uint8]
xdefer_buf: Array[2048, uint8]         # 64 events * 32 bytes
xdefer_n: uint64 = 0
xwait:  Array[2, int32]

xw_win:   Array[64, int32]
xw_serlo: Array[64, int32]
xw_serhi: Array[64, int32]
xw_have:  Array[64, int32]             # a WL_SURFACE_SERIAL has been read
xw_or:    Array[64, int32]             # override-redirect: a menu, not a window
xw_map:   Array[64, int32]
xw_x:     Array[64, int32]
xw_y:     Array[64, int32]
xw_w:     Array[64, int32]
xw_h:     Array[64, int32]
xw_del:   Array[64, int32]             # lists WM_DELETE_WINDOW in WM_PROTOCOLS
xw_tfor:  Array[64, int32]             # WM_TRANSIENT_FOR: the X window this
                                       #   dialog belongs to, or 0
# WHICH TOPLEVEL A MENU BELONGS TO, and where inside it. An override-redirect
# window places ITSELF, in X screen coordinates, worked out from where its
# parent was when the menu opened. Nothing tells it the parent has since been
# dragged, so the compositor has to carry the offset instead: on map, the menu
# is attributed to the toplevel whose rectangle it opened over, and the offset
# is remembered. When that toplevel moves, the menu moves with it.
xw_par:   Array[64, int32]             # the X window this menu opened over
xw_dx:    Array[64, int32]             # ... and where inside it
xw_dy:    Array[64, int32]
xw_surf:  Array[64, int32]             # the paired wl_surface object id
xw_conn:  Array[64, int32]
xw_title: Array[4096, uint8]           # 64 * 64
xw_tlen:  Array[64, uint64]
n_xw:     uint64 = 0


def xput16(buf: Ptr[uint8], at: uint64, v: uint32):
    buf[at]     = cast[uint8](v & 255)
    buf[at + 1] = cast[uint8]((v >> 8) & 255)


def xget16(buf: Ptr[uint8], at: uint64) -> uint32:
    return cast[uint32](buf[at]) | (cast[uint32](buf[at + 1]) << 8)


# X sends window positions as SIGNED 16-bit. A window at -20 read unsigned is a
# window at 65516, which the placement below would clamp to the far edge of the
# screen -- a menu that opens off the side of the display for no visible reason.
def xs16(buf: Ptr[uint8], at: uint64) -> int32:
    v: uint32 = xget16(buf, at)
    if v >= 32768:
        return cast[int32](v) - 65536
    return cast[int32](v)


def xzero(n: uint64):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n:
        xreq[i] = 0
        i = i + 1


def x_send(n: uint64) -> uint32:
    # EVERY request bumps the sequence, including the ones with no reply: the
    # server counts them all, and a reply matched against a sequence that
    # skipped the silent requests matches the wrong thing.
    xseq = xseq + 1
    sent: uint64 = 0
    while sent < n:
        k: int64 = sys_write(xfd, &xreq[sent], n - sent)
        if k <= 0:
            xdead = 1
            return xseq
        sent = sent + cast[uint64](k)
    return xseq


def x_read_exact(buf: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64) -> int32:
    got: uint64 = 0
    while got < n:
        k: int64 = sys_read(xfd, &buf[got], n - got)
        if k <= 0:
            return 0
        got = got + cast[uint64](k)
    return 1


# One X message, or nothing. 1 = a 32-byte header is in xin (and a reply's
# variable part in xrep), 0 = the timeout expired quiet, -1 = the server went.
# THE VARIABLE PART IS ALWAYS DRAINED HERE, in one place: a reply left
# half-read desynchronises the stream permanently, and the symptom is an
# unrelated event arriving mangled minutes later.
def x_poll_msg(timeout_ms: int64) -> int32:
    if xfd < 0 or xdead != 0:
        return -1
    xwait[0] = xfd
    r: int64 = sys_waitfds(&xwait[0], 1, timeout_ms)
    if r <= 0:
        return 0
    if x_read_exact(&xin[0], 32) == 0:
        xdead = 1
        return -1
    xrep_n = 0
    if xin[0] != 1:
        return 1
    extra: uint64 = cast[uint64](ru32(&xin[0], 4)) * 4
    take: uint64 = extra
    if take > 4096:
        take = 4096
    if take > 0:
        if x_read_exact(&xrep[0], take) == 0:
            xdead = 1
            return -1
    xrep_n = take
    left: uint64 = extra - take
    while left > 0:
        chunk: uint64 = left
        if chunk > 1024:
            chunk = 1024
        if x_read_exact(&xsink[0], chunk) == 0:
            xdead = 1
            return -1
        left = left - chunk
    return 1


# An event that arrived while we were waiting for a reply is PARKED, not
# dropped. A MapRequest lost because it landed in the middle of an InternAtom
# round trip is an X window that never appears, and nothing would say so.
def x_defer_push():
    if xdefer_n >= XDEFER:
        drop(S_XWLERR, "the XWM's parked-event ring overflowed -- an X window may be unmanaged")
        return
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < 32:
        xdefer_buf[xdefer_n * 32 + i] = xin[i]
        i = i + 1
    xdefer_n = xdefer_n + 1


# A ROUND TRIP HERE STOPS THE WHOLE COMPOSITOR, which is why the budget is a
# second and not the five xsnarfd allows itself. Everything this waits for is a
# local X server answering GetProperty or InternAtom, so a second is already
# generous; and losing the race costs a TITLE or a property fallback, never a
# window -- the association itself arrives as a ClientMessage and waits for
# nothing. A compositor that blocks for five seconds because an X server is
# busy is a desktop that stutters for reasons no counter would explain.
def x_await(seq: uint32) -> int32:
    budget: int32 = 20                          # 20 x 50 ms = one second
    while budget > 0:
        r: int32 = x_poll_msg(50)
        if r < 0:
            return 0
        if r == 0:
            budget = budget - 1
            continue
        s: uint32 = xget16(&xin[0], 2)
        if xin[0] == 1 and s == (seq & 65535):
            return 1
        if xin[0] == 0:
            xerr_seq = s
            xerr_code = cast[int32](xin[1])
            if s == (seq & 65535):
                return 0
        elif xin[0] != 1:
            x_defer_push()
    return 0


def x_intern(name: Ptr[uint8]) -> uint32:
    n: uint64 = slen(name)
    total: uint64 = 8 + (n + 3) / 4 * 4
    xzero(total)
    xreq[0] = 16                                # InternAtom
    xreq[1] = 0                                 # only-if-exists = False
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, cast[uint32](total / 4))
    xput16(&xreq[0], 4, cast[uint32](n))
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n:
        xreq[8 + i] = name[i]
        i = i + 1
    seq: uint32 = x_send(total)
    if x_await(seq) == 0:
        return 0
    return ru32(&xin[0], 8)


def x_select_input(win: uint32, mask: uint32) -> uint32:
    xzero(16)
    xreq[0] = 2                                 # ChangeWindowAttributes
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, 4)
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 4, cast[int32](win))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 8, 0x0800)               # CWEventMask
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 12, cast[int32](mask))
    return x_send(16)


def x_map_window(win: uint32):
    xzero(8)
    xreq[0] = 8                                 # MapWindow
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, 2)
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 4, cast[int32](win))
    x_send(8)


# _XWAYLAND_ALLOW_COMMITS, set to 1 the moment a window is seen. Xwayland asks
# its window manager's permission before it will commit a redirected window's
# pixels; a compositor that never answers gets a window that is mapped,
# associated, and permanently blank. Setting it unconditionally is the honest
# answer for a compositor that does no frame synchronisation: we are always
# ready.
def x_allow_commits(win: uint32):
    if x_atom_allow == 0:
        return
    xzero(28)
    xreq[0] = 18                                # ChangeProperty
    xreq[1] = 0                                 # mode = Replace
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, 7)
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 4, cast[int32](win))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 8, cast[int32](x_atom_allow))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 12, 6)                   # type = CARDINAL
    xreq[16] = 32                               # format
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 20, 1)                   # one value
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 24, 1)                   # ... and it is 1
    x_send(28)


# ASK AN X CLIENT TO CLOSE ITSELF -- WM_DELETE_WINDOW, the oldest and most
# universally implemented convention in X, and the reason a title-bar close
# button can exist at all.
#
# The alternative, which is what this compositor did until now because it had
# no button to press, is to take the window away and leave the process
# running. For an X client that means a program with no way to be seen and no
# way to be quit; for a text editor it means unsaved work with nobody to ask.
#
# A client advertises the protocol by listing WM_DELETE_WINDOW in its
# WM_PROTOCOLS property, which is read once when the window maps. A client
# that does NOT advertise it has said it cannot be asked, and the convention's
# answer for that case is KillClient -- which is what a close button on such a
# window has to mean, and is what every other window manager does. Both are
# below, and which one was used is visible in the log rather than guessed at.
def x_send_delete(win: uint32) -> int32:
    if x_atom_protocols == 0 or x_atom_delete == 0:
        return 0
    xzero(44)
    xreq[0] = 25                                # SendEvent
    xreq[1] = 0                                 # propagate = False
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, 11)
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 4, cast[int32](win))     # destination
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 8, 0)                    # event-mask: to the client
    # ... and the 32-byte event itself.
    xreq[12] = 33                               # ClientMessage
    xreq[13] = 32                               # format
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 16, cast[int32](win))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 20, cast[int32](x_atom_protocols))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 24, cast[int32](x_atom_delete))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 28, 0)                   # timestamp = CurrentTime
    x_send(44)
    return 1


def x_kill_client(win: uint32):
    xzero(8)
    xreq[0] = 113                               # KillClient
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, 2)
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 4, cast[int32](win))
    x_send(8)


# Does this window list WM_DELETE_WINDOW in WM_PROTOCOLS? One round trip, on
# map, and never again -- the answer is a property of the toolkit, not of the
# moment.
#
# "I COULD NOT ASK" AND "IT SAID NO" ARE DIFFERENT ANSWERS, and conflating them
# is how a close button becomes a `kill -9`. x_get_prop returns 0 both when the
# property is genuinely absent AND when the round trip lost its race inside
# x_await's one-second budget or the server returned an error -- and several
# windows mapping at once (Firefox's helpers, a toolkit's dialogs) is exactly
# when that race is lost. Defaulting a FAILED READ to "cannot be asked" means
# the next title-bar click terminates a client that implements
# WM_DELETE_WINDOW perfectly well, with a log line saying the client said so.
#
# So the two are separate values: 1 = it advertises the protocol, 0 = it
# answered and did not, -1 = we never got an answer. close_requested treats -1
# as "ask anyway": a delete message to a client that ignores it does nothing at
# all, which is the cheaper of the two mistakes by a wide margin.
def xwm_read_protocols(i: uint64):
    xw_del[i] = -1
    if x_atom_protocols == 0 or x_atom_delete == 0:
        return
    if x_get_prop(cast[uint32](xw_win[i]), x_atom_protocols) == 0:
        return
    if x_prop_fmt != 32:
        return
    xw_del[i] = 0
    k: uint64 = 0
    while k < cast[uint64](x_prop_n) and k * 4 + 4 <= xrep_n:
        if ru32(&xrep[0], k * 4) == x_atom_delete:
            xw_del[i] = 1
            return
        k = k + 1


# Grant a ConfigureRequest exactly as asked, minus the stacking half. An X
# client that asks for 700x440 at +290+180 gets 700x440 at +290+180; wsysd
# decides where that window LANDS ON THE DESKTOP, which is a different
# question and the one this whole section is about.
def x_grant_configure(win: uint32, mask: uint32, x: int32, y: int32,
                      w: int32, h: int32, bw: int32):
    m: uint32 = mask & 31                       # X|Y|W|H|BorderWidth
    if m == 0:
        return
    at: uint64 = 12
    if (m & 1) != 0:
        put_le32(&xreq[0], at, x)
        at = at + 4
    if (m & 2) != 0:
        put_le32(&xreq[0], at, y)
        at = at + 4
    if (m & 4) != 0:
        put_le32(&xreq[0], at, w)
        at = at + 4
    if (m & 8) != 0:
        put_le32(&xreq[0], at, h)
        at = at + 4
    if (m & 16) != 0:
        put_le32(&xreq[0], at, bw)
        at = at + 4
    xreq[0] = 12                                # ConfigureWindow
    xreq[1] = 0
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, cast[uint32](at / 4))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 4, cast[int32](win))
    xput16(&xreq[0], 8, m)
    xput16(&xreq[0], 10, 0)
    x_send(at)


# 1 = a reply is in xrep, x_prop_n items of x_prop_fmt bits each.
def x_get_prop(win: uint32, prop: uint32) -> int32:
    x_prop_fmt = 0
    x_prop_n = 0
    if prop == 0:
        return 0
    xzero(24)
    xreq[0] = 20                                # GetProperty
    xreq[1] = 0                                 # delete = False
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, 6)
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 4, cast[int32](win))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 8, cast[int32](prop))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 12, 0)                   # type = AnyPropertyType
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 16, 0)                   # long-offset
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 20, 256)                 # long-length: 1 KiB is plenty
    seq: uint32 = x_send(24)
    if x_await(seq) == 0:
        return 0
    x_prop_fmt = cast[int32](xin[1])
    x_prop_n = ru32(&xin[0], 16)
    return 1


def x_query_ext(name: Ptr[uint8]) -> uint32:
    n: uint64 = slen(name)
    total: uint64 = 8 + (n + 3) / 4 * 4
    xzero(total)
    xreq[0] = 98                                # QueryExtension
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, cast[uint32](total / 4))
    xput16(&xreq[0], 4, cast[uint32](n))
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n:
        xreq[8 + i] = name[i]
        i = i + 1
    seq: uint32 = x_send(total)
    if x_await(seq) == 0:
        return 0
    if xin[8] == 0:                             # present = False
        return 0
    return cast[uint32](xin[9])                 # its major opcode


# THE ONE REQUEST WITHOUT WHICH ROOTLESS IS A NO-OP, and it took a measurement
# to find because everything else looks right without it.
#
# With SubstructureRedirect held, WL_SURFACE_SERIAL interned and
# xwayland_shell_v1 bound by Xwayland, two X clients mapped, were managed, and
# produced ZERO wl_surfaces: `xwl_managed 2`, `commits 0`, `max_object_id 16`.
# Xwayland only builds a wl_surface for a window whose drawing is REDIRECTED --
# `if (window->redirectDraw != RedirectDrawManual) return;` in its realize path
# -- and it does not redirect anything itself. The window manager has to, with
# CompositeRedirectSubwindows(root, Manual), which is what every other
# compositor's XWM does in its first breath. Without it a rootless session is a
# window manager managing windows that are not on any screen: the exact
# success-shaped failure NORTH_STAR.md names, arrived at from the inside.
def x_composite_redirect() -> int32:
    op: uint32 = x_query_ext("Composite")
    if op == 0:
        return 0
    # QueryVersion FIRST -- the extension refuses every other request until the
    # client has said which version it speaks. (xsnarfd learned the same about
    # XFIXES, at the cost of an afternoon.)
    xzero(12)
    xreq[0] = cast[uint8](op)
    xreq[1] = 0                                 # CompositeQueryVersion
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, 3)
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 4, 0)                    # client major 0
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 8, 4)                    # client minor 4
    seq: uint32 = x_send(12)
    if x_await(seq) == 0:
        return 0
    xzero(12)
    xreq[0] = cast[uint8](op)
    xreq[1] = 2                                 # CompositeRedirectSubwindows
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, 3)
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 4, cast[int32](x_root))
    xreq[8] = 1                                 # update = Manual
    x_send(12)
    return 1


# --- EWMH ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHAT A TOOLKIT ACTUALLY ASKS, and what it did before this. GTK and Qt read
# _NET_SUPPORTED to find out what the window manager can do, _NET_WM_STATE to
# find out whether they are already maximised, _NET_WORKAREA to find out how
# much of the screen they may use, and _NET_SUPPORTING_WM_CHECK to find out
# whether there is a window manager at ALL. This compositor answered nothing,
# which is not "answered badly" -- it is byte-for-byte the answer a bare X
# screen gives, so a toolkit correctly concluded there was no window manager
# and laid itself out accordingly. `jwm` publishes 66 of these atoms, and the
# difference between rootless and jwm as a default is partly this list.
#
# ONLY WHAT IS TRUE IS ADVERTISED. _NET_SUPPORTED lists the properties below
# and nothing else: an EWMH hint claimed and not honoured is worse than one
# absent, because absent is a fact a client can act on. There is no
# _NET_WM_STATE_FULLSCREEN here, no _NET_MOVERESIZE_WINDOW and no
# _NET_WM_STATE_HIDDEN, because this compositor does not do those things yet.
def x_prop32(win: uint32, prop: uint32, ptype: uint32, vals: Ptr[int32],
             n: uint64):
    if prop == 0 or win == 0:
        return
    total: uint64 = 24 + n * 4
    if total > 1000:
        return
    xzero(total)
    xreq[0] = 18                                # ChangeProperty
    xreq[1] = 0                                 # mode = Replace
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, cast[uint32](total / 4))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 4, cast[int32](win))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 8, cast[int32](prop))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 12, cast[int32](ptype))
    xreq[16] = 32                               # format
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 20, cast[int32](n))
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n:
        put_le32(&xreq[0], 24 + i * 4, vals[i])
        i = i + 1
    x_send(total)


def x_prop_str(win: uint32, prop: uint32, ptype: uint32, str_: Ptr[uint8]):
    if prop == 0 or win == 0:
        return
    n: uint64 = slen(str_)
    total: uint64 = 24 + (n + 3) / 4 * 4
    if total > 1000:
        return
    xzero(total)
    xreq[0] = 18
    xreq[1] = 0
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, cast[uint32](total / 4))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 4, cast[int32](win))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 8, cast[int32](prop))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 12, cast[int32](ptype))
    xreq[16] = 8                                # format: bytes
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 20, cast[int32](n))
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n:
        xreq[24 + i] = str_[i]
        i = i + 1
    x_send(total)


# THE CHECK WINDOW. _NET_SUPPORTING_WM_CHECK must point at a window that
# exists, is never destroyed, and carries the same property pointing at
# ITSELF plus the window manager's _NET_WM_NAME. A client that finds the
# property on the root and then cannot read it back on the window it names
# concludes -- correctly -- that a window manager published its hints and
# then died, which docs/linux_window_manager.md §4 records matchbox doing.
def x_make_check_window() -> uint32:
    w: uint32 = x_resbase | 1
    xzero(32)
    xreq[0] = 1                                 # CreateWindow
    xreq[1] = 0                                 # depth = CopyFromParent
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, 8)
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 4, cast[int32](w))
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 8, cast[int32](x_root))
    xput16(&xreq[0], 12, 0)                     # x
    xput16(&xreq[0], 14, 0)                     # y
    xput16(&xreq[0], 16, 1)                     # width
    xput16(&xreq[0], 18, 1)                     # height
    xput16(&xreq[0], 20, 0)                     # border width
    xput16(&xreq[0], 22, 2)                     # class = InputOnly
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 24, 0)                   # visual = CopyFromParent
    put_le32(&xreq[0], 28, 0)                   # value-mask = none
    x_send(32)
    return w


ewmh_vals: Array[64, int32]


def x_ewmh_init():
    x_atom_supported  = x_intern("_NET_SUPPORTED")
    x_atom_wmcheck    = x_intern("_NET_SUPPORTING_WM_CHECK")
    x_atom_active     = x_intern("_NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW")
    x_atom_clientlist = x_intern("_NET_CLIENT_LIST")
    x_atom_clientstk  = x_intern("_NET_CLIENT_LIST_STACKING")
    x_atom_wmstate    = x_intern("_NET_WM_STATE")
    x_atom_workarea   = x_intern("_NET_WORKAREA")
    x_atom_desktopgeo = x_intern("_NET_DESKTOP_GEOMETRY")
    x_atom_viewport   = x_intern("_NET_DESKTOP_VIEWPORT")
    x_atom_ndesktops  = x_intern("_NET_NUMBER_OF_DESKTOPS")
    x_atom_curdesktop = x_intern("_NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP")
    x_atom_wmwindowtype = x_intern("_NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE")
    x_atom_wintype_normal = x_intern("_NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE_NORMAL")
    x_atom_wintype_dialog = x_intern("_NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE_DIALOG")
    x_atom_wmstate_max_h = x_intern("_NET_WM_STATE_MAXIMIZED_HORZ")
    x_atom_wmstate_max_v = x_intern("_NET_WM_STATE_MAXIMIZED_VERT")
    x_atom_wmstate_focused = x_intern("_NET_WM_STATE_FOCUSED")
    x_atom_utf8       = x_intern("UTF8_STRING")

    x_check_win = x_make_check_window()
    # The check window names itself and carries the WM's name, which is what
    # a client reads to find out WHICH window manager it is talking to.
    ewmh_vals[0] = cast[int32](x_check_win)
    x_prop32(x_check_win, x_atom_wmcheck, 33, &ewmh_vals[0], 1)   # 33 = WINDOW
    x_prop32(x_root, x_atom_wmcheck, 33, &ewmh_vals[0], 1)
    x_prop_str(x_check_win, x_atom_netname, x_atom_utf8, "wsyswl")

    n: uint64 = 0
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_supported)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_wmcheck)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_active)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_clientlist)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_clientstk)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_wmstate)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_workarea)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_desktopgeo)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_viewport)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_ndesktops)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_curdesktop)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_netname)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_wmwindowtype)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_wintype_normal)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_wintype_dialog)
    n = n + 1
    ewmh_vals[n] = cast[int32](x_atom_wmstate_focused)
    n = n + 1
    x_prop32(x_root, x_atom_supported, 4, &ewmh_vals[0], n)   # 4 = ATOM

    # ONE DESKTOP, and it is the whole screen. There is no workspace switcher
    # on this desktop; saying so is a fact, and a client that reads
    # _NET_WORKAREA gets the real geometry rather than the 640x480 an
    # unanswered question leaves it guessing at.
    ewmh_vals[0] = 1
    x_prop32(x_root, x_atom_ndesktops, 6, &ewmh_vals[0], 1)       # 6 = CARDINAL
    ewmh_vals[0] = 0
    x_prop32(x_root, x_atom_curdesktop, 6, &ewmh_vals[0], 1)
    ewmh_vals[0] = 0
    ewmh_vals[1] = 0
    x_prop32(x_root, x_atom_viewport, 6, &ewmh_vals[0], 2)
    ewmh_vals[0] = scr_w
    ewmh_vals[1] = scr_h
    x_prop32(x_root, x_atom_desktopgeo, 6, &ewmh_vals[0], 2)
    # THE WORKAREA IS NOT THE SCREEN. wsysd's decoration takes a title bar and
    # a one-pixel frame out of every window, so a client handed the whole
    # display loses its own chrome off the bottom edge -- which is the same
    # arithmetic MAX_INSET_W/H already does for xdg_toplevel.configure. The
    # two answers have to agree or a maximised X client and a maximised
    # Wayland client are different sizes on the same desktop.
    ewmh_vals[0] = 0
    ewmh_vals[1] = 0
    ewmh_vals[2] = MAX_W
    ewmh_vals[3] = MAX_H
    x_prop32(x_root, x_atom_workarea, 6, &ewmh_vals[0], 4)


# _NET_CLIENT_LIST and _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW, republished whenever the set of
# managed windows changes. A taskbar, a pager and `wmctrl -l` all read the
# first; the second is what "which window has the keyboard" means to a
# toolkit. Republished on a CHANGE only -- a property write per frame is a
# round trip per frame to an X server, and this loop is the compositor's.
def x_ewmh_publish():
    if x_atom_clientlist == 0 or xfd < 0:
        return
    n: uint64 = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n_xw and n < 64:
        if xw_map[i] != 0 and xw_or[i] == 0:
            ewmh_vals[n] = xw_win[i]
            n = n + 1
        i = i + 1
    # A cheap signature of the list, so an unchanged set costs nothing.
    sig: int32 = cast[int32](n)
    k: uint64 = 0
    while k < n:
        sig = sig * 31 + ewmh_vals[k]
        k = k + 1
    if sig == x_ewmh_seen:
        return
    x_ewmh_seen = sig
    x_prop32(x_root, x_atom_clientlist, 33, &ewmh_vals[0], n)
    # STACKING ORDER IS BOTTOM-TO-TOP and this list is neither: wsysd owns z
    # and this compositor does not read it back. Publishing the same order
    # under both names would be a claim about stacking that is not measured,
    # so _NET_CLIENT_LIST_STACKING carries the same set -- which is what the
    # spec requires of the SET -- and its order is not to be trusted. Said
    # here rather than left for a pager author to discover.
    x_prop32(x_root, x_atom_clientstk, 33, &ewmh_vals[0], n)


# FOCUS MOVED. Both halves matter and the second one is the half that was
# missing: the window LOSING focus has to have _NET_WM_STATE_FOCUSED taken off
# it, or two windows claim it at once and a toolkit draws two active title
# bars. Published on a CHANGE, so re-focusing the window that already has it
# costs nothing and a burst of keystrokes costs one round trip, not one per
# key.
def x_ewmh_active(win: int32):
    if x_atom_active == 0 or xfd < 0:
        return
    if win == x_ewmh_active_win:
        return
    prev: int32 = x_ewmh_active_win
    x_ewmh_active_win = win
    if prev != 0 and xwm_find(prev) >= 0:
        x_prop32(cast[uint32](prev), x_atom_wmstate, 4, &ewmh_vals[0], 0)
    ewmh_vals[0] = win
    x_prop32(x_root, x_atom_active, 33, &ewmh_vals[0], 1)
    # _NET_WM_STATE_FOCUSED on the window itself, which is what a toolkit
    # reads to draw its own active/inactive chrome.
    if win != 0:
        ewmh_vals[0] = cast[int32](x_atom_wmstate_focused)
        x_prop32(cast[uint32](win), x_atom_wmstate, 4, &ewmh_vals[0], 1)


def xwm_find(win: int32) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n_xw:
        if xw_win[i] == win:
            return cast[int32](i)
        i = i + 1
    return -1


def xwm_add(win: int32) -> int32:
    s: int32 = xwm_find(win)
    if s >= 0:
        return s
    if n_xw >= XWMAX:
        drop(S_XWLERR, "more X windows at once than the XWM table holds -- raise XWMAX")
        return -1
    i: uint64 = n_xw
    xw_win[i] = win
    xw_serlo[i] = 0
    xw_serhi[i] = 0
    xw_have[i] = 0
    xw_or[i] = 0
    xw_map[i] = 0
    xw_del[i] = -1                      # not asked yet; see xwm_read_protocols
    xw_tfor[i] = 0
    xw_par[i] = 0
    xw_dx[i] = 0
    xw_dy[i] = 0
    xw_x[i] = 0
    xw_y[i] = 0
    xw_w[i] = 0
    xw_h[i] = 0
    xw_surf[i] = 0
    xw_conn[i] = -1
    xw_tlen[i] = 0
    n_xw = n_xw + 1
    # xwl_managed is published out of n_xw, and publish_state only rewrites the
    # file when something is dirty. Without this line the state file said
    # `xwl_managed 0` while the window manager was holding two X windows -- a
    # counter lying by omission, which is the one thing this file may not do.
    stat_dirty = 1
    if verbose != 0:
        puts(1, "wsyswl: XWM now manages ")
        putd(1, cast[int32](n_xw))
        puts(1, " X windows (added ")
        putd(1, win)
        puts(1, ")\n")
    return cast[int32](i)


# Take the wsys window away and forget the association, but keep the entry: an
# X client may unmap and remap the same window, and Xwayland makes a NEW
# wl_surface with a NEW serial each time.
def xwm_unpair(i: uint64):
    if xw_surf[i] != 0 and xw_conn[i] >= 0:
        c: uint64 = cast[uint64](xw_conn[i])
        si: uint64 = oidx(c, cast[uint32](xw_surf[i]))
        if obj_type[si] == T_SURFACE:
            if obj_a[si] > 0:
                win_close(obj_a[si])
                obj_a[si] = 0
            obj_e[si] = -1
    xw_surf[i] = 0
    xw_conn[i] = -1
    xw_have[i] = 0
    xw_serlo[i] = 0
    xw_serhi[i] = 0


# A CONNECTION WENT AWAY, so every association through it is stale -- and
# staleness here is not harmless. conn_free blanks the object table and the
# NEXT client to take that slot re-uses the same small object ids, so an
# xw_surf left pointing at "surface 7 on connection 0" would come back to life
# aimed at an unrelated client's surface. Cleared by hand rather than left to
# the obj_type guard, which cannot tell a dead id from a recycled one.
def xwm_conn_gone(c: uint64):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n_xw:
        if xw_conn[i] == cast[int32](c):
            xw_surf[i] = 0
            xw_conn[i] = -1
            xw_have[i] = 0
            xw_serlo[i] = 0
            xw_serhi[i] = 0
        i = i + 1


def xwm_del(i: uint64):
    xwm_unpair(i)
    stat_dirty = 1
    k: uint64 = i
    while k + 1 < n_xw:
        xw_win[k] = xw_win[k + 1]
        xw_serlo[k] = xw_serlo[k + 1]
        xw_serhi[k] = xw_serhi[k + 1]
        xw_have[k] = xw_have[k + 1]
        xw_or[k] = xw_or[k + 1]
        xw_map[k] = xw_map[k + 1]
        xw_del[k] = xw_del[k + 1]
        xw_tfor[k] = xw_tfor[k + 1]
        xw_par[k] = xw_par[k + 1]
        xw_dx[k] = xw_dx[k + 1]
        xw_dy[k] = xw_dy[k + 1]
        xw_x[k] = xw_x[k + 1]
        xw_y[k] = xw_y[k + 1]
        xw_w[k] = xw_w[k + 1]
        xw_h[k] = xw_h[k + 1]
        xw_surf[k] = xw_surf[k + 1]
        xw_conn[k] = xw_conn[k + 1]
        xw_tlen[k] = xw_tlen[k + 1]
        t: uint64 = 0
        while t < XTITLE_CAP:
            xw_title[k * XTITLE_CAP + t] = xw_title[(k + 1) * XTITLE_CAP + t]
            t = t + 1
        k = k + 1
    n_xw = n_xw - 1


# THE ASSOCIATION, from the X side: this X window's serial is known, find the
# wl_surface that claimed the same one. obj_e on a wl_surface is -1 for "an
# Xwayland surface waiting for its X window" and the X window id once paired.
def xwm_pair(i: uint64) -> int32:
    if xw_have[i] == 0:
        return 0
    if xw_surf[i] != 0:
        return 1
    c: uint64 = 0
    while c < MAXCONN:
        if conn_inuse[c] != 0:
            o: uint64 = 1
            while o < MAXOBJ:
                oi: uint64 = c * MAXOBJ + o
                if obj_type[oi] == T_SURFACE and obj_e[oi] == -1 \
                        and obj_h[oi] == xw_serlo[i] \
                        and obj_i[oi] == xw_serhi[i]:
                    obj_e[oi] = xw_win[i]
                    xw_surf[i] = cast[int32](o)
                    xw_conn[i] = cast[int32](c)
                    hit(S_XWLPAIR)
                    return 1
                o = o + 1
        c = c + 1
    return 0


# THE WSYS WINDOW THIS X WINDOW IS BEING SHOWN IN, or 0. The chain is
# X window -> wl_surface -> wsys wid, and every link can be absent: a window
# that has not been paired, a connection that has gone, a surface that has not
# committed a frame yet.
def xwm_wid(k: uint64) -> int32:
    if xw_surf[k] == 0 or xw_conn[k] < 0:
        return 0
    c: uint64 = cast[uint64](xw_conn[k])
    if conn_inuse[c] == 0:
        return 0
    si: uint64 = oidx(c, cast[uint32](xw_surf[k]))
    if obj_type[si] != T_SURFACE:
        return 0
    return obj_a[si]


# TELL THE X CLIENT WHERE IT IS -- the ConfigureNotify half of rootless, and
# the thing whose absence §8b named.
#
# wsysd decides where a window LANDS ON THE DESKTOP. Until this, the X client
# inside it was never told: a program that asked its own geometry got the
# position it opened at for the rest of its life, and -- worse -- an
# override-redirect menu placed at "my parent's corner plus twenty" was placed
# relative to a corner that had not been true since the user first dragged the
# window. Pointer coordinates were never affected (they are surface-local and
# Xwayland adds the origin itself), which is why everything LOOKED right.
#
# So the X window is kept at the same coordinates as its Hamnix window. The X
# server turns a ConfigureWindow on a managed toplevel into a ConfigureNotify
# to its client, which is the event the client is waiting for. It also means
# the client's own idea of the screen and the desktop's agree, so a menu
# opened AFTER a move lands in the right place with no help at all.
def xwm_push_geom(k: uint64, gx: int32, gy: int32, gw: int32, gh: int32):
    if xw_x[k] == gx and xw_y[k] == gy and xw_w[k] == gw and xw_h[k] == gh:
        return
    xw_x[k] = gx
    xw_y[k] = gy
    xw_w[k] = gw
    xw_h[k] = gh
    x_grant_configure(cast[uint32](xw_win[k]), 15, gx, gy, gw, gh, 0)
    hit(S_XCONF)


# A menu that is ALREADY OPEN when its parent is dragged. Everything opened
# after the move is placed correctly by the client itself, because of the sync
# above; this is the one case the client cannot get right, because nothing
# tells it.
def xwm_move_or_children(k: uint64):
    par: int32 = xw_win[k]
    j: uint64 = 0
    while j < n_xw:
        if xw_or[j] != 0 and xw_map[j] != 0 and xw_par[j] == par:
            nx: int32 = xw_x[k] + xw_dx[j]
            ny: int32 = xw_y[k] + xw_dy[j]
            wid: int32 = xwm_wid(j)
            if wid > 0 and (xw_x[j] != nx or xw_y[j] != ny):
                xwm_push_geom(j, nx, ny, xw_w[j], xw_h[j])
                send_geometry(wid, nx, ny, xw_w[j], xw_h[j])
                sl: int32 = win_slot(wid)
                if sl >= 0:
                    w_x[cast[uint64](sl)] = nx
                    w_y[cast[uint64](sl)] = ny
                hit(S_ORFOLLOW)
        j = j + 1


# Attribute a just-mapped override-redirect window to the toplevel it opened
# over, and remember where inside it. WM_TRANSIENT_FOR first when the client
# bothered to set it -- a menu that names its own parent is not guessed at --
# and otherwise the topmost managed toplevel whose rectangle contains the
# menu's corner, which is what a menu opening off a menu bar looks like.
def xwm_attach_or(i: uint64):
    if xw_or[i] == 0:
        return
    par: int32 = xw_tfor[i]
    if par == 0:
        # THE INNERMOST CONTAINING RECTANGLE, not the last one in the table.
        # This loop used to keep the last match, which is X CREATION order and
        # not stacking order -- with two overlapping toplevels a menu opened
        # over the older one was attributed to the newer, so dragging the
        # WRONG window took the menu with it and dragging the real parent left
        # it behind. This compositor cannot read wsysd's z from here, so the
        # smallest enclosing window is the honest proxy: a menu bar inside a
        # dialog inside a main window belongs to the dialog.
        best: int64 = -1
        j: uint64 = 0
        while j < n_xw:
            if j != i and xw_or[j] == 0 and xw_map[j] != 0 \
                    and xw_w[j] > 0 and xw_h[j] > 0:
                if xw_x[i] >= xw_x[j] and xw_x[i] < xw_x[j] + xw_w[j] \
                        and xw_y[i] >= xw_y[j] and xw_y[i] < xw_y[j] + xw_h[j]:
                    area: int64 = cast[int64](xw_w[j]) * cast[int64](xw_h[j])
                    if best < 0 or area < best:
                        best = area
                        par = xw_win[j]
            j = j + 1
    if par == 0:
        return
    k: int32 = xwm_find(par)
    if k < 0:
        return
    xw_par[i] = par
    xw_dx[i] = xw_x[i] - xw_x[cast[uint64](k)]
    xw_dy[i] = xw_y[i] - xw_y[cast[uint64](k)]


# WM_TRANSIENT_FOR, read on map. Two things use it: a menu that names its own
# parent above, and the z band below -- a dialog belongs on top of the window
# it came from, and until now it stayed there only by the luck of being
# created later.
def xwm_read_transient(i: uint64):
    xw_tfor[i] = 0
    if x_get_prop(cast[uint32](xw_win[i]), 68) == 0:     # WM_TRANSIENT_FOR
        return
    if x_prop_fmt != 32 or x_prop_n < 1 or xrep_n < 4:
        return
    xw_tfor[i] = cast[int32](ru32(&xrep[0], 0))


# ... and from the Wayland side, because set_serial and the X property arrive
# in either order and neither is allowed to be the one that gets there second
# and is ignored.
def xwm_pair_surface(c: uint64, surf: uint32):
    si: uint64 = oidx(c, surf)
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n_xw:
        if xw_have[i] != 0 and xw_surf[i] == 0 \
                and xw_serlo[i] == obj_h[si] and xw_serhi[i] == obj_i[si]:
            obj_e[si] = xw_win[i]
            xw_surf[i] = cast[int32](surf)
            xw_conn[i] = cast[int32](c)
            hit(S_XWLPAIR)
            return
        i = i + 1


def xwm_read_serial(i: uint64):
    if x_atom_serial == 0:
        return
    r: int32 = x_get_prop(cast[uint32](xw_win[i]), x_atom_serial)
    if verbose != 0:
        puts(1, "wsyswl: WL_SURFACE_SERIAL on ")
        putd(1, xw_win[i])
        puts(1, ": reply ")
        putd(1, r)
        puts(1, " fmt ")
        putd(1, x_prop_fmt)
        puts(1, " nitems ")
        putd(1, cast[int32](x_prop_n))
        puts(1, "\n")
    if r == 0:
        return
    if x_prop_fmt != 32 or x_prop_n < 2:
        return
    xw_serlo[i] = cast[int32](ru32(&xrep[0], 0))
    xw_serhi[i] = cast[int32](ru32(&xrep[0], 4))
    xw_have[i] = 1
    p: int32 = xwm_pair(i)
    if verbose != 0:
        puts(1, "wsyswl: serial ")
        putd(1, xw_serlo[i])
        puts(1, ",")
        putd(1, xw_serhi[i])
        puts(1, " paired ")
        putd(1, p)
        puts(1, "\n")


# WHERE THIS TITLE GOES, AND WHERE IT DOES NOT. It is the wsys window's
# `title` verb -- the same one send_title() writes for a native Wayland client
# -- so an X window is named in exactly the place anything reading the window
# table would look for a name. It is NOT visible on the title bar: wsysd's
# decoration (user/wsysd.ad, `Server-side decoration`) fills a coloured bar and
# renders no text for ANY window, native or bridged. Checked rather than
# assumed -- a 4x crop of the bar over a window titled "Hello Rootless" is
# plain blue. Saying so here so the next person does not spend an afternoon
# debugging an X property that is arriving correctly.
def xwm_apply_title(i: uint64, wid: int32):
    if xw_tlen[i] == 0:
        return
    at: uint64 = 0
    p: Ptr[uint8] = "title "
    k: uint64 = 0
    while p[k] != 0:
        ctlbuf[at] = p[k]
        at = at + 1
        k = k + 1
    k = 0
    while k < xw_tlen[i]:
        b: uint8 = xw_title[i * XTITLE_CAP + k]
        # A newline inside a title would end the ctl line early and turn the
        # rest of somebody's window title into a verb.
        if b == 10 or b == 0:
            b = 32
        ctlbuf[at] = b
        at = at + 1
        k = k + 1
    ctlbuf[at] = 10
    ctlbuf[at + 1] = 0
    win_ctl(wid, &ctlbuf[0])


def xwm_read_title(i: uint64):
    win: uint32 = cast[uint32](xw_win[i])
    got: int32 = 0
    if x_get_prop(win, x_atom_netname) != 0 and x_prop_n > 0:
        got = 1
    if got == 0:
        if x_get_prop(win, 39) != 0 and x_prop_n > 0:      # WM_NAME, predefined
            got = 1
    if got == 0:
        return
    n: uint64 = cast[uint64](x_prop_n)
    if n > XTITLE_CAP - 1:
        n = XTITLE_CAP - 1
    if n > xrep_n:
        n = xrep_n
    k: uint64 = 0
    while k < n:
        xw_title[i * XTITLE_CAP + k] = xrep[k]
        k = k + 1
    xw_tlen[i] = n
    # If the window is already on the screen, rename it now: a title that only
    # arrives if you happen to ask before the first frame is a title bar that
    # says "Wayland" for the life of the window.
    if xw_surf[i] != 0 and xw_conn[i] >= 0:
        si: uint64 = oidx(cast[uint64](xw_conn[i]), cast[uint32](xw_surf[i]))
        if obj_type[si] == T_SURFACE and obj_a[si] > 0:
            xwm_apply_title(i, obj_a[si])


def xwm_event():
    # int32 and not uint8: the event code is compared against a dozen small
    # literals below, and a uint8 on one side of those comparisons is the kind
    # of quiet mismatch that makes every branch fall through and the window
    # manager sit there receiving events and managing nothing.
    t: int32 = cast[int32](xin[0] & 127)
    if verbose != 0:
        puts(1, "wsyswl: X event ")
        putd(1, t)
        puts(1, " win ")
        putd(1, cast[int32](ru32(&xin[0], 8)))
        puts(1, "\n")
    win: int32 = 0
    s: int32 = 0
    if t == 16:                                  # CreateNotify
        win = cast[int32](ru32(&xin[0], 8))
        s = xwm_add(win)
        if s >= 0:
            i: uint64 = cast[uint64](s)
            xw_x[i] = xs16(&xin[0], 12)
            xw_y[i] = xs16(&xin[0], 14)
            xw_w[i] = cast[int32](xget16(&xin[0], 16))
            xw_h[i] = cast[int32](xget16(&xin[0], 18))
            xw_or[i] = cast[int32](xin[22])
            # PropertyChange BEFORE the window is mapped, or WL_SURFACE_SERIAL
            # can be set in the gap and never announced.
            x_select_input(cast[uint32](win), 0x420000)
            x_allow_commits(cast[uint32](win))
    elif t == 20:                                # MapRequest
        win = cast[int32](ru32(&xin[0], 8))
        s = xwm_add(win)
        if s >= 0:
            x_allow_commits(cast[uint32](win))
        # THE WHOLE POINT OF SubstructureRedirect: the map does not happen
        # unless we do it. A window manager that redirects and then never maps
        # is matchbox's failure with the volume turned down.
        x_map_window(cast[uint32](win))
    elif t == 19:                                # MapNotify
        win = cast[int32](ru32(&xin[0], 8))
        s = xwm_add(win)
        if s >= 0:
            i2: uint64 = cast[uint64](s)
            xw_map[i2] = 1
            xw_or[i2] = cast[int32](xin[12])
            xwm_read_serial(i2)
            xwm_read_title(i2)
            xwm_read_protocols(i2)
            xwm_read_transient(i2)
            xwm_attach_or(i2)
            # _NET_WM_STATE exists on every managed window from the moment it
            # is mapped, even when it is empty: a toolkit asking "am I
            # maximised" must be able to tell "no" from "nobody answered".
            x_prop32(cast[uint32](win), x_atom_wmstate, 4, &ewmh_vals[0], 0)
            x_ewmh_publish()
    elif t == 18:                                # UnmapNotify
        win = cast[int32](ru32(&xin[0], 8))
        s = xwm_find(win)
        if s >= 0:
            xw_map[cast[uint64](s)] = 0
            xwm_unpair(cast[uint64](s))
            x_ewmh_publish()
    elif t == 17:                                # DestroyNotify
        win = cast[int32](ru32(&xin[0], 8))
        s = xwm_find(win)
        if s >= 0:
            xwm_del(cast[uint64](s))
            x_ewmh_publish()
    elif t == 22:                                # ConfigureNotify
        win = cast[int32](ru32(&xin[0], 8))
        s = xwm_find(win)
        if s >= 0:
            i3: uint64 = cast[uint64](s)
            xw_x[i3] = xs16(&xin[0], 16)
            xw_y[i3] = xs16(&xin[0], 18)
            xw_w[i3] = cast[int32](xget16(&xin[0], 20))
            xw_h[i3] = cast[int32](xget16(&xin[0], 22))
            # An X client is free to move its OWN toplevel, and an open menu
            # has to come with it whoever asked. This is the same follow the
            # desktop's drag gets, on the other side of the same fact.
            if xw_or[i3] == 0:
                xwm_move_or_children(i3)
    elif t == 23:                                # ConfigureRequest
        win = cast[int32](ru32(&xin[0], 8))
        x_grant_configure(cast[uint32](win), xget16(&xin[0], 26),
                          xs16(&xin[0], 16), xs16(&xin[0], 18),
                          cast[int32](xget16(&xin[0], 20)),
                          cast[int32](xget16(&xin[0], 22)),
                          cast[int32](xget16(&xin[0], 24)))
    elif t == 33:                                # ClientMessage
        # HOW THE SERIAL ACTUALLY ARRIVES, and it is not where the protocol
        # description made it sound. xwayland-shell-v1 says the serial "is set
        # on the X11 window", and it is not: Xwayland 24.1 delivers it as a
        # ClientMessage of type WL_SURFACE_SERIAL, addressed to the X window,
        # sent to the ROOT with SubstructureRedirect -- which is to say, to
        # whoever manages the screen. Measured: the toplevel's full property
        # list has WM_NAME, WM_CLASS, WM_PROTOCOLS, _NET_WM_PID and the
        # _XWAYLAND_ALLOW_COMMITS we set ourselves, and NO WL_SURFACE_SERIAL --
        # with and without a `-wm` fd, both tried. data.l[0] is the low half,
        # data.l[1] the high half, exactly as set_serial sends them.
        atom2: uint32 = ru32(&xin[0], 8)
        if atom2 == x_atom_serial:
            win = cast[int32](ru32(&xin[0], 4))
            s = xwm_add(win)
            if s >= 0:
                i4: uint64 = cast[uint64](s)
                xw_serlo[i4] = cast[int32](ru32(&xin[0], 12))
                xw_serhi[i4] = cast[int32](ru32(&xin[0], 16))
                xw_have[i4] = 1
                xwm_pair(i4)
    elif t == 28:                                # PropertyNotify
        win = cast[int32](ru32(&xin[0], 4))
        atom: uint32 = ru32(&xin[0], 8)
        s = xwm_find(win)
        if s >= 0 and xin[16] == 0:              # state = NewValue
            if atom == x_atom_serial:
                xwm_read_serial(cast[uint64](s))
            elif atom == x_atom_netname or atom == 39:
                xwm_read_title(cast[uint64](s))


def x_drop_conn():
    if xfd >= 0:
        sys_close(xfd)
    xfd = -1
    xdead = 0
    xdefer_n = 0
    while n_xw > 0:
        xwm_del(n_xw - 1)


def xwm_connect() -> int32:
    fd: int32 = sys_unix_connect(cast[Ptr[char]](&xwm_path[0]))
    if fd < 0:
        return 0
    xfd = fd
    xdead = 0
    xseq = 0
    xerr_seq = 0
    xerr_code = 0
    # 'l', major 11, minor 0, no authorisation. The Xwayland this manages is
    # started by the session with no -auth; a server that wanted a cookie
    # refuses here and says so rather than hanging.
    xzero(12)
    xreq[0] = 108
    xput16(&xreq[0], 2, 11)
    sent: uint64 = 0
    while sent < 12:
        k: int64 = sys_write(xfd, &xreq[sent], 12 - sent)
        if k <= 0:
            x_drop_conn()
            return 0
        sent = sent + cast[uint64](k)
    if x_read_exact(&xin[0], 8) == 0:
        x_drop_conn()
        return 0
    if xin[0] != 1:
        puts(2, "wsyswl: the X server REFUSED this compositor's window-manager connection\n")
        x_drop_conn()
        return 0
    body: uint64 = cast[uint64](xget16(&xin[0], 6)) * 4
    if body > 4096:
        body = 4096
    if x_read_exact(&xrep[0], body) == 0:
        x_drop_conn()
        return 0
    x_resbase = ru32(&xrep[0], 4)
    x_maxreq  = xget16(&xrep[0], 18)
    vlen: uint64 = cast[uint64](xget16(&xrep[0], 16))
    nfmt: uint64 = cast[uint64](xrep[21])
    nscr: uint64 = cast[uint64](xrep[20])
    off: uint64 = 32 + (vlen + 3) / 4 * 4 + nfmt * 8
    if nscr == 0 or off + 4 > body:
        puts(2, "wsyswl: the X setup reply has no screen -- cannot manage windows\n")
        x_drop_conn()
        return 0
    x_root = ru32(&xrep[0], off)

    # SubstructureRedirect | SubstructureNotify on the root. This is the whole
    # claim: from here on nothing gets mapped on that screen unless this
    # compositor maps it.
    rseq: uint32 = x_select_input(x_root, 0x180000)
    x_atom_serial  = x_intern("WL_SURFACE_SERIAL")
    x_atom_allow   = x_intern("_XWAYLAND_ALLOW_COMMITS")
    x_atom_netname = x_intern("_NET_WM_NAME")
    x_atom_protocols = x_intern("WM_PROTOCOLS")
    x_atom_delete    = x_intern("WM_DELETE_WINDOW")
    # Only ONE X client may hold SubstructureRedirect on a root window; the
    # second gets BadAccess and silently manages nothing. That is exactly the
    # shape a `jwm` left running inside the namespace would produce, so it is
    # named rather than discovered later as "rootless does not work".
    if xerr_code != 0 and xerr_seq == (rseq & 65535):
        puts(2, "wsyswl: ANOTHER X CLIENT ALREADY MANAGES THIS DISPLAY.\n")
        puts(2, "wsyswl: SubstructureRedirect on the root was refused (BadAccess), so no\n")
        puts(2, "wsyswl: X window can become a wsys window. A rootless session must start\n")
        puts(2, "wsyswl: NO window manager inside the namespace -- HAMNIX_X11_WM=rootless.\n")
        drop(S_XWLERR, "SubstructureRedirect refused -- a window manager is already running inside the namespace")
        x_drop_conn()
        return 0
    if x_atom_serial == 0:
        puts(2, "wsyswl: could not intern WL_SURFACE_SERIAL -- this X server is not an Xwayland\n")
        x_drop_conn()
        return 0
    x_ewmh_init()
    if x_composite_redirect() == 0:
        puts(2, "wsyswl: this X server has no Composite extension, so nothing on it can be\n")
        puts(2, "wsyswl: redirected -- Xwayland will make no wl_surface for any X window and\n")
        puts(2, "wsyswl: the display would come up managed and EMPTY. Refusing it instead.\n")
        drop(S_XWLERR, "no Composite extension -- a rootless X display cannot hand over surfaces")
        x_drop_conn()
        return 0
    if xerr_code != 0:
        puts(2, "wsyswl: XWM setup saw X error code ")
        putd(2, xerr_code)
        puts(2, " for request sequence ")
        putd(2, cast[int32](xerr_seq))
        puts(2, "\n")
    puts(1, "wsyswl: managing the X display at ")
    puts(1, &xwm_path[0])
    puts(1, " -- root window ")
    putd(1, cast[int32](x_root))
    puts(1, "\n")
    return 1


def xwm_pump():
    if xwm_on == 0:
        return
    if xfd < 0 or xdead != 0:
        # Xwayland is started by the session AFTER the compositor, so a failed
        # dial is normal for the first second or two. Retry about twice a
        # second and never busier than that.
        if xdead != 0:
            x_drop_conn()
        xwm_retry = xwm_retry + 1
        if xwm_retry >= 32:
            xwm_retry = 0
            xwm_connect()
        return
    # BOUNDED, because this is the compositor's frame loop. An X server with
    # something to say every microsecond -- a client resizing in a tight loop,
    # or a broken one -- would otherwise hold this `while` for ever and every
    # WAYLAND client on the server would stop being served, which is a
    # compositor-wide freeze caused by one X display. 256 messages is far more
    # than a frame's worth; whatever is left is read on the next pass, 16 ms
    # later, and nothing is dropped.
    n: int32 = 0
    while n < 256 and x_poll_msg(0) == 1:
        n = n + 1
        if xin[0] >= 2:
            xwm_event()
        elif xin[0] == 0:
            xerr_seq = xget16(&xin[0], 2)
            xerr_code = cast[int32](xin[1])
    # Parked events, in arrival order. xwm_event may park more (a GetProperty
    # round trip inside it), which is why this walks by index and only resets
    # the ring when it has caught up with itself.
    di: uint64 = 0
    while di < xdefer_n:
        i: uint64 = 0
        while i < 32:
            xin[i] = xdefer_buf[di * 32 + i]
            i = i + 1
        di = di + 1
        xwm_event()
    xdefer_n = 0


# --- shm buffer -> the window's backbuffer ----------------------------

def commit_buffer(c: uint64, surface_id: uint32, buf_id: uint32):
    if obj_get_type(c, buf_id) != T_BUFFER:
        drop(S_NOTBUF, "the attached object is not a wl_buffer this server knows")
        return
    bi: uint64 = oidx(c, buf_id)
    mi:     int32 = obj_g[bi]
    offset: int32 = obj_b[bi]
    width:  int32 = obj_c[bi]
    height: int32 = obj_d[bi]
    stride: int32 = obj_e[bi]
    if verbose != 0:
        puts(1, "wsyswl: commit_buffer map ")
        putd(1, mi)
        puts(1, " ")
        putd(1, width)
        puts(1, "x")
        putd(1, height)
        puts(1, "\n")
    if width < 1 or height < 1:
        drop(S_BADDIM, "a wl_buffer with no width or no height")
        return
    if mi < 0:
        drop(S_NOMAP, "the wl_buffer has no shm mapping (map_alloc failed earlier)")
        return
    base: uint64 = map_addr[c * MAXMAP + cast[uint64](mi)]
    maplen: uint64 = map_len[c * MAXMAP + cast[uint64](mi)]
    if base == 0:
        drop(S_NOBASE, "the shm mapping behind this wl_buffer is not mapped")
        return

    # A wl_subsurface's pixels belong to the toplevel that owns the window --
    # the child never gets a window of its own. Walk up so a client that draws
    # its content into a subsurface (Firefox does) still lands somewhere.
    eff: uint32 = surface_id
    guard: int32 = 0
    while guard < 4:
        p: int32 = surf_parent[oidx(c, eff)]
        if p == 0 or obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](p)) != T_SURFACE:
            guard = 4
        else:
            eff = cast[uint32](p)
            guard = guard + 1
    si: uint64 = oidx(c, eff)
    wid: int32 = obj_a[si]
    if wid <= 0:
        # A popup already knows where it goes -- its xdg_surface carries the
        # screen position worked out from the positioner at get_popup time --
        # and it goes there bare. Everything else is a toplevel and cascades.
        px: int32 = -1
        py: int32 = -1
        decor: int32 = 1
        xsl: int32 = -1
        role: int32 = obj_f[si]
        if role != 0 and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](role)) == T_XDG_SURF:
            ri: uint64 = oidx(c, cast[uint32](role))
            if obj_e[ri] == 1:
                px = obj_h[ri]
                py = obj_i[ri]
                decor = 0
        else:
            # A SURFACE WITHOUT A ROLE IS NOT A WINDOW. wl_pointer.set_cursor
            # takes an ordinary wl_surface and the client then attaches a
            # cursor image to it and commits, which is indistinguishable from
            # a window's first frame except for this: it never got an
            # xdg_surface. Before this check, moving the mouse over a client
            # opened a second 24x24 Hamnix window containing an arrow.
            #
            # ...UNLESS IT IS AN XWAYLAND SURFACE, which has no xdg_surface by
            # design: its role is an X window, and it arrives over
            # xwayland_shell_v1 instead. obj_e is 0 for an ordinary surface,
            # -1 for an X surface still waiting to be paired with its X
            # window, and the X window id once the two halves have met.
            xwl: int32 = obj_e[si]
            if xwl == 0:
                hit(S_NOROLE)
                return
            if xwl < 0:
                drop(S_XWLUNP,
                     "an Xwayland surface committed before its X window was associated")
                return
            xsl = xwm_find(xwl)
            if xsl < 0:
                drop(S_XWLUNP,
                     "an Xwayland surface whose X window this compositor no longer manages")
                return
            # An override-redirect X window is a menu or a tooltip: it placed
            # ITSELF, and it gets no title bar. A menu with a title bar is not
            # a menu -- the same rule this file already applies to xdg popups.
            if xw_or[cast[uint64](xsl)] != 0:
                px = xw_x[cast[uint64](xsl)]
                py = xw_y[cast[uint64](xsl)]
                decor = 0
        wid = win_open(c, eff, width, height, px, py, decor)
        if wid < 0:
            drop(S_NOWIN, "no wsys window could be opened for this surface")
            return
        obj_a[si] = wid
        if xsl >= 0:
            xk: uint64 = cast[uint64](xsl)
            xwm_apply_title(xk, wid)
            # WM_TRANSIENT_FOR: a dialog goes one z band above an ordinary
            # toplevel, which is where "keep this above its parent" is spelled
            # on this desktop. Until now it stayed above by the luck of having
            # been created later, and one click on the parent buried it.
            if xw_tfor[xk] != 0 and xw_or[xk] == 0:
                win_ctl(wid, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("z 8\n"))
            # AND TELL THE CLIENT WHERE IT LANDED. win_open cascades a new
            # toplevel; without this the X window still believes it is at the
            # corner it asked for, and the first menu it opens is placed there.
            sl0: int32 = win_slot(wid)
            if sl0 >= 0 and xw_or[xk] == 0:
                xwm_push_geom(xk, w_x[cast[uint64](sl0)], w_y[cast[uint64](sl0)],
                              width, height)
        high(S_WINHI, cast[int32](n_win))
    elif obj_c[si] != width or obj_d[si] != height:
        # A resize is a geometry announcement and nothing else: linux-wsys.c's
        # `geometry` verb calls bb_resize, which re-fits the v2 backbuffer to
        # the new size, so the stride follows the client. The window keeps the
        # corner it was placed at -- re-announcing (60,60) would walk a popup
        # away from the menu item that opened it.
        rs: int32 = win_slot(wid)
        if rs >= 0:
            send_geometry(wid, w_x[cast[uint64](rs)], w_y[cast[uint64](rs)],
                          width, height)
    obj_c[si] = width
    obj_d[si] = height

    slot: int32 = win_slot(wid)
    if slot < 0:
        drop(S_NOSLOT, "the surface's window has no wsysd slot any more")
        return
    dfd: int32 = w_draw[cast[uint64](slot)]

    rowbytes: uint64 = cast[uint64](width) * 4
    if rowbytes > 8192:
        rowbytes = 8192
    cols: int32 = cast[int32](rowbytes / 4)
    src: Ptr[uint8] = cast[Ptr[uint8]](base)
    y: int32 = 0
    while y < height:
        pos: uint64 = cast[uint64](offset) \
                    + cast[uint64](y) * cast[uint64](stride)
        if pos + rowbytes > maplen:
            # The row is off the end of what we have mapped. Everything below
            # it is too, so the rest of the window is simply not sent -- which
            # is a HALF-DRAWN window, not a missing one, and worth its own name.
            drop(S_SHORT, "a buffer row past the end of the shm mapping -- window cut short")
            y = height
        else:
            i: uint64 = 0
            while i < rowbytes:
                rowbuf[i] = src[pos + i]
                i = i + 1
            # ALPHA IS FORCED OPAQUE, for both formats. XRGB8888's fourth byte
            # is undefined and would show as transparent; ARGB8888's is real,
            # but a client's translucency is measured against ITS idea of what
            # is behind the window, and here that is the Hamnix desktop, which
            # it has never seen. An opaque window is the honest rendering of a
            # surface whose background we cannot reproduce.
            k: int32 = 0
            while k < cols:
                rowbuf[cast[uint64](k) * 4 + 3] = 255
                k = k + 1
            blithdr[0] = 66                              # 'B'
            put_le32(&blithdr[0], 1, 0)
            put_le32(&blithdr[0], 5, y)
            put_le32(&blithdr[0], 9, cols)
            put_le32(&blithdr[0], 13, y + 1)
            blithdr[17] = FMT_BGRA8888
            sys_write(dfd, &blithdr[0], 18)
            sys_write(dfd, &rowbuf[0], rowbytes)
            hit(S_ROWS)
            y = y + 1
    blithdr[0] = 68                                      # 'D', publish
    put_le32(&blithdr[0], 1, 0)
    put_le32(&blithdr[0], 5, 0)
    put_le32(&blithdr[0], 9, cols)
    put_le32(&blithdr[0], 13, height)
    sys_write(dfd, &blithdr[0], 17)
    hit(S_COMMIT)
    if verbose != 0:
        puts(1, "wsyswl: commit ")
        putd(1, width)
        puts(1, "x")
        putd(1, height)
        puts(1, " -> wid ")
        putd(1, wid)
        puts(1, "\n")


# --- request dispatch, one function per interface ---------------------

def do_display(c: uint64, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8], arglen: uint64):
    if opcode == 0:
        # sync(callback): answer immediately. Everything this server does is
        # synchronous inside one pump, so a roundtrip has nothing to wait for.
        if arglen >= 4:
            cb: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, cb, T_CALLBACK)
            serial = serial + 1
            send_callback_done(cb, serial)
            send_delete_id(cb)
            free_obj(c, cb)
    elif opcode == 1:
        if arglen >= 4:
            reg: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, reg, T_REGISTRY)
            conn_reg[c] = cast[int32](reg)
            send_global(reg, G_COMPOSITOR, "wl_compositor", 4)
            send_global(reg, G_SHM, "wl_shm", 1)
            send_global(reg, G_XDG, "xdg_wm_base", 1)
            send_global(reg, G_SEAT, "wl_seat", SEAT_VERSION)
            send_global(reg, G_OUTPUT, "wl_output", OUTPUT_VERSION)
            send_global(reg, G_DDM, "wl_data_device_manager", 3)
            send_global(reg, G_SUBCOMPOSITOR, "wl_subcompositor", 1)
            send_global(reg, G_VIEWPORTER, "wp_viewporter", 1)
            # v2 and not v3: at v3 the xdg_output events are latched by
            # wl_output.done rather than by zxdg_output_v1.done, and this
            # server sends the whole output description once and never
            # changes it, so the older latch is the one it can honour.
            send_global(reg, G_XDG_OUTPUT, "zxdg_output_manager_v1", 2)
            # ONLY WHEN THIS COMPOSITOR IS ACTUALLY MANAGING AN X DISPLAY.
            # xwayland_shell_v1 is a promise, not a courtesy: a client that
            # binds it hands over surfaces that will never be shown unless
            # something in here holds SubstructureRedirect and can pair them
            # with an X window. Advertising it without the window manager is
            # how a rootless Xwayland ends up with zero windows and no error.
            if xwm_on != 0:
                send_global(reg, G_XWL_SHELL, "xwayland_shell_v1", 1)


def do_registry(c: uint64, args: Ptr[uint8], arglen: uint64):
    # bind(name, interface(string), version, new_id)
    if arglen < 12:
        return
    name: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
    ilen: uint32 = ru32(args, 4)
    pad: uint64 = (cast[uint64](ilen) + 3) / 4 * 4
    after: uint64 = 8 + pad
    if arglen < after + 8:
        return
    bver:  uint32 = ru32(args, after)
    newid: uint32 = ru32(args, after + 4)
    if name == G_COMPOSITOR:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_COMPOSITOR)
    elif name == G_SHM:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_SHM)
        send_shm_format(newid, SHM_ARGB8888)
        send_shm_format(newid, SHM_XRGB8888)
    elif name == G_XDG:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_XDG_WM)
        conn_xdg_wm[c] = cast[int32](newid)
    elif name == G_SEAT:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_SEAT)
        conn_seat[c] = cast[int32](newid)
        obj_a[oidx(c, newid)] = cast[int32](bver)
        send_seat_caps(newid, SEAT_CAP_POINTER | SEAT_CAP_KEYBOARD)
        # wl_seat.name is a v2 event. A client that bound at v1 has no
        # listener slot for it and libwayland ABORTS on an event it cannot
        # dispatch, so this is gated rather than always sent.
        if bver >= 2:
            send_seat_name(newid, "seat0")
    elif name == G_OUTPUT:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_OUTPUT)
        conn_output[c] = cast[int32](newid)
        send_output_all(newid)
    elif name == G_DDM:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_DDM)
    elif name == G_SUBCOMPOSITOR:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_SUBCOMPOSITOR)
    elif name == G_VIEWPORTER:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_VIEWPORTER)
    elif name == G_XDG_OUTPUT:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_XDG_OUT_MGR)
    elif name == G_XWL_SHELL:
        new_obj(c, newid, T_XWL_SHELL)


def do_seat(c: uint64, obj: uint32, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
            arglen: uint64):
    if opcode == 0:
        if arglen >= 4:
            p: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, p, T_POINTER)
            # THE FIRST ONE WINS. A client may bind wl_seat more than once --
            # Firefox binds it through two registries -- and only put a
            # listener on the first. libwayland silently DISCARDS an event
            # addressed to a proxy with no listener, so aiming at the newest
            # wl_pointer produced a browser that rendered perfectly and could
            # not be clicked, with nothing logged anywhere. The first seat a
            # toolkit binds is the one it is actually using.
            if conn_pointer[c] == 0:
                conn_pointer[c] = cast[int32](p)
                # obj is the wl_seat, and do_registry stored the version it
                # was bound at in its obj_a. Read it here rather than
                # assuming SEAT_VERSION: what the server ADVERTISES and what
                # the client BOUND are different numbers.
                conn_ptrver[c] = obj_a[oidx(c, obj)]
    elif opcode == 1:
        if arglen >= 4:
            k: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, k, T_KEYBOARD)
            if conn_keyboard[c] == 0:            # the first one wins; see above
                conn_keyboard[c] = cast[int32](k)
            build_keymap()
            if keymap_fd >= 0:
                # The fd rides out with the keymap event itself: the client's
                # recvmsg has to see them together or it has a size and no
                # descriptor. out_fd is consumed by the next flush.
                out_fd = keymap_fd
                keymap_fd = -1
                send_kbd_keymap(k, cast[uint32](keymap_len))
                build_keymap()                  # rebuild for the next client
            sver: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, obj)]
            if sver >= 4:
                send_kbd_repeat_info(k, 25, 600)


# After a wl_pointer or wl_keyboard goes away, find another one on the same
# connection, or give up.
#
# THIS IS NOT HOUSEKEEPING. A wl_pointer.release frees the object id, and the
# client immediately RE-USES that number for the next thing it makes -- in
# Firefox's case a wl_region. Keeping the released id in conn_pointer meant
# every subsequent motion and click was addressed to a region, which libwayland
# silently drops: the browser rendered perfectly and could not be clicked, with
# no error anywhere. Firefox releases a pointer because it binds wl_seat twice,
# through two registries, and tidies the second one up.
def rebind_input(c: uint64, want: int32) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = MAXOBJ
    while i > 0:
        i = i - 1
        if obj_type[c * MAXOBJ + i] == want:
            return cast[int32](i)
    return 0


def do_shm(c: uint64, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8], arglen: uint64):
    if opcode != 0:
        return
    # create_pool(id, fd, size). The fd argument is not in `args` -- it came
    # over the socket as ancillary data and is waiting at the head of this
    # connection's descriptor queue.
    if arglen < 8:
        return
    nfd: int32 = fdq_pop(c)
    poolid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
    size:   uint32 = ru32(args, 4)
    new_obj(c, poolid, T_SHM_POOL)
    if valid_id(poolid) == 0:
        return
    pi: uint64 = oidx(c, poolid)
    obj_a[pi] = nfd
    if verbose != 0:
        puts(1, "wsyswl: create_pool fd ")
        putd(1, nfd)
        puts(1, "\n")
    if nfd < 0:
        return
    mi: int32 = map_alloc(c)
    if mi < 0:
        sys_close(nfd)
        obj_a[pi] = -1
        return
    # The DESCRIPTOR is the authority on how much is safe to map, not the size
    # in the request: a later wl_shm_pool.resize grows the pool without
    # re-sending the fd, and mapping past the end of the memfd is a SIGBUS on
    # the first touched page.
    fsz: int64 = sys_fd_size(nfd)
    want: uint64 = cast[uint64](size)
    if fsz > 0 and cast[uint64](fsz) > want:
        want = cast[uint64](fsz)
    m: uint64 = c * MAXMAP + cast[uint64](mi)
    map_addr[m] = sys_mmap_shared(nfd, want)
    map_len[m] = want
    obj_g[pi] = mi
    obj_b[pi] = cast[int32](size)


def do_shm_pool(c: uint64, obj: uint32, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
                arglen: uint64):
    pi: uint64 = oidx(c, obj)
    if opcode == 0:
        # create_buffer(id, offset, width, height, stride, format)
        if arglen >= 24:
            bufid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, bufid, T_BUFFER)
            if valid_id(bufid) != 0:
                bi: uint64 = oidx(c, bufid)
                obj_a[bi] = cast[int32](obj)             # the pool it came from
                obj_b[bi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 4))
                obj_c[bi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 8))
                obj_d[bi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 12))
                obj_e[bi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 16))
                obj_f[bi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 20))
                obj_g[bi] = obj_g[pi]                    # share the mapping
                map_ref_up(c, obj_g[pi])                 # and hold it open
    elif opcode == 1:
        # destroy: the pool object goes, the MAPPING stays -- the buffers cut
        # from it are still live and are the whole reason it was created.
        if obj_a[pi] >= 0:
            sys_close(obj_a[pi])
            obj_a[pi] = -1
        map_unref(c, obj_g[pi])                  # drop the pool's reference
        obj_g[pi] = -1
        free_obj(c, obj)
    elif opcode == 2:
        # resize(size): remap, because the pages past the old end are not in
        # our mapping at all. Buffers carved before the resize keep working:
        # they hold the same map index, which now points at the new address.
        if arglen >= 4:
            mi: int32 = obj_g[pi]
            ns: uint64 = cast[uint64](ru32(args, 0))
            if mi >= 0 and obj_a[pi] >= 0:
                m: uint64 = c * MAXMAP + cast[uint64](mi)
                if ns > map_len[m]:
                    if map_addr[m] != 0:
                        sys_munmap_at(map_addr[m], map_len[m])
                    map_addr[m] = sys_mmap_shared(obj_a[pi], ns)
                    map_len[m] = ns
            obj_b[pi] = cast[int32](ns)


def do_surface(c: uint64, obj: uint32, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
               arglen: uint64):
    si: uint64 = oidx(c, obj)
    if opcode == 1:
        # attach(buffer, x, y)
        if arglen >= 4:
            obj_b[si] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 0))
    elif opcode == 3:
        # frame(callback): queue it against this surface; it is armed by the
        # next commit of this surface and answered then, together with every
        # other callback queued on it.
        if arglen >= 4:
            fcb: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, fcb, T_CALLBACK)
            if fc_add(c, obj, fcb) == 0:
                # The table is full, which means a client is asking for frames
                # faster than it commits. Answering now is wrong by a frame and
                # right by everything else -- a dropped callback stalls a
                # client permanently.
                hit(S_FCFULL)
                evt_time = evt_time + 16
                send_callback_done(fcb, evt_time)
                send_delete_id(fcb)
                free_obj(c, fcb)
    elif opcode == 6:
        # commit. An xdg_surface's buffer only maps AFTER the client has acked
        # a configure, and the spec is strict about when that configure may be
        # sent: only IN RESPONSE TO the client's first, bufferless commit. GTK
        # (so Firefox, so XWayland) ignores a configure that arrives before it
        # and then waits forever for the one that never comes -- which looks
        # exactly like a client that failed to start.
        if obj_g[si] < 0:
            # First commit on this surface: it is now on the one output there
            # is. obj_g doubles as the "already told" flag -- enter must not
            # repeat, a client keeps a list and would end up on the same
            # monitor twice.
            outp: int32 = conn_output[c]
            if outp != 0 and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](outp)) == T_OUTPUT:
                send_surface_enter(obj, cast[uint32](outp))
            obj_g[si] = 1
        xrole: int32 = obj_f[si]
        configured: int32 = 1
        if xrole != 0 and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](xrole)) == T_XDG_SURF:
            xi: uint64 = oidx(c, cast[uint32](xrole))
            configured = obj_c[xi]
            if configured == 0 and obj_b[xi] == 0:
                popid: int32 = obj_g[xi]
                if obj_e[xi] == 1 and popid > 0 \
                        and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](popid)) == T_XDG_POPUP:
                    pp: uint64 = oidx(c, cast[uint32](popid))
                    send_popup_configure(cast[uint32](popid), obj_d[pp],
                                         obj_e[pp], obj_b[pp], obj_c[pp])
                tid: int32 = obj_d[xi]
                if tid != 0:
                    cw: int32 = 0
                    ch: int32 = 0
                    maxed: int32 = obj_b[oidx(c, cast[uint32](tid))]
                    if maxed != 0:
                        cw = MAX_W
                        ch = MAX_H
                    send_toplevel_configure(cast[uint32](tid),
                                            cast[uint32](cw), cast[uint32](ch),
                                            maxed)
                serial = serial + 1
                send_xdg_configure(cast[uint32](xrole), serial)
                obj_b[xi] = cast[int32](serial)
        pend: int32 = obj_b[si]
        if pend != 0:
            if configured != 0:
                commit_buffer(c, obj, cast[uint32](pend))
            else:
                drop(S_UNCONF,
                     "a buffer committed on an xdg_surface that never acked a configure")
            send_buffer_release(cast[uint32](pend))
            obj_b[si] = 0
        # Every frame callback queued on this surface is answered at once
        # rather than at a vblank. There is no vblank to wait for here, and a
        # client that throttles on it would otherwise never draw a second
        # frame. They all get the SAME timestamp, because they are all the
        # same frame -- a client that sees two times for one frame can compute
        # a nonsense refresh rate from the difference.
        evt_time = evt_time + 16
        fi: uint64 = c * FCPERCONN
        fend: uint64 = fi + FCPERCONN
        while fi < fend:
            if fc_conn[fi] == cast[int32](c) and fc_surf[fi] == cast[int32](obj):
                fcb2: uint32 = cast[uint32](fc_cb[fi])
                fc_conn[fi] = -1
                send_callback_done(fcb2, evt_time)
                send_delete_id(fcb2)
                free_obj(c, fcb2)
            fi = fi + 1
    elif opcode == 0:
        if obj_a[si] > 0:
            win_close(obj_a[si])
            obj_a[si] = 0
        fc_drop_surf(c, obj)
        free_obj(c, obj)


def do_xdg_wm(c: uint64, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8], arglen: uint64):
    if opcode == 2:
        # get_xdg_surface(id, surface): link both ways so commit can find the
        # role and the role can find the window.
        if arglen >= 8:
            xid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            surf: uint32 = ru32(args, 4)
            new_obj(c, xid, T_XDG_SURF)
            if valid_id(xid) != 0:
                obj_a[oidx(c, xid)] = cast[int32](surf)
            if obj_get_type(c, surf) == T_SURFACE:
                obj_f[oidx(c, surf)] = cast[int32](xid)
    elif opcode == 1:
        # create_positioner(id)
        if arglen >= 4:
            pid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, pid, T_POSITIONER)
            if valid_id(pid) != 0:
                obj_g[oidx(c, pid)] = 0     # anchor and gravity, both "none"


def do_positioner(c: uint64, obj: uint32, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
                  arglen: uint64):
    pi: uint64 = oidx(c, obj)
    if opcode == 1 and arglen >= 8:              # set_size(w, h)
        obj_a[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 0))
        obj_b[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 4))
    elif opcode == 2 and arglen >= 16:           # set_anchor_rect(x, y, w, h)
        obj_c[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 0))
        obj_d[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 4))
        obj_e[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 8))
        obj_f[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 12))
    elif opcode == 3 and arglen >= 4:            # set_anchor(anchor)
        obj_g[pi] = (obj_g[pi] & 0xff00) | (cast[int32](ru32(args, 0)) & 255)
    elif opcode == 4 and arglen >= 4:            # set_gravity(gravity)
        obj_g[pi] = (obj_g[pi] & 255) \
                  | ((cast[int32](ru32(args, 0)) & 255) << 8)
    elif opcode == 6 and arglen >= 8:            # set_offset(x, y)
        obj_h[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 0))
        obj_i[pi] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 4))


# Where a popup goes, in the coordinates of its parent xdg_surface's window
# geometry. This is the whole of xdg_positioner that a menu actually uses:
# take a point on the anchor rectangle chosen by ANCHOR, then hang the popup
# off that point in the direction given by GRAVITY, then add the offset.
#
# The constraint-adjustment rules (flip, slide, resize when the popup would
# leave the screen) are NOT implemented -- win_open clamps the popup onto the
# screen instead, which slides it without telling the client. A menu near the
# right edge therefore overlaps its parent item rather than flipping to its
# left.
#
# The anchor/gravity enumerations are the same eight values in the same order:
# 1 top, 2 bottom, 3 left, 4 right, 5 top_left, 6 bottom_left, 7 top_right,
# 8 bottom_right; 0 is "centre", and so is anything unrecognised.
def anchor_is_left(a: int32) -> int32:
    if a == 3 or a == 5 or a == 6:
        return 1
    return 0


def anchor_is_right(a: int32) -> int32:
    if a == 4 or a == 7 or a == 8:
        return 1
    return 0


def anchor_is_top(a: int32) -> int32:
    if a == 1 or a == 5 or a == 7:
        return 1
    return 0


def anchor_is_bottom(a: int32) -> int32:
    if a == 2 or a == 6 or a == 8:
        return 1
    return 0


def popup_x(c: uint64, pos: uint32) -> int32:
    pi: uint64 = oidx(c, pos)
    a: int32 = obj_g[pi] & 255
    g: int32 = (obj_g[pi] >> 8) & 255
    x: int32 = obj_c[pi] + obj_e[pi] / 2
    if anchor_is_left(a) != 0:
        x = obj_c[pi]
    elif anchor_is_right(a) != 0:
        x = obj_c[pi] + obj_e[pi]
    if anchor_is_left(g) != 0:
        x = x - obj_a[pi]
    elif anchor_is_right(g) == 0:
        x = x - obj_a[pi] / 2
    return x + obj_h[pi]


def popup_y(c: uint64, pos: uint32) -> int32:
    pi: uint64 = oidx(c, pos)
    a: int32 = obj_g[pi] & 255
    g: int32 = (obj_g[pi] >> 8) & 255
    y: int32 = obj_d[pi] + obj_f[pi] / 2
    if anchor_is_top(a) != 0:
        y = obj_d[pi]
    elif anchor_is_bottom(a) != 0:
        y = obj_d[pi] + obj_f[pi]
    if anchor_is_top(g) != 0:
        y = y - obj_b[pi]
    elif anchor_is_bottom(g) == 0:
        y = y - obj_b[pi] / 2
    return y + obj_i[pi]


def send_popup_configure(pop: uint32, x: int32, y: int32, w: int32, h: int32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(pop)
    o_u32(cast[uint32](x))
    o_u32(cast[uint32](y))
    o_u32(cast[uint32](w))
    o_u32(cast[uint32](h))
    emit_end(s, 0)


def do_xdg_surf(c: uint64, obj: uint32, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
                arglen: uint64):
    xi: uint64 = oidx(c, obj)
    if opcode == 1:
        # get_toplevel(id). No configure here -- see the commit handler.
        if arglen >= 4:
            tid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, tid, T_XDG_TOP)
            if valid_id(tid) != 0:
                obj_a[oidx(c, tid)] = cast[int32](obj)
            obj_d[xi] = cast[int32](tid)
    elif opcode == 2:
        # get_popup(id, parent, positioner). THIS xdg_surface takes the popup
        # role; `id` is the xdg_popup handle for it. The position is worked out
        # once, here, and stashed on the xdg_surface, because by the time a
        # buffer arrives the positioner may already have been destroyed -- a
        # client is free to destroy it as soon as the popup exists.
        if arglen >= 12:
            popid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            parent: uint32 = ru32(args, 4)
            pos:    uint32 = ru32(args, 8)
            new_obj(c, popid, T_XDG_POPUP)
            if valid_id(popid) != 0:
                obj_a[oidx(c, popid)] = cast[int32](obj)
            obj_e[xi] = 1                            # this surface is a popup
            obj_f[xi] = cast[int32](parent)
            obj_g[xi] = cast[int32](popid)
            lx: int32 = 0
            ly: int32 = 0
            pw: int32 = 0
            ph: int32 = 0
            if obj_get_type(c, pos) == T_POSITIONER:
                lx = popup_x(c, pos)
                ly = popup_y(c, pos)
                pw = obj_a[oidx(c, pos)]
                ph = obj_b[oidx(c, pos)]
            # The client is told the position in ITS parent's coordinates; the
            # window is placed in screen coordinates. They differ by wherever
            # wsysd put the parent, which is why win_open records that.
            obj_h[xi] = lx
            obj_i[xi] = ly
            if obj_get_type(c, parent) == T_XDG_SURF:
                psurf: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, parent)]
                if psurf != 0 and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](psurf)) == T_SURFACE:
                    pwid: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](psurf))]
                    ps: int32 = win_slot(pwid)
                    if ps >= 0:
                        obj_h[xi] = w_x[cast[uint64](ps)] + lx
                        obj_i[xi] = w_y[cast[uint64](ps)] + ly
            # No configure here, for the same reason a toplevel gets none
            # here: xdg-shell says the compositor answers the client's first,
            # bufferless commit, and a toolkit that is handed a configure
            # before it has asked drops it and waits for one that never comes.
            if valid_id(popid) != 0:
                popi: uint64 = oidx(c, popid)
                obj_b[popi] = pw
                obj_c[popi] = ph
                obj_d[popi] = lx
                obj_e[popi] = ly
    elif opcode == 4:
        # ack_configure(serial)
        obj_c[xi] = 1
        obj_b[xi] = 0


# Re-answer a toplevel whose state changed after it was already mapped.
#
# Deliberately silent before the first ack: the initial configure may only be
# sent in response to the client's first bufferless commit (see do_surface), so
# a set_maximized that arrives during window construction -- which is when
# Firefox sends it -- must be left for that path to pick up. Sending one here
# too would give the client two configures with two serials and it would ack
# the wrong one.
def reconfigure(c: uint64, top: uint32, xsid: uint32):
    if obj_get_type(c, xsid) != T_XDG_SURF:
        return
    xi: uint64 = oidx(c, xsid)
    if obj_c[xi] == 0:
        return
    maxed: int32 = obj_b[oidx(c, top)]
    cw: int32 = 0
    ch: int32 = 0
    if maxed != 0:
        cw = MAX_W
        ch = MAX_H
    send_toplevel_configure(top, cast[uint32](cw), cast[uint32](ch), maxed)
    serial = serial + 1
    send_xdg_configure(xsid, serial)


def do_xdg_top(c: uint64, obj: uint32, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
               arglen: uint64):
    ti: uint64 = oidx(c, obj)
    xsid: int32 = obj_a[ti]
    if opcode == 2:
        # set_title(title). It usually arrives before the window exists, so it
        # is stashed on the connection and applied at window-open time; if the
        # window is already up, push it now.
        if arglen >= 4:
            tlen: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            n: uint64 = 0
            while n + 1 < TITLE_CAP and n + 4 < arglen \
                  and cast[uint32](n) < tlen and args[4 + n] != 0:
                conn_title[c * TITLE_CAP + n] = args[4 + n]
                n = n + 1
            conn_title_len[c] = n
            if obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](xsid)) == T_XDG_SURF:
                surf: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](xsid))]
                if surf != 0 and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](surf)) == T_SURFACE:
                    wid: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](surf))]
                    if wid > 0:
                        send_title(wid, c)
    elif opcode == 9 or opcode == 11:
        # set_maximized / set_fullscreen. Both are answered the same way: this
        # desktop has one size for a big window and no fullscreen mode of its
        # own. What matters is that the answer ARRIVES -- see
        # send_toplevel_configure.
        obj_b[ti] = 1
        reconfigure(c, obj, cast[uint32](xsid))
    elif opcode == 10 or opcode == 12:
        # unset_maximized / unset_fullscreen: give the size back to the client.
        obj_b[ti] = 0
        reconfigure(c, obj, cast[uint32](xsid))
    elif opcode == 0:
        # destroy: the window goes with the role.
        if obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](xsid)) == T_XDG_SURF:
            surf2: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](xsid))]
            if surf2 != 0 and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](surf2)) == T_SURFACE:
                wid2: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](surf2))]
                if wid2 > 0:
                    win_close(wid2)
                    obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](surf2))] = 0
        free_obj(c, obj)


def do_subcompositor(c: uint64, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
                     arglen: uint64):
    if opcode == 1:
        # get_subsurface(id, surface, parent)
        if arglen >= 12:
            subid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            ch: uint32 = ru32(args, 4)
            pa: uint32 = ru32(args, 8)
            new_obj(c, subid, T_SUBSURFACE)
            if valid_id(subid) != 0:
                si: uint64 = oidx(c, subid)
                obj_a[si] = cast[int32](ch)
                obj_b[si] = cast[int32](pa)
            if obj_get_type(c, ch) == T_SURFACE \
                    and obj_get_type(c, pa) == T_SURFACE:
                surf_parent[oidx(c, ch)] = cast[int32](pa)


# wp_viewporter: the object graph, and nothing behind it.
#
# A viewport crops (set_source) and scales (set_destination) a surface's buffer
# on its way to the screen. Neither is needed here -- everything runs at scale
# 1 and a client's destination is its buffer size -- but the GLOBAL has to
# EXIST, because a toolkit that means to scale through a viewport and cannot
# find one does not fall back, it stops. Firefox creates a viewport for its
# content subsurface as part of bringing the window up.
#
# If a client ever asks for a destination that is not its buffer size, this
# silently draws the buffer instead. Scaling in the blit path is the fix, and
# is a real piece of work, not a line.
def do_viewporter(c: uint64, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8], arglen: uint64):
    if opcode == 1:
        # get_viewport(id, surface)
        if arglen >= 8:
            vid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
            new_obj(c, vid, T_VIEWPORT)
            if valid_id(vid) != 0:
                obj_a[oidx(c, vid)] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 4))


# --- the Wayland clipboard --------------------------------------------
#
# ONE CLIPBOARD, and this is the third road into it. /dev/snarf is the
# clipboard (user/linux-snarf.c); user/xsnarfd.ad bridges the X selections to
# it, one bridge per distribution; and this bridges wl_data_device. The reason
# the third one matters more than it sounds is that FIREFOX IN THE DEBIAN
# NAMESPACE IS A NATIVE WAYLAND CLIENT. Copying a URL out of it reached
# nothing before this, while the same copy out of an X application worked --
# and a user cannot tell which toolkit an application uses, so the whole thing
# read as "the clipboard works sometimes".
#
# THE MODEL, and why it is not X's. In X a selection has an OWNER and a paster
# asks that owner to convert it, which is why the bridge over there had to be
# a client that owns selections. Wayland inverts it: a client offers a
# wl_data_source, the COMPOSITOR tracks the current selection, and a client
# that wants to paste is handed a PIPE by the compositor. So there is no
# outside process that could do this job, and it lives here.
#
# THE SHAPE IN ONE PARAGRAPH. When a client sets a selection, the compositor
# immediately asks its source for the bytes over a pipe and puts them in
# /dev/snarf -- so the clipboard's single copy of the truth is the device, not
# a client that may exit. Every paste, from any client, is then answered out
# of /dev/snarf rather than forwarded to the owner. That is what makes it ONE
# clipboard rather than two that agree: a Wayland client pasting after the
# owning Firefox tab closed still gets the text, and text that arrived from an
# xterm through xsnarfd is offered to Wayland clients on exactly the same
# footing as text that came from a Wayland client.
#
# WHAT THAT COSTS, said plainly: a non-text offer is not carried. A client
# that copies an image and offers image/png and nothing else is REFUSED, by
# name, on the log -- rather than the compositor advertising image/png to the
# next client and having nothing to put down the pipe.


# A Wayland string argument at `at` in a request body, into `dst`.
def clip_arg_str(args: Ptr[uint8], arglen: uint64, at: uint64,
                 dst: Ptr[uint8], cap: uint64) -> uint64:
    if at + 4 > arglen:
        dst[0] = 0
        return arglen
    n: uint64 = cast[uint64](ru32(args, at))
    p: uint64 = at + 4
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i + 1 < n and i + 1 < cap and p + i < arglen:
        dst[i] = args[p + i]
        i = i + 1
    dst[i] = 0
    pad: uint64 = (n + 3) / 4 * 4
    return p + pad


def send_data_offer(ddev: uint32, offer: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ddev)
    o_u32(offer)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_offer_mime(offer: uint32, mime: Ptr[uint8]):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(offer)
    o_cstr(mime)
    emit_end(s, 0)


def send_selection(ddev: uint32, offer: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(ddev)
    o_u32(offer)
    emit_end(s, 5)


def send_source_send(src: uint32, mime: Ptr[uint8]):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(src)
    o_cstr(mime)
    emit_end(s, 1)


def send_source_cancelled(src: uint32):
    s: uint64 = emit_begin(src)
    emit_end(s, 2)


# Announce the CURRENT contents of /dev/snarf to one connection as a fresh
# wl_data_offer. Callers must have flushed anything already staged: `out` is
# one buffer for the whole server and this sends it to a specific client.
def clip_announce_to(c: uint64):
    dd: int32 = conn_ddev[c]
    if dd <= 0:
        return
    doff_next = doff_next + 1
    off: uint32 = DOFF_BASE + doff_next
    conn_offer[c] = cast[int32](off)
    send_data_offer(cast[uint32](dd), off)
    # EXACTLY WHAT lib/wlsnarf.ad SAYS IT CAN DELIVER, in preference order.
    # Advertising a type this compositor cannot answer is the same class of
    # defect as a device path with no server behind it, and user/xsnarfd.ad
    # refuses TIMESTAMP for the identical reason.
    send_offer_mime(off, "text/plain;charset=utf-8")
    send_offer_mime(off, "text/plain")
    send_offer_mime(off, "UTF8_STRING")
    send_offer_mime(off, "TEXT")
    send_selection(cast[uint32](dd), off)
    flush(conn_fd[c])


# ... to every client that has a wl_data_device, optionally skipping one.
#
# THE PROTOCOL SAYS "the client with keyboard focus" AND THIS SAYS "everyone",
# which is a deliberate simplification and is written down rather than
# implied. A client that is not focused has no reason to act on a selection
# and every toolkit simply records the latest offer; the alternative --
# withholding the clipboard until focus arrives -- is how a paste into a
# window that was never clicked returns nothing with no error. Skipping the
# connection that just gave us the bytes is not politeness: announcing an
# offer back at the owner is how a toolkit concludes it LOST the selection.
def clip_announce_all(skip: int32):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < MAXCONN:
        if conn_inuse[i] != 0 and conn_ddev[i] > 0:
            if cast[int32](i) != skip:
                clip_announce_to(i)
        i = i + 1


# A client asked to paste: wl_data_offer.receive(mime, fd).
def clip_offer_receive(c: uint64, args: Ptr[uint8], arglen: uint64):
    clip_arg_str(args, arglen, 0, &mimebuf[0], 256)
    fd: int32 = fdq_pop(c)
    if fd < 0:
        puts(2, "wsyswl: clipboard: a paste arrived with NO descriptor; ignored\n")
        return
    if wlsn_mime_bit(&mimebuf[0]) == 0:
        # REFUSED, AND SAID. Closing the fd is what the protocol gives a
        # compositor to say no with -- the client sees end-of-file and an
        # empty paste -- so the name of the type nobody could deliver is
        # printed here or it exists nowhere.
        puts(2, "wsyswl: clipboard: REFUSED a paste asking for ")
        puts(2, &mimebuf[0])
        puts(2, " -- this bridge delivers UTF-8 text only\n")
        sys_close(fd)
        return
    n: uint64 = wlsn_len()
    # A BLOCKING WRITE, AND IT IS BOUNDED BY CONSTRUCTION. The cap is 64 KiB
    # (SNARF_MAX) and a pipe's default capacity is 64 KiB, so a full clipboard
    # fits without a reader having read a byte and this cannot park the
    # compositor. A client that hands over something with a smaller buffer and
    # then does not read it could; that is the one case, and it is named here
    # rather than discovered.
    done: uint64 = 0
    while done < n:
        k: int64 = sys_write(fd, &wlsn_ptr()[done], n - done)
        if k <= 0:
            break
        done = done + cast[uint64](k)
    sys_close(fd)


# --- pulling a client's bytes into /dev/snarf -------------------------

def clip_pull_abort(why: Ptr[uint8]):
    if pull_fd >= 0:
        sys_close(pull_fd)
    pull_fd = -1
    pull_conn = -1
    pull_got = 0
    pull_over = 0
    puts(2, "wsyswl: clipboard: ")
    puts(2, why)
    puts(2, " -- /dev/snarf is UNCHANGED\n")


def clip_pull_finish():
    n: uint64 = pull_got
    over: uint64 = pull_over
    src: int32 = pull_conn
    sys_close(pull_fd)
    pull_fd = -1
    pull_conn = -1
    pull_got = 0
    pull_over = 0
    if over > 0:
        # TRUNCATED LOUDLY AND BY SIZE, the same rule /dev/snarf applies to a
        # 70 000-byte write from a Hamnix program and user/xsnarfd.ad applies
        # to an oversized X selection.
        puts(2, "wsyswl: clipboard: a Wayland client offered more than the 64 KiB clipboard: TRUNCATED, ")
        putd(2, cast[int32](over))
        puts(2, " bytes dropped\n")
    if wlsn_put(&pullbuf[0], n) == 0:
        puts(2, "wsyswl: clipboard: could not write /dev/snarf -- is this compositor running where the Hamnix runtime serves it?\n")
        return
    puts(1, "wsyswl: clipboard: Wayland -> /dev/snarf, ")
    putd(1, cast[int32](n))
    puts(1, " bytes\n")
    # Everyone ELSE now sees it. The owner is skipped for the reason
    # clip_announce_all gives, and xsnarfd's 4 Hz poll of the device carries
    # the same bytes on to the X side of every namespace.
    clip_announce_all(src)


# One non-blocking step of an in-flight pull, called every 16 ms frame.
def clip_pull_step():
    if pull_fd < 0:
        return
    while 1 == 1:
        room: uint64 = WLSN_MAX - pull_got
        if room == 0:
            # Past the cap: keep draining so the client's write completes and
            # it does not sit blocked on a pipe for ever, but count what is
            # being thrown away so the truncation can be named.
            n2: int64 = sys_read_nb(pull_fd, &mimebuf[0], 256)
            if n2 > 0:
                pull_over = pull_over + cast[uint64](n2)
                continue
            if n2 < 0:
                clip_pull_finish()
            return
        n: int64 = sys_read_nb(pull_fd, &pullbuf[pull_got], room)
        if n > 0:
            pull_got = pull_got + cast[uint64](n)
            continue
        if n < 0:                       # sys_read_nb: negative is TRUE EOF
            clip_pull_finish()
            return
        break                           # 0 = nothing yet; come back next frame
    pull_ticks = pull_ticks + 1
    if pull_ticks > PULL_TICKS_MAX:
        clip_pull_abort("a Wayland client never answered for the bytes it offered")


# wl_data_device.set_selection(source, serial)
def clip_set_selection(c: uint64, args: Ptr[uint8], arglen: uint64):
    if arglen < 4:
        return
    src: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
    if src == 0:
        # THE SELECTION IS GIVEN UP, AND THE CLIPBOARD IS NOT CLEARED. That is
        # the choice every clipboard manager makes and the one user/xsnarfd.ad
        # already made for X (§6.5): the alternative is that closing the
        # window you copied from empties the clipboard, which no one expects.
        if sel_conn == cast[int32](c):
            sel_conn = -1
            puts(1, "wsyswl: clipboard: a Wayland client gave up the selection; /dev/snarf keeps what it had\n")
        return
    if obj_get_type(c, src) != T_DATA_SOURCE:
        return
    bits: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, src)]
    pick: int32 = wlsn_pick(bits)
    if pick == 0:
        # NOTHING WE CAN CARRY. Refuse by name and tell the client, which is
        # what wl_data_source.cancelled is for -- a source left believing it
        # owns a selection the compositor dropped is the silent half of this.
        puts(2, "wsyswl: clipboard: a Wayland client offered the selection in NO type this bridge can deliver (it wants UTF-8 text); REFUSED, /dev/snarf is unchanged\n")
        flush(conn_fd[c])
        send_source_cancelled(src)
        flush(conn_fd[c])
        return
    if pull_fd >= 0:
        clip_pull_abort("a second copy arrived before the first was answered")
    if sys_pipe(&pipebuf[0]) != 0:
        puts(2, "wsyswl: clipboard: pipe(2) failed; the copy is LOST\n")
        return
    sel_conn = cast[int32](c)
    pull_conn = cast[int32](c)
    pull_fd = pipebuf[0]
    pull_got = 0
    pull_over = 0
    pull_ticks = 0
    # The write end rides out WITH the event, the way the keymap memfd does:
    # the client's recvmsg has to see them together or it has a mime type and
    # no descriptor. flush() closes it afterwards, which is what makes the
    # read end reach EOF when the client is done.
    flush(conn_fd[c])
    out_fd = pipebuf[1]
    send_source_send(src, wlsn_mime_name(pick))
    flush(conn_fd[c])


# The 4 Hz poll of /dev/snarf: has the HAMNIX side (or the X side, through
# user/xsnarfd.ad, which writes the same device) copied something new?
def clip_tick():
    if wlsn_poll() == 1:
        puts(1, "wsyswl: clipboard: /dev/snarf -> Wayland, ")
        putd(1, cast[int32](wlsn_len()))
        puts(1, " bytes\n")
        sel_conn = -1
        clip_announce_all(-1)


def clip_conn_reset(c: uint64):
    conn_ddev[c] = 0
    conn_offer[c] = 0
    if sel_conn == cast[int32](c):
        sel_conn = -1
    if pull_conn == cast[int32](c):
        clip_pull_abort("the client that owned the selection went away mid-copy")


def dispatch(c: uint64, obj: uint32, opcode: uint32, args: Ptr[uint8],
             arglen: uint64):
    # A wl_data_offer is an object THIS SERVER created, so its id is in the
    # server half of the id space and cannot be looked up in the per-connection
    # object table. It is matched by id, before anything else.
    if obj >= DOFF_BASE:
        if opcode == 1:                          # receive(mime, fd)
            clip_offer_receive(c, args, arglen)
        elif opcode == 2:                        # destroy
            if conn_offer[c] == cast[int32](obj):
                conn_offer[c] = 0
        # accept / finish / set_actions: consumed. This compositor carries a
        # selection, not a drag, so there is no action to negotiate.
        flush(conn_fd[c])
        return
    otype: int32 = obj_get_type(c, obj)
    fd: int32 = conn_fd[c]
    if obj == DISPLAY_ID:
        do_display(c, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_REGISTRY:
        if opcode == 0:
            do_registry(c, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_COMPOSITOR:
        if opcode == 0:
            if arglen >= 4:
                new_obj(c, ru32(args, 0), T_SURFACE)
        elif opcode == 1:
            if arglen >= 4:
                new_obj(c, ru32(args, 0), T_REGION)
    elif otype == T_SHM:
        do_shm(c, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_SHM_POOL:
        do_shm_pool(c, obj, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_SURFACE:
        do_surface(c, obj, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_XDG_WM:
        if opcode == 2 or opcode == 1:
            do_xdg_wm(c, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_XDG_SURF:
        do_xdg_surf(c, obj, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_XDG_TOP:
        do_xdg_top(c, obj, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_POSITIONER:
        do_positioner(c, obj, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_XDG_POPUP:
        if opcode == 0:
            # destroy: take the menu off the screen with it. Nothing else
            # closes an undecorated window -- there is no frame to click.
            xs: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, obj)]
            if obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](xs)) == T_XDG_SURF:
                ps2: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](xs))]
                if ps2 != 0 and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](ps2)) == T_SURFACE:
                    pw2: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](ps2))]
                    if pw2 > 0:
                        win_close(pw2)
                        obj_a[oidx(c, cast[uint32](ps2))] = 0
            free_obj(c, obj)
    elif otype == T_XWL_SHELL:
        if opcode == 1:
            # get_xwayland_surface(id, surface). The surface is marked as an X
            # surface HERE and not at set_serial, because a commit can land in
            # between and the difference between "no role" and "an X window
            # that has not been paired yet" is the difference between a
            # cursor and a missing window.
            if arglen >= 8:
                xsid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
                xsurf: uint32 = ru32(args, 4)
                new_obj(c, xsid, T_XWL_SURF)
                if valid_id(xsid) != 0:
                    obj_a[oidx(c, xsid)] = cast[int32](xsurf)
                if obj_get_type(c, xsurf) == T_SURFACE:
                    if obj_e[oidx(c, xsurf)] == 0:
                        obj_e[oidx(c, xsurf)] = -1
        elif opcode == 0:
            free_obj(c, obj)
    elif otype == T_XWL_SURF:
        if opcode == 0:
            # set_serial(serial_lo, serial_hi) -- the ONE fact this protocol
            # carries, and the only thing that ties a wl_surface to an X
            # window. The X half is the window's WL_SURFACE_SERIAL property.
            if arglen >= 8:
                s2: int32 = obj_a[oidx(c, obj)]
                if s2 != 0 and obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](s2)) == T_SURFACE:
                    s2i: uint64 = oidx(c, cast[uint32](s2))
                    obj_h[s2i] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 0))
                    obj_i[s2i] = cast[int32](ru32(args, 4))
                    if obj_e[s2i] == 0:
                        obj_e[s2i] = -1
                    xwm_pair_surface(c, cast[uint32](s2))
        elif opcode == 1:
            free_obj(c, obj)
    elif otype == T_SEAT:
        do_seat(c, obj, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_POINTER:
        if opcode == 1:                              # release
            free_obj(c, obj)
            if conn_pointer[c] == cast[int32](obj):
                conn_pointer[c] = rebind_input(c, T_POINTER)
    elif otype == T_KEYBOARD:
        if opcode == 0:                              # release
            free_obj(c, obj)
            if conn_keyboard[c] == cast[int32](obj):
                conn_keyboard[c] = rebind_input(c, T_KEYBOARD)
    elif otype == T_SUBCOMPOSITOR:
        do_subcompositor(c, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_VIEWPORTER:
        do_viewporter(c, opcode, args, arglen)
    elif otype == T_VIEWPORT:
        if opcode == 0:
            free_obj(c, obj)
    elif otype == T_XDG_OUT_MGR:
        if opcode == 1:
            # get_xdg_output(id, output)
            if arglen >= 8:
                xo: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
                new_obj(c, xo, T_XDG_OUT)
                send_xdg_output_all(xo)
    elif otype == T_XDG_OUT:
        if opcode == 0:
            free_obj(c, obj)
    elif otype == T_DDM:
        if opcode == 0:                          # create_data_source(id)
            if arglen >= 4:
                sid: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
                new_obj(c, sid, T_DATA_SOURCE)
                obj_a[oidx(c, sid)] = 0          # the mime types it offers
        elif opcode == 1:                        # get_data_device(id, seat)
            if arglen >= 4:
                did: uint32 = ru32(args, 0)
                new_obj(c, did, T_DATA_DEVICE)
                conn_ddev[c] = cast[int32](did)
                # A CLIENT THAT ARRIVES AFTER THE COPY MUST STILL SEE IT.
                # Without this, whatever is on the clipboard is announced only
                # on the next change, so the first paste of every newly
                # started program returns nothing -- and returns it silently.
                flush(conn_fd[c])
                if wlsn_len() > 0:
                    clip_announce_to(c)
    elif otype == T_DATA_SOURCE:
        if opcode == 0:                          # offer(mime_type)
            clip_arg_str(args, arglen, 0, &mimebuf[0], 256)
            b: int32 = wlsn_mime_bit(&mimebuf[0])
            obj_a[oidx(c, obj)] = obj_a[oidx(c, obj)] | b
        elif opcode == 1:                        # destroy
            free_obj(c, obj)
        # set_actions is v3 drag-and-drop; this compositor carries selections.
    elif otype == T_DATA_DEVICE:
        if opcode == 1:                          # set_selection(source, serial)
            clip_set_selection(c, args, arglen)
        elif opcode == 2:                        # release
            if conn_ddev[c] == cast[int32](obj):
                conn_ddev[c] = 0
            free_obj(c, obj)
        # start_drag (opcode 0) is drag-and-drop, which this compositor does
        # not carry: there is no drag on a wsys desktop to carry it to. It is
        # consumed rather than answered, and NOT advertised as working
        # anywhere -- docs/linux_clipboard.md §7.4 says so by name.
    elif otype == T_BUFFER:
        if opcode == 0:
            map_unref(c, obj_g[oidx(c, obj)])
            obj_g[oidx(c, obj)] = -1
            free_obj(c, obj)
            send_delete_id(obj)
    elif otype == T_OUTPUT:
        if opcode == 0:
            free_obj(c, obj)
    # Anything else -- region, callback, data device, an opcode from a newer
    # protocol version -- is consumed silently. A server that killed the
    # connection over an unknown request would break on every toolkit update.
    flush(fd)


# --- connections ------------------------------------------------------

def conn_gauge():
    n: int32 = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < MAXCONN:
        if conn_inuse[i] != 0:
            n = n + 1
        i = i + 1
    gauge(S_CONN, n)
    high(S_CONNHI, n)


def conn_reset(c: uint64):
    conn_acclen[c] = 0
    conn_reg[c] = 0
    conn_seat[c] = 0
    conn_pointer[c] = 0
    conn_ptrver[c] = 0
    conn_keyboard[c] = 0
    conn_xdg_wm[c] = 0
    conn_output[c] = 0
    conn_title_len[c] = 0
    clip_conn_reset(c)
    fc_drop_conn(c)
    fdq_head[c] = 0
    fdq_tail[c] = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < MAXOBJ:
        obj_type[c * MAXOBJ + i] = T_NONE
        surf_parent[c * MAXOBJ + i] = 0
        obj_g[c * MAXOBJ + i] = -1
        i = i + 1
    i = 0
    while i < MAXMAP:
        map_used[c * MAXMAP + i] = 0
        map_addr[c * MAXMAP + i] = 0
        map_len[c * MAXMAP + i] = 0
        map_ref[c * MAXMAP + i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    obj_type[c * MAXOBJ + 1] = T_DISPLAY        # wl_display is always id 1


def conn_free(c: uint64):
    if conn_inuse[c] == 0:
        return
    xwm_conn_gone(c)
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < MAXOBJ:
        oi: uint64 = c * MAXOBJ + i
        if obj_type[oi] == T_SHM_POOL:
            if obj_a[oi] >= 0:
                sys_close(obj_a[oi])
                obj_a[oi] = -1
        elif obj_type[oi] == T_SURFACE:
            if obj_a[oi] > 0:
                win_close(obj_a[oi])
        obj_type[oi] = T_NONE
        i = i + 1
    fc_drop_conn(c)
    clip_conn_reset(c)
    while fdq_head[c] != fdq_tail[c]:
        sys_close(fdq_pop(c))
    i = 0
    while i < MAXMAP:
        m: uint64 = c * MAXMAP + i
        if map_addr[m] != 0:
            sys_munmap_at(map_addr[m], map_len[m])
            map_addr[m] = 0
        map_used[m] = 0
        map_ref[m] = 0
        i = i + 1
    sys_close(conn_fd[c])
    conn_inuse[c] = 0
    conn_fd[c] = -1
    conn_gauge()
    puts(1, "wsyswl: client gone\n")


# Tell a client, in the protocol, that there was no room for it -- then close.
#
# wl_display.error(object_id, code, message) on object 1, opcode 0. Built here
# rather than in `out` so a refusal cannot touch another client's staged bytes
# or its pending descriptor; see the call site. The message names the limit and
# its CURRENT VALUE, read from MAXCONN rather than typed, so it can never drift
# from the number that actually refused the client.
def conn_refuse(fd: int32):
    m: uint64 = 0
    m = str_copy(&refusebuf[REFUSE_STR], "wsyswl: connection table full (MAXCONN=",
                 REFUSE_CAP - REFUSE_STR)
    m = emit_int(&refusebuf[REFUSE_STR], m, cast[int32](MAXCONN))
    m = m + str_copy(&refusebuf[REFUSE_STR + m],
                     ") -- this client was refused, not one of its windows",
                     REFUSE_CAP - REFUSE_STR - m)
    refusebuf[REFUSE_STR + m] = 0
    slen_with_nul: uint64 = m + 1
    # the string is padded to a 32-bit boundary, like every Wayland string arg
    padded: uint64 = (slen_with_nul + 3) & (0 - 4)
    size: uint64 = REFUSE_STR + padded
    i: uint64 = slen_with_nul
    while i < padded:
        refusebuf[REFUSE_STR + i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    put_le32(&refusebuf[0], 0, cast[int32](DISPLAY_ID))
    put_le32(&refusebuf[0], 4, cast[int32]((cast[uint32](size) << 16) | 0))
    put_le32(&refusebuf[0], 8, cast[int32](DISPLAY_ID))     # the object that failed
    put_le32(&refusebuf[0], 12, 2)                          # no_memory
    put_le32(&refusebuf[0], 16, cast[int32](slen_with_nul))
    sys_scm_send(fd, &refusebuf[0], size, -1)
    sys_close(fd)


def conn_accept():
    while 1 == 1:
        fd: int32 = sys_unix_accept(listen_fd)
        if fd < 0:
            return
        c: uint64 = 0
        placed: int32 = 0
        while c < MAXCONN and placed == 0:
            if conn_inuse[c] == 0:
                conn_inuse[c] = 1
                conn_fd[c] = fd
                conn_reset(c)
                placed = 1
                puts(1, "wsyswl: client connected\n")
            c = c + 1
        conn_gauge()
        if placed == 0:
            # THE CLIENT USED TO BE TOLD A LIE, and the server's honesty was
            # not the same thing as the client's.
            #
            # This closed the descriptor and printed the line below. The line
            # is true and it goes to the SERVER's stderr, where on a real
            # desktop nobody is looking. What the refused program saw was a
            # connect(2) that SUCCEEDED and then end-of-file, and what
            # libwayland makes of that is not "the server is full" -- measured
            # here, weston-simple-shm's ninth instance died saying:
            #
            #     No wl_shm global
            #
            # which sends whoever reads it looking for a missing protocol
            # global that is present and advertised. That is a gap answering
            # something SHAPED like an answer instead of the truth, which is
            # the one thing NORTH_STAR says must never happen.
            #
            # So the refusal is spoken in the protocol before the socket goes.
            # wl_display.error (object 1, opcode 0) is the wire's own way to
            # say why a connection is over: object id, a code, and a string
            # libwayland prints verbatim. no_memory (2) is the honest code --
            # a table with no free slot is the resource exhaustion the wire
            # has a word for -- and the string names the limit AND its value,
            # so the person reading it knows which number to raise.
            #
            # IT IS BUILT IN ITS OWN BUFFER AND NOT IN `out`, deliberately.
            # `out` is the shared staging area and `out_fd` is a DESCRIPTOR
            # waiting to be passed with the next flush -- the keymap memfd, or
            # a clipboard pipe. Staging this refusal there would, on the pass
            # where one of those is pending, hand a real client's descriptor to
            # the client being turned away and close it. A refusal must cost
            # the refused client and nobody else, which is exactly what this
            # test asserts three sections further down, so it must be true of
            # the refusal's own machinery first.
            conn_refuse(fd)
            # MAXCONN is the ceiling on how many INDEPENDENT clients this
            # server can carry, and independence is what the per-connection
            # tables above are for -- so refusing a connection is refusing a
            # whole client, not one window. Say so, and count it.
            # The line, VERBATIM, and the counter beside it. `drop` would
            # have printed "DROPPING FRAMES", which is the wrong noun for a
            # refused connection -- nothing was dropped, a whole program was
            # turned away -- and tests/linux/wsyswl_shared_fate.sh greps for
            # this exact sentence, which is the contract that a client getting
            # nothing is never turned away in silence.
            puts(2, "wsyswl: too many clients -- raise MAXCONN\n")
            hit(S_CONNFULL)


# Drain one client's socket and run every complete message in it.
#
# THE FD AND THE BYTES ARRIVE TOGETHER and must be kept together: the kernel
# attaches a client's shm memfd to the first byte of the sendmsg that carried
# wl_shm.create_pool, so a reader that took bytes with read(2) and went looking
# for the descriptor afterwards would find it gone. Every read here is a
# recvmsg, whether or not a descriptor is expected, and the descriptor is held
# until the create_pool in the same buffer consumes it.
def conn_pump(c: uint64) -> int32:
    fd: int32 = conn_fd[c]
    base: uint64 = c * ACC
    acclen: uint64 = conn_acclen[c]
    if acclen < ACC:
        recvn[0] = 0
        got: int64 = sys_scm_recv(fd, &conn_acc[base + acclen], ACC - acclen,
                                  &recvfds[0], 4, &recvn[0])
        if got < 0:
            return -1
        k: int32 = 0
        while k < recvn[0]:
            fdq_push(c, recvfds[k])
            k = k + 1
        acclen = acclen + cast[uint64](got)
    pos: uint64 = 0
    while acclen - pos >= 8:
        obj: uint32 = ru32(&conn_acc[base], pos)
        w2:  uint32 = ru32(&conn_acc[base], pos + 4)
        size: uint64 = cast[uint64](w2 >> 16)
        opcode: uint32 = w2 & 0xffff
        if size < 8:
            # A corrupt header cannot be resynchronised by scanning -- any
            # four bytes look like an object id. Drop the connection instead
            # of inventing a message boundary.
            conn_acclen[c] = 0
            return -1
        if acclen - pos < size:
            acclen = acclen                              # incomplete; wait
            pos = pos
            break
        dispatch(c, obj, opcode, &conn_acc[base + pos + 8], size - 8)
        pos = pos + size
    if pos > 0:
        rem: uint64 = acclen - pos
        k2: uint64 = 0
        while k2 < rem:
            conn_acc[base + k2] = conn_acc[base + pos + k2]
            k2 = k2 + 1
        acclen = rem
    conn_acclen[c] = acclen
    return 0


# --- input: the window's rings -> wl_pointer / wl_keyboard ------------
#
# wsysd routes input to a window by WRITING its /keys and /pointer rings --
# "d <code>\n" and "<kind> <x> <y> <buttons> <dz>\n" -- exactly as it does for
# a scene app. Nothing about Wayland reaches wsysd; this end reads the same two
# files any hamui client reads and turns the lines into protocol events. That
# is the whole reason a Wayland client can share a desktop with hamterm.

def parse_int_at(buf: Ptr[uint8], p: uint64, end: uint64) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = p
    neg: int32 = 0
    if i < end and buf[i] == 45:
        neg = 1
        i = i + 1
    v: int32 = 0
    while i < end and buf[i] >= 48 and buf[i] <= 57:
        v = v * 10 + cast[int32](buf[i]) - 48
        i = i + 1
    if neg != 0:
        return 0 - v
    return v


def skip_field(buf: Ptr[uint8], p: uint64, end: uint64) -> uint64:
    i: uint64 = p
    while i < end and buf[i] != 32:
        i = i + 1
    while i < end and buf[i] == 32:
        i = i + 1
    return i


# An ASCII byte from the cooked keyboard, as an evdev keycode plus whether it
# needs Shift. Returns code, or 0 for a byte with no key on a us layout.
def ascii_evdev(a: int32) -> int32:
    if a >= 97 and a <= 122:
        a = a - 32                                       # fold to uppercase
    if a >= 65 and a <= 90:
        # QWERTYUIOP ASDFGHJKL ZXCVBNM in evdev order.
        row: Ptr[uint8] = "QWERTYUIOP"
        i: int32 = 0
        while i < 10:
            if cast[int32](row[i]) == a:
                return 16 + i
            i = i + 1
        row = "ASDFGHJKL"
        i = 0
        while i < 9:
            if cast[int32](row[i]) == a:
                return 30 + i
            i = i + 1
        row = "ZXCVBNM"
        i = 0
        while i < 7:
            if cast[int32](row[i]) == a:
                return 44 + i
            i = i + 1
        return 0
    if a >= 49 and a <= 57:
        return 2 + (a - 49)                              # 1..9
    if a == 48:
        return 11                                        # 0
    if a == 32:
        return 57
    if a == 10 or a == 13:
        return 28                                        # Return
    if a == 8 or a == 127:
        return 14                                        # BackSpace
    if a == 9:
        return 15                                        # Tab
    if a == 45:
        return 12
    if a == 61:
        return 13
    if a == 59:
        return 39
    if a == 39:
        return 40
    if a == 44:
        return 51
    if a == 46:
        return 52
    if a == 47:
        return 53
    return 0


def needs_shift(a: int32) -> int32:
    if a >= 65 and a <= 90:
        return 1
    return 0


def key_tap(c: uint64, code: int32, shift: int32):
    kbd: int32 = conn_keyboard[c]
    if kbd == 0 or code == 0:
        return
    evt_time = evt_time + 8
    if shift != 0:
        serial = serial + 1
        send_kbd_modifiers(cast[uint32](kbd), serial, MOD_SHIFT)
    serial = serial + 1
    send_kbd_key(cast[uint32](kbd), serial, evt_time, cast[uint32](code),
                 STATE_PRESSED)
    evt_time = evt_time + 8
    serial = serial + 1
    send_kbd_key(cast[uint32](kbd), serial, evt_time, cast[uint32](code),
                 STATE_RELEASED)
    if shift != 0:
        serial = serial + 1
        send_kbd_modifiers(cast[uint32](kbd), serial, 0)


def btn_edges(c: uint64, i: uint64, surf: uint32, mask: int32):
    ptr: int32 = conn_pointer[c]
    if ptr == 0:
        return
    old: int32 = w_btn[i]
    b: int32 = 0
    while b < 3:
        bit: int32 = 1
        if b == 1:
            bit = 2
        elif b == 2:
            bit = 4
        code: uint32 = BTN_LEFT
        if b == 1:
            code = BTN_MIDDLE
        elif b == 2:
            code = BTN_RIGHT
        was: int32 = old & bit
        now: int32 = mask & bit
        if was != now:
            st: uint32 = STATE_RELEASED
            if now != 0:
                st = STATE_PRESSED
            serial = serial + 1
            evt_time = evt_time + 4
            send_ptr_button(cast[uint32](ptr), serial, evt_time, code, st)
        b = b + 1
    w_btn[i] = mask


# `at` is where this line STARTS, and it matters: wsysd writes a burst of
# pointer lines and one read picks up several, so a handler that always parsed
# from offset 0 re-read the first line for every line in the buffer. The
# visible symptom was that button presses never arrived -- a move and a click
# land in the same read, and the click was read as a second copy of the move.
def handle_ptr_line(c: uint64, i: uint64, surf: uint32, at: uint64, n: uint64):
    ptr: int32 = conn_pointer[c]
    if ptr == 0:
        return
    # "<kind> <x> <y> <buttons> <dz>"
    p: uint64 = skip_field(&inbuf[0], at, n)
    x: int32 = parse_int_at(&inbuf[0], p, n)
    p = skip_field(&inbuf[0], p, n)
    y: int32 = parse_int_at(&inbuf[0], p, n)
    p = skip_field(&inbuf[0], p, n)
    btn: int32 = parse_int_at(&inbuf[0], p, n)
    # THE FIFTH FIELD, which this function used to stop one short of. wsysd
    # writes the accumulated REL_WHEEL delta here; positive is a wheel pushed
    # AWAY from the hand, which every toolkit calls scrolling UP.
    p = skip_field(&inbuf[0], p, n)
    dz: int32 = parse_int_at(&inbuf[0], p, n)
    if w_in[i] == 0:
        serial = serial + 1
        send_ptr_enter(cast[uint32](ptr), serial, surf, x, y)
        w_in[i] = 1
    evt_time = evt_time + 4
    send_ptr_motion(cast[uint32](ptr), evt_time, x, y)
    btn_edges(c, i, surf, btn)
    if dz != 0:
        # THE SIGN IS INVERTED ON PURPOSE. evdev REL_WHEEL is positive away
        # from the hand; wl_pointer.axis is positive in the direction the
        # CONTENT moves, which for a wheel pushed away is up the page, i.e.
        # a negative axis value. Getting this backwards is a wheel that works
        # and scrolls the wrong way, which is worse than one that is dead
        # because nothing about it looks broken.
        v: int32 = 0 - dz
        pv: int32 = conn_ptrver[c]
        if pv >= 5:
            send_ptr_axis_source(cast[uint32](ptr), AXIS_SOURCE_WHEEL)
        send_ptr_axis(cast[uint32](ptr), evt_time, AXIS_VERTICAL,
                      v * AXIS_NOTCH)
        if pv >= 5:
            send_ptr_axis_discrete(cast[uint32](ptr), AXIS_VERTICAL, v)
    send_ptr_frame(cast[uint32](ptr))


# SOMEBODY ELSE MOVED THIS WINDOW. The desktop dragged it, a script wrote
# `geometry` on its ctl, hamdesktop tiled it -- the device posts the new
# rectangle on the window's event ring and this is where it lands. For a
# native Wayland toplevel there is nothing to say: a Wayland client is not
# told where it is, by design. For an X client there is everything to say.
def win_moved(i: uint64, gx: int32, gy: int32, gw: int32, gh: int32):
    w_x[i] = gx
    w_y[i] = gy
    if w_conn[i] < 0:
        return
    c: uint64 = cast[uint64](w_conn[i])
    if conn_inuse[c] == 0:
        return
    si: uint64 = oidx(c, cast[uint32](w_surf[i]))
    if obj_type[si] != T_SURFACE:
        return
    xwl: int32 = obj_e[si]
    if xwl <= 0:
        return
    xsl: int32 = xwm_find(xwl)
    if xsl < 0:
        return
    k: uint64 = cast[uint64](xsl)
    xwm_push_geom(k, gx, gy, gw, gh)
    xwm_move_or_children(k)


# --- the desktop asking a window to close -----------------------------
#
# ONE REQUEST, TWO CLIENTS, AND NEITHER OF THEM IS THIS PROGRAM. The user
# pressed a button on a title bar that wsysd painted; what has to happen is
# that the program inside the window is asked to quit, in whichever protocol
# that program speaks. This compositor is the only thing that knows which.
def close_requested(i: uint64):
    c: uint64 = cast[uint64](w_conn[i])
    if conn_inuse[c] == 0:
        return
    surf: uint32 = cast[uint32](w_surf[i])
    si: uint64 = oidx(c, surf)
    if obj_type[si] != T_SURFACE:
        return
    # AN X WINDOW. obj_e on the surface is the X window id once the two halves
    # have met (0 for an ordinary surface, -1 for one still waiting).
    xwl: int32 = obj_e[si]
    if xwl > 0:
        xsl: int32 = xwm_find(xwl)
        if xsl < 0:
            return
        k: uint64 = cast[uint64](xsl)
        if xw_del[k] != 0 and x_send_delete(cast[uint32](xwl)) != 0:
            # != 0 is 1 (it advertises) OR -1 (we never got an answer).
            hit(S_CLOSEREQ)
            puts(1, "wsyswl: WM_DELETE_WINDOW -> X window ")
            putd(1, xwl)
            puts(1, "\n")
        else:
            # It did not advertise WM_PROTOCOLS/WM_DELETE_WINDOW, which is a
            # client saying it cannot be asked. KillClient is the convention's
            # answer and what every other window manager does; saying which
            # one happened matters, because "the window would not close" and
            # "the client was killed" are different bugs.
            x_kill_client(cast[uint32](xwl))
            hit(S_CLOSEKILL)
            puts(1, "wsyswl: no WM_DELETE_WINDOW on X window ")
            putd(1, xwl)
            puts(1, " -- KillClient\n")
        return
    # A NATIVE WAYLAND TOPLEVEL. xdg_toplevel.close, event opcode 1: the
    # client is asked, and closes its own surface. Firefox honours it; so does
    # every toolkit. A popup has no close event and is not given one.
    role: int32 = obj_f[si]
    if role == 0 or obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](role)) != T_XDG_SURF:
        return
    top: int32 = obj_d[oidx(c, cast[uint32](role))]
    if top == 0 or obj_get_type(c, cast[uint32](top)) != T_XDG_TOP:
        return
    st: uint64 = emit_begin(cast[uint32](top))
    emit_end(st, 1)                             # xdg_toplevel.close
    flush(conn_fd[c])
    hit(S_CLOSEREQ)
    puts(1, "wsyswl: xdg_toplevel.close -> object ")
    putd(1, top)
    puts(1, "\n")


# One line off a window's event ring. `at` is where the line starts.
def handle_evt_line(i: uint64, at: uint64, n: uint64):
    if n - at >= 5 and inbuf[at] == 99 and inbuf[at + 1] == 108 \
            and inbuf[at + 2] == 111 and inbuf[at + 3] == 115 \
            and inbuf[at + 4] == 101:                    # "close"
        close_requested(i)
        return
    if n - at >= 8 and inbuf[at] == 103 and inbuf[at + 1] == 101 \
            and inbuf[at + 2] == 111:                    # "geometry"
        p: uint64 = skip_field(&inbuf[0], at, n)
        gx: int32 = parse_int_at(&inbuf[0], p, n)
        p = skip_field(&inbuf[0], p, n)
        gy: int32 = parse_int_at(&inbuf[0], p, n)
        p = skip_field(&inbuf[0], p, n)
        gw: int32 = parse_int_at(&inbuf[0], p, n)
        p = skip_field(&inbuf[0], p, n)
        gh: int32 = parse_int_at(&inbuf[0], p, n)
        win_moved(i, gx, gy, gw, gh)


# Read whatever is in one window's rings and translate it. The rings are
# written a whole line at a time by wsysd, so a read almost never splits one;
# a split line is DROPPED rather than carried, because the alternative is a
# per-window carry buffer for an event that is one lost mouse position.
def drain_window(i: uint64):
    c: uint64 = cast[uint64](w_conn[i])
    if conn_inuse[c] == 0:
        return
    surf: uint32 = cast[uint32](w_surf[i])
    fd: int32 = w_ptr[i]
    if fd >= 0:
        got: int64 = sys_read_nb(fd, &inbuf[0], 1000)
        s: uint64 = 0
        k: uint64 = 0
        while k < cast[uint64](got):
            if inbuf[k] == 10:
                if k > s:
                    handle_ptr_line(c, i, surf, s, k)
                s = k + 1
            k = k + 1
    fd = w_evt[i]
    if fd >= 0:
        got = sys_read_nb(fd, &inbuf[0], 1000)
        s = 0
        k = 0
        while k < cast[uint64](got):
            if inbuf[k] == 10:
                if k > s:
                    handle_evt_line(i, s, k)
                s = k + 1
            k = k + 1
    fd = w_keys[i]
    if fd >= 0:
        got = sys_read_nb(fd, &inbuf[0], 1000)
        if got > 0:
            if w_focus[i] == 0:
                kbd: int32 = conn_keyboard[c]
                if kbd != 0:
                    serial = serial + 1
                    send_kbd_enter(cast[uint32](kbd), serial, surf)
                    w_focus[i] = 1
            # _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW, for an X window. wsysd owns focus and this
            # is where the compositor finds out about it: a window that is
            # being given keys is the window that has the keyboard.
            #
            # OUTSIDE the w_focus guard, and that is the whole point. w_focus
            # is set on the FIRST key a window ever receives and is never
            # cleared, so hanging this off it published each window's focus
            # exactly once, ever: focus A, focus B, focus A again, and
            # _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW still names B for the rest of the session
            # while both A and B claim _NET_WM_STATE_FOCUSED. A property that
            # is wrong is worse than one that is absent -- which is what this
            # file's own EWMH comment says -- so the publish is driven by the
            # keys arriving and deduplicated inside x_ewmh_active.
            si2: uint64 = oidx(c, surf)
            if obj_type[si2] == T_SURFACE and obj_e[si2] > 0:
                x_ewmh_active(obj_e[si2])
        s = 0
        k = 0
        while k < cast[uint64](got):
            if inbuf[k] == 10:
                # "d <code>": a press of a cooked ASCII byte. wsysd has no
                # release edge to give us, so each byte is delivered as a
                # press immediately followed by a release.
                if k > s + 1:
                    p: uint64 = skip_field(&inbuf[0], s, k)
                    a: int32 = parse_int_at(&inbuf[0], p, k)
                    key_tap(c, ascii_evdev(a), needs_shift(a))
                s = k + 1
            k = k + 1
    if out_pos > 0:
        flush(conn_fd[c])


# --- the screen -------------------------------------------------------

# Top-level BSS, not frame locals: an Adder frame local array is not a place
# to put a buffer on this line.
scr_wp: Array[1, int64]
scr_hp: Array[1, int64]
scrpath: Array[256, uint8]
scrline: Array[64, uint8]


# HOW BIG IS THE SCREEN? This server answers that question for every Wayland
# client -- wl_output.mode, wl_output.geometry and zxdg_output_v1.logical_size
# are all it -- and, through Xwayland, for every X11 client behind it too. It
# used to answer 1280x800 because that is what the VM happens to be, which is
# the exact bug lib/hamscreen.ad exists to end: a plausible wrong answer, on
# any display that is not 1280x800, for the entire Debian namespace at once.
#
# So it comes from the compositor, which is the process that actually set the
# mode, and there is NO fallback. A Wayland server that does not know how big
# its output is has nothing true to tell a client, and inventing a number here
# would be inventing it for Firefox and for Steam simultaneously.
def resolve_screen() -> int32:
    if hamscreen_dims_or_fail(&scr_wp[0], &scr_hp[0],
                              cast[Ptr[uint8]]("wsyswl")) == 0:
        return 0
    scr_w = cast[int32](scr_wp[0])
    scr_h = cast[int32](scr_hp[0])
    # "Maximised" is the screen minus what wsysd's title bar and frame take,
    # not the screen: a client handed the whole thing loses its own chrome off
    # the bottom edge. Small screens get a floor rather than a negative size.
    MAX_W = scr_w - MAX_INSET_W
    MAX_H = scr_h - MAX_INSET_H
    if MAX_W < 320:
        MAX_W = 320
    if MAX_H < 240:
        MAX_H = 240
    return 1


# PUBLISH IT NEXT TO THE SOCKET, because a program on the other side of the
# namespace boundary cannot read /dev/wsys.
#
# Xwayland is started INSIDE the Debian namespace, whose root is the Debian
# tree, so /dev/wsys does not exist for it and lib/hamscreen is unreachable.
# The one name it already shares with us is the directory holding the Wayland
# socket (docs/steam_namespace.md §6). A sibling file there is therefore the
# only fact we can hand across, and it is exactly the Plan 9 answer: publish
# the geometry as a file, and let the client name it.
#
# It matters because a ROOTFUL Xwayland does not size itself from the
# wl_output. Bookworm's 22.1.9 happens to; 24.1.6 does not and comes up
# 640x480 -- measured, tests/linux/x11_geom_probe.sh -- so an X11 session's
# screen size silently depends on the Xwayland version unless it is told.
def publish_screen():
    i: uint64 = 0
    cut: uint64 = 0
    while sockpath[i] != 0:
        if sockpath[i] == 47:                  # '/'
            cut = i
        i = i + 1
    if cut == 0:
        return
    j: uint64 = 0
    while j < cut and j + 16 < 256:
        scrpath[j] = sockpath[j]
        j = j + 1
    str_copy(&scrpath[j], "/hamnix-screen", 256 - j)
    fd: int32 = sys_open_write(cast[Ptr[char]](&scrpath[0]))
    if fd < 0:
        return
    n: uint64 = emit_int(&scrline[0], 0, scr_w)
    scrline[n] = 32
    n = emit_int(&scrline[0], n + 1, scr_h)
    scrline[n] = 10
    sys_write(fd, &scrline[0], n + 1)
    sys_close(fd)


# AND PUBLISH WHAT THIS SERVER IS DOING WITH THE FRAMES IT IS GIVEN, in the
# same directory and for the same reason: it is the one name a program on
# either side of the namespace boundary can open. `cat /run/wayland-0`'s
# sibling `wsyswl-state` from a Debian shell, or from the Hamnix side at
# /n/distro/run/wsyswl-state, and the compositor tells you how many buffers it
# has put on the screen and how many it has thrown away, by reason.
def build_statepath():
    i: uint64 = 0
    cut: uint64 = 0
    while sockpath[i] != 0:
        if sockpath[i] == 47:                  # '/'
            cut = i
        i = i + 1
    if cut == 0:
        return
    j: uint64 = 0
    while j < cut and j + 24 < 256:
        statepath[j] = sockpath[j]
        j = j + 1
    str_copy(&statepath[j], "/wsyswl-state", 256 - j)


def publish_state():
    if statepath[0] == 0:
        return
    if stat_dirty == 0:
        return
    fd: int32 = sys_open_write(cast[Ptr[char]](&statepath[0]))
    if fd < 0:
        return
    st_kv(fd, "commits", S_COMMIT)
    st_kv(fd, "rows", S_ROWS)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_not_a_buffer", S_NOTBUF)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_zero_size", S_BADDIM)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_no_mapping", S_NOMAP)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_unmapped", S_NOBASE)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_no_role", S_NOROLE)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_no_window", S_NOWIN)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_no_slot", S_NOSLOT)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_unconfigured", S_UNCONF)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_row_past_mapping", S_SHORT)
    st_kv(fd, "map_alloc_failed", S_MAPFULL)
    st_kv(fd, "obj_id_refused", S_IDHIGH)
    st_kv(fd, "frame_callbacks_full", S_FCFULL)
    st_kv(fd, "maps_in_use", S_MAPUSE)
    st_kv(fd, "maps_high_water", S_MAPHI)
    st_kv(fd, "max_object_id", S_IDHI)
    st_kv(fd, "windows_high_water", S_WINHI)
    st_kv(fd, "window_budget_full", S_WINFULL)
    st_kv(fd, "conns", S_CONN)
    st_kv(fd, "conns_high_water", S_CONNHI)
    # ROOTLESS. `xwm 0` is the rootful path, unchanged and unmeasured by these
    # three. `xwm 1` means this compositor holds SubstructureRedirect on an X
    # display: `xwl_paired` is how many X windows have found their wl_surface,
    # `drop_xwl_unpaired` is how many frames were thrown away because one half
    # arrived without the other, and `xwl_managed` is how many X windows the
    # XWM has on its books right now. A rootless session where
    # windows_high_water stays at 1 is answered by these lines and by nothing
    # else in the file.
    puts(fd, "xwm ")
    putd(fd, xwm_on)
    puts(fd, "\n")
    puts(fd, "xwm_connected ")
    if xfd >= 0:
        putd(fd, 1)
    else:
        putd(fd, 0)
    puts(fd, "\n")
    puts(fd, "xwl_managed ")
    putd(fd, cast[int32](n_xw))
    puts(fd, "\n")
    st_kv(fd, "xwl_paired", S_XWLPAIR)
    st_kv(fd, "drop_xwl_unpaired", S_XWLUNP)
    st_kv(fd, "xwm_refused", S_XWLERR)
    st_kv(fd, "close_requested", S_CLOSEREQ)
    st_kv(fd, "close_killed", S_CLOSEKILL)
    st_kv(fd, "x_configure_pushed", S_XCONF)
    st_kv(fd, "or_children_followed", S_ORFOLLOW)
    st_kv(fd, "conn_refused", S_CONNFULL)
    # THE LIMITS, SPLIT BY WHOSE THEY ARE. A reader cannot tell what any of the
    # counters above mean without knowing which of these is shared and which is
    # not, and the first three passes of the Steam investigation are what a
    # reader guessing looks like. `per_conn` is a budget this client is
    # guaranteed; `shared` is a ceiling every client on the server pays into.
    puts(fd, "limits per_conn MAXMAP=")
    putd(fd, cast[int32](map_limit))
    puts(fd, " MAXOBJ=")
    putd(fd, cast[int32](MAXOBJ))
    puts(fd, " WINPERCONN=")
    putd(fd, cast[int32](WINPERCONN))
    puts(fd, " FCPERCONN=")
    putd(fd, cast[int32](FCPERCONN))
    puts(fd, "\n")
    puts(fd, "limits shared MAXCONN=")
    putd(fd, cast[int32](MAXCONN))
    puts(fd, " MAXWIN=")
    putd(fd, cast[int32](MAXWIN))
    puts(fd, " FCMAX=")
    putd(fd, cast[int32](FCMAX))
    puts(fd, " MAXMAP_BUILT=")
    putd(fd, cast[int32](MAXMAP))
    puts(fd, "\n")
    sys_close(fd)
    stat_dirty = 0


# --- main -------------------------------------------------------------

def main(argc: int32, argv: Ptr[uint64]) -> int32:
    n: int64 = sys_getenv(cast[Ptr[char]]("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR"), &envbuf[0], 256)
    if n > 0:
        k: uint64 = str_copy(&sockpath[0], &envbuf[0], 256)
        str_copy(&sockpath[k], "/wayland-0", 256 - k)
    else:
        str_copy(&sockpath[0], "/tmp/wayland-0", 256)
    if argc > 1:
        str_copy(&sockpath[0], cast[Ptr[uint8]](argv[1]), 256)
    if sys_getenv(cast[Ptr[char]]("WSYSWL_VERBOSE"), &envbuf[0], 256) >= 0:
        verbose = 1

    # WSYSWL_XWM=<path to an X11 socket> -- ROOTLESS MODE, and it is opt-in for
    # the reason docs/linux_window_manager.md §8 gives: the rootful path works,
    # ships, and has a window manager inside the namespace, and this is a third
    # session arm rather than a replacement until it has earned the swap. With
    # it unset, not one byte of behaviour changes: xwayland_shell_v1 is not
    # advertised, no X connection is dialled, and the commit path takes the
    # branch it always took.
    xn: int64 = sys_getenv(cast[Ptr[char]]("WSYSWL_XWM"), &envbuf[0], 256)
    if xn > 0:
        str_copy(&xwm_path[0], &envbuf[0], 256)
        xwm_on = 1
        puts(1, "wsyswl: ROOTLESS -- this compositor will manage the X display at ")
        puts(1, &xwm_path[0])
        puts(1, "\n")
        puts(1, "wsyswl: no window manager may run inside the namespace on that display.\n")

    # WSYSWL_MAXMAP -- shrink the per-connection wl_shm mapping table, so
    # tests/linux/wsyswl_shared_fate.sh can exhaust it in seconds instead of
    # needing Steam and a VM. It can only ever LOWER the ceiling: a test that
    # could raise a limit would be testing something the distribution does not
    # ship. Clamped to [4, MAXMAP], and NAMED on stderr when it is in force,
    # because a compositor running with a deliberately crippled limit and not
    # saying so is the exact silence this file's counters exist to end.
    map_limit = MAXMAP
    ml: int64 = sys_getenv(cast[Ptr[char]]("WSYSWL_MAXMAP"), &envbuf[0], 256)
    if ml > 0:
        want: int32 = parse_int_at(&envbuf[0], 0, cast[uint64](ml))
        if want >= 4 and cast[uint64](want) < MAXMAP:
            map_limit = cast[uint64](want)
            puts(2, "wsyswl: WSYSWL_MAXMAP -- per-connection mapping table capped at ")
            putd(2, want)
            puts(2, " of ")
            putd(2, cast[int32](MAXMAP))
            puts(2, "\n")

    # BEFORE the socket exists. A client that connects and binds wl_output in
    # the same breath must not be able to catch a zero.
    if resolve_screen() == 0:
        return 1
    puts(1, "wsyswl: screen ")
    putd(1, scr_w)
    puts(1, "x")
    putd(1, scr_h)
    puts(1, "\n")

    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < MAXCONN:
        conn_inuse[i] = 0
        conn_fd[i] = -1
        i = i + 1
    i = 0
    while i < FCMAX:
        fc_conn[i] = -1                 # 0 is a real connection number
        i = i + 1

    # THE BACKLOG IS A CEILING TOO, and it was the OLD MAXCONN written a
    # second time. It was the literal 8, and it was chosen when 8 was also the
    # connection limit, so the two could not disagree and nothing noticed that
    # they were the same number twice.
    #
    # It has to be BIGGER than MAXCONN, not equal to it, and the reason is the
    # refusal above. Telling a client "the connection table is full" requires
    # ACCEPTING it first -- wl_display.error is sent on an accepted socket. A
    # client that overflows the LISTEN QUEUE never reaches accept(2) at all:
    # the kernel bounces its connect(2) with ECONNREFUSED, and what a Wayland
    # client prints then is that it cannot open the display, exactly as if no
    # compositor were running. That is the honest refusal replaced by a
    # misleading one at the last possible moment, and it is not hypothetical
    # -- it is what tests/linux/wsyswl_conn_ceiling.sh caught, intermittently,
    # under build load, when four clients arrived past a ceiling of sixteen
    # against a queue of eight.
    #
    # MAXCONN + 16 leaves room for a burst of clients that will all be refused
    # to each be refused BY NAME.
    listen_fd = sys_unix_listen(cast[Ptr[char]](&sockpath[0]),
                                cast[int32](MAXCONN + 16))
    if listen_fd < 0:
        puts(2, "wsyswl: cannot listen on ")
        puts(2, &sockpath[0])
        puts(2, "\n")
        return 1

    # 0666, AND THE MODE IS THE WHOLE OF IT: connect(2) on a unix socket needs
    # WRITE permission on the socket inode. bind(2) creates it 0777 masked by
    # the umask, which is 022 in every boot here, so it came out `srwxr-xr-x`
    # -- writable by the owner alone. /etc/rc.distros-wl starts one wsyswl per
    # distribution AS ROOT at rc.5, so the owner was uid 0, and uid 0 is not
    # even mapped into the user namespace an unprivileged `enter` acquires: the
    # session saw `srwxr-xr-x 1 nobody nogroup`. The one thing a menu-launched
    # application must do -- connect to the display -- was the one thing the
    # session user could not do, and the only symptom was Xwayland's
    # `could not connect to wayland server` / `Couldn't add screen`.
    # docs/linux_distro_namespaces.md 8.5.
    #
    # NOT A LOOSENING, AND THE PRECEDENT IS THIS TREE'S OWN. `/srv/wsys` is
    # 0666 deliberately (user/linux-wsys.c, THE SPLIT; commit ad440707): a
    # uid-1001 client has to be able to map and draw its OWN window, and a
    # desktop whose session cannot is unprivileged and blind. The system chrome
    # was moved to a SECOND segment at 0644 so the kernel enforces the gate
    # against programs that skip the protocol. The same line falls in the same
    # place here: what a connection to this socket buys is a Wayland client
    # session -- map a surface, commit your own pixels -- which is exactly the
    # 0666 half. wsyswl issues no gated verb on the client's behalf: it drives
    # `newwindow` (devwsys's explicit pre-gate exception) and the per-window
    # `<wid>/ctl` verbs decorate/z/version/title on windows it owns itself,
    # every one of them owner-or-hostowner. Nothing about connecting reaches
    # the chrome, so this is 0666 for the same reason /srv/wsys is, not a
    # wider one.
    #
    # THE BLAST RADIUS IS THE DIRECTORY, WHICH IS THE POINT. The mode is on the
    # socket; the access control is the path, which is how every Wayland
    # compositor's $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR socket has always worked. This socket lives
    # at /n/<name>/run/wayland-0 -- inside ONE distribution's own /run -- so
    # "anyone who can reach this path" is "anyone already inside that
    # distribution's namespace, plus root". That is exactly the set that should
    # be able to draw on that distribution's display.
    #
    # AND IT IS SAID OUT LOUD IF IT FAILS. A display server whose socket nobody
    # can connect to is the silent success-shaped failure this file's counters
    # exist to end (NORTH_STAR.md): it would accept zero connections and report
    # `listening on ...` exactly as it does when it works.
    if sys_chmod(cast[Ptr[char]](&sockpath[0]), 0o666) != 0:
        puts(2, "wsyswl: WARNING could not chmod 0666 ")
        puts(2, &sockpath[0])
        puts(2, "\n")
        puts(2, "wsyswl: connect(2) needs WRITE on the socket, so an unprivileged\n")
        puts(2, "wsyswl: client will fail with `could not connect to wayland server'.\n")

    publish_screen()
    build_statepath()
    publish_state()
    puts(1, "wsyswl: state file ")
    puts(1, &statepath[0])
    puts(1, "\n")
    puts(1, "wsyswl: listening on ")
    puts(1, &sockpath[0])
    puts(1, "\n")
    puts(1, "wsyswl: point a client at it with WAYLAND_DISPLAY=wayland-0\n")

    # PROVE THE HAMNIX HALF BEFORE ADVERTISING THE WAYLAND ONE. This
    # compositor has always advertised wl_data_device_manager, because GTK
    # will not make a seat without it -- and for the whole port it advertised
    # it over nothing at all: every set_selection was dropped and no selection
    # event was ever sent, in silence. If /dev/snarf is not served in this
    # process the same silence is back, so it is named here, once, on the log
    # that the machine's owner reads.
    if wlsn_present() == 0:
        puts(2, "wsyswl: WARNING /dev/snarf is NOT served in this process.\n")
        puts(2, "wsyswl: wl_data_device_manager is still advertised (a client\n")
        puts(2, "wsyswl: that cannot bind it will not start), but there is NO\n")
        puts(2, "wsyswl: clipboard behind it -- copy and paste between Wayland\n")
        puts(2, "wsyswl: clients and Hamnix programs will do nothing.\n")
    else:
        wlsn_poll()                     # prime the cache; do not announce it
        puts(1, "wsyswl: clipboard bridged to /dev/snarf (wl_data_device)\n")

    while 1 == 1:
        nfd: uint64 = 0
        waitset[0] = listen_fd
        nfd = 1
        i = 0
        while i < MAXCONN:
            if conn_inuse[i] != 0 and nfd < WAITSET:
                waitset[nfd] = conn_fd[i]
                nfd = nfd + 1
            i = i + 1
        # A short timeout rather than a pure wait: the input rings are files,
        # not descriptors that poll can speak for, so they have to be looked
        # at on a clock. 16 ms is one frame.
        if xfd >= 0 and nfd < WAITSET:
            waitset[nfd] = xfd
            nfd = nfd + 1
        # An in-flight clipboard pull is a real pipe, so it can be PARKED ON
        # rather than spun on: the HANDOFF IDLE CENSUS is what this line is
        # for. Without it a copy would still work -- clip_pull_step runs on
        # the 16 ms frame either way -- but the wait would end 16 ms late.
        if pull_fd >= 0 and nfd < WAITSET:
            waitset[nfd] = pull_fd
            nfd = nfd + 1
        sys_waitfds(&waitset[0], nfd, 16)
        clip_pull_step()
        xwm_pump()
        conn_accept()
        i = 0
        while i < MAXCONN:
            if conn_inuse[i] != 0:
                if conn_pump(i) < 0:
                    conn_free(i)
            i = i + 1
        i = 0
        while i < n_win:
            drain_window(i)
            i = i + 1
        # Twice a second, not every 16 ms frame: this is a file rewritten in
        # full and the point of it was to be cheaper than a log.
        tick = tick + 1
        # FOUR TIMES A SECOND, the same rate user/xsnarfd.ad polls at and for
        # the same reason: struct snarfshm has no generation counter, so a
        # change on the Hamnix side can only be found by comparing bytes.
        # docs/linux_clipboard.md §6.5 has the two-line request that would
        # retire both polls at once.
        if tick % 8 == 0:
            clip_tick()
        if tick >= 32:
            tick = 0
            publish_state()
    return 0
